/* Year 11 textbook: retained authored explanations reviewed and expanded; new inquiry, applications and sourced visuals. */
(function(root){'use strict';const lessons=[{"id":"cells","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"01 · Human cells, organelles and microscopy","goal":"Relate the structures of human cells to their functions and interpret microscopic measurements.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["Cells as living systems","Cells are the basic structural and functional units of life. They exchange matter with their surroundings, transform energy, carry out regulated chemical reactions and maintain conditions compatible with survival. Inputs include water, ions, nutrients and, for many cells, oxygen. Outputs include metabolic wastes and heat. Human cell requirements differ; mature erythrocytes obtain ATP without mitochondrial aerobic respiration. Structure supports function: membranes separate processes, proteins catalyse reactions and genetic information directs many cellular activities. A cell is an interacting system rather than a collection of independent parts. Explain how a structural feature affects a process and therefore contributes to the cell's survival."],["Eukaryotes, nuclei and chromosomes","Eukaryotic cells contain a nucleus enclosing chromosomes and membrane-bound organelles that compartmentalise functions. Nuclear pores regulate exchange with the cytoplasm, while the nucleolus participates in ribosome production. Chromosomal DNA is associated with proteins, and genetic material also occurs in mitochondria . Human cells lack cell walls and chloroplasts; their shapes and organelle arrangements reflect their particular functions. Specialised cells may lose structures during development, so textbook cell diagrams represent common patterns rather than a requirement that every cell contains every labelled organelle."],["Ribosomes, rough ER and protein secretion","Ribosomes join amino acids into proteins using information carried by messenger RNA. Free ribosomes commonly make proteins used within the cytosol, while ribosomes associated with rough endoplasmic reticulum make many proteins destined for secretion, membranes or parts of the endomembrane system. The rough ER supports synthesis and early processing, with transport vesicles carrying material toward the Golgi apparatus. The Golgi modifies, sorts and packages proteins for destinations, including secretion by exocytosis. Smooth ER lacks attached ribosomes and participates in functions such as lipid synthesis and, in suitable cell types, detoxification. Explain the path of a secreted protein by linking structures to processes. The Golgi does not translate mRNA, and a vesicle carries a protein rather than replacing the ribosome's synthetic role."],["Specialisation and cellular organisation","Specialised cells have features suited to particular functions, such as a large exchange surface, abundant mitochondria or extensive rough ER. A cell that secretes large quantities of protein may have prominent rough ER and Golgi apparatus because it synthesises and packages those products. An intestinal epithelial cell has microvilli supporting absorption, while a neuron has long processes supporting communication. Cells of a multicellular organism generally share the same inherited genome but express different sets of genes. Specialisation creates interdependence because one cell type cannot independently perform all functions needed by the organism. State the feature, the process it supports and the effect on function. Larger numbers of an organelle suggest a functional demand but should be interpreted alongside the cell type and evidence."],["Light and electron microscopy","Light microscopy uses visible light and can examine living specimens under suitable conditions. Electron microscopy uses electrons and offers much higher resolving power, but routine preparation normally requires non-living material. Transmission electron microscopy shows internal ultrastructure in thin sections, while scanning electron microscopy primarily represents surface features. Magnification enlarges an image; resolution is the ability to distinguish close features as separate. Increasing magnification without improving resolution produces a larger blurred image rather than new detail. Preparation, staining and sectioning can alter specimens, so images are interpreted with awareness of artefacts. Compare the methods according to the question: watching living cell movement and examining fine internal membrane structure have different requirements."],["Magnification and scale-bar calculations","Magnification equals image size divided by actual size, with both sizes in the same units. One millimetre equals 1,000 micrometres, and one micrometre equals 1,000 nanometres. If a 20 μm cell appears 40 mm long, convert 40 mm to 40,000 μm and calculate 40,000 ÷ 20 = 2,000 times magnification. A scale bar can remain useful when an image is resized because its printed length changes with the image. If a 10 μm bar measures 15 mm and a cell measures 45 mm, the cell is 30 μm long. Show conversions and units rather than treating μm and mm as interchangeable. Report a sensible precision given the image measurement and avoid implying that an uncertain boundary supports many decimal places."],["Biological drawings and changing cell models","A biological drawing should communicate the observed structures clearly, with a title, appropriate scale and labelled features. Use clean lines and connect labels unambiguously to the intended structure. Distinguish what is actually visible in the preparation from an idealised textbook diagram. Labelled teaching models can show invisible processes, but their legends should identify simplification and scale limitations. Improved microscopy and preparation have revised cell models by revealing finer structures and correcting earlier interpretations. Improved imaging and preparation provide new evidence for evaluating cellular models. A useful explanation links the technological improvement to the new evidence and the resulting model revision rather than claiming a microscope photograph independently proves every aspect of a cell's function."],["Lysosomes and the cytoskeleton","Lysosomes contain digestive enzymes and break down material taken into the cell or worn-out cellular components. Their membranes separate these enzymes from the cytosol. The cytoskeleton supports shape, internal transport and cell movement. A cell can therefore be active internally even when its outline seems unchanged in a microscope image. Organelles interact: lysosomal enzymes are proteins assembled by ribosomes, processed through the rough endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi, then delivered in vesicles."],["Human specialisation is a structure–function problem","Most nucleated human somatic cells have essentially the same inherited DNA, but express different subsets of genes. A muscle fibre has structures supporting force production and ATP demand; a secretory cell has abundant rough ER and Golgi; a neuron has long processes for communication. Mature erythrocytes are a specialised exception: they lack a nucleus and most organelles, leaving more space for haemoglobin. Specialisation improves one task but imposes limits, such as the erythrocyte’s inability to divide."]],"mis":"An enlarged image is not automatically a more detailed image. Magnification and resolution describe different properties.","worked":["A human cell image is 24.0 mm across and the actual cell diameter is 12.0 μm. Calculate magnification and explain why magnification and resolution differ.","Convert the image dimension: 24.0 mm = 24 000 μm. Magnification = image size/actual size = 24 000/12.0 = 2000×. Resolution is the ability to distinguish close points separately; enlarging an already blurred image does not add resolved detail."],"workedMore":[["A pancreatic cell secretes a protein enzyme. Explain the roles of ribosomes, rough ER, Golgi and vesicles in this process.","Ribosomes assemble the polypeptide using mRNA instructions; many secreted proteins enter rough ER for folding and initial processing. The Golgi modifies, sorts and packages the protein for delivery. A secretory vesicle moves to and fuses with the plasma membrane, releasing the protein by exocytosis."],["An image has a 10.0 μm scale bar measuring 20.0 mm on screen. A cell nucleus measures 12.0 mm on that same image. Find its actual diameter.","The nucleus is 12.0/20.0 = 0.600 of the scale-bar image length. Actual nucleus diameter = 0.600 × 10.0 = 6.00 μm. Use measurements from the same resized image; a scale bar changes size with the image and remains useful."]],"practice":[["Compare expected mitochondrial abundance in an actively contracting muscle cell and a cell with much lower ATP demand. State a limit of your prediction.","Aerobic ATP production includes mitochondrial processes, so greater sustained ATP demand generally supports greater mitochondrial abundance. Muscle contraction and ion pumping require ATP, linking structure to function. Actual numbers depend on cell type, training, metabolic state and sampling; a single image cannot establish a universal ratio.",6],["Describe how you would record a microscopy observation so that another student can interpret its structures and scale.","Give a clear title identifying specimen and viewing method; draw observed outlines without invented structures. Use clean lines and straight labels identifying relevant structures; include a scale bar or appropriately calculated magnification. Keep observation separate from inference, such as labelling an unresolved dark region as a confirmed organelle.",6],["Explain two advantages and two limitations of the mature erythrocyte’s structure.","A biconcave shape increases exchange area and shortens diffusion distance; flexibility supports movement through narrow capillaries. Haemoglobin-rich cytoplasm supports oxygen transport and nucleus loss creates space. Absence of nucleus prevents division and much protein synthesis; absence of mitochondria means energy metabolism differs from that of a typical nucleated cell.",6]],"quiz":["What primarily distinguishes typical eukaryotic from prokaryotic organisation?",["An enclosed nucleus and membrane-bound organelles","Presence of ribosomes alone","Presence of DNA alone","Presence of cytoplasm alone"],0,"Both cell types share DNA, ribosomes, cytoplasm and membranes, but typical eukaryotes have compartmentalised organelles."],"quizzes":[["Where are polypeptides assembled?",["Ribosomes","Only cell walls","Only vacuoles","Phospholipid heads"],0,"Ribosomes translate mRNA using amino acids; Golgi and ER have subsequent processing/transport roles."],["A 30 mm image represents a 10 μm object. What is magnification?",["3000×","3×","300×","0.003×"],0,"Convert 30 mm to 30,000 μm then divide by 10 μm to obtain 3,000×."],["What does higher resolution permit?",["Distinguishing closer points separately","Only a larger blurry image","Changing the organism’s genome","Making any specimen live"],0,"Resolution concerns detail separation, while magnification concerns image size."],["Which feature distinguishes a mature human erythrocyte?",["It lacks a nucleus.","It has a chloroplast.","It has a thick cellulose wall.","It makes all its ATP in mitochondria."],0,"Nucleus loss helps provide haemoglobin space, but mature erythrocytes cannot divide and lack mitochondria."]],"images":["y11-cell","y11-mitochondria"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"membranes","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"02 · Membrane structure and transport","goal":"Explain selective permeability, diffusion, osmosis, active transport and vesicular transport.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["The fluid mosaic model","A plasma membrane consists mainly of a phospholipid bilayer with associated proteins and other components. Each phospholipid has a hydrophilic region facing the watery environment and hydrophobic tails directed into the bilayer. Many components can move laterally, producing the term fluid, while the mixture of proteins and lipids contributes to the mosaic. Proteins can provide channels, carriers, receptors and enzymes. Carbohydrate groups on outer surfaces participate in recognition, and cholesterol in animal membranes influences membrane properties. Selective permeability depends on the properties of the bilayer and its proteins. A membrane is not a solid wall or an unrestricted sieve. The model helps explain movement and communication while simplifying the dynamic arrangement and diversity of real membranes."],["Simple diffusion and concentration gradients","Diffusion is net movement arising from random particle motion down a concentration gradient. Individual particles move in both directions, but more move from the region of higher concentration to lower concentration until the net difference decreases. At equilibrium, particles continue moving even though there is no net transfer in one direction. Small non-polar molecules such as oxygen can diffuse through the phospholipid bilayer. The rate depends on the concentration gradient, surface area, distance, temperature and properties of the substance and membrane. Diffusion does not require direct metabolic energy input. Explain the direction using the concentration of the particular substance, and avoid saying particles deliberately seek empty space or stop moving when equilibrium is reached."],["Facilitated diffusion and transport proteins","Facilitated diffusion moves substances down their concentration gradient through membrane proteins. Channels provide selective pathways, while carriers bind substances and change shape during transport. Polar molecules and ions that cross the lipid bilayer poorly can therefore move without direct ATP use when an appropriate pathway and gradient exist. Protein availability can limit the rate, so a transport graph may plateau even as concentration increases. A membrane protein does not automatically mean active transport: direction relative to the gradient and the source of energy must also be considered. Distinguish the protein's selective role from the random molecular movement underlying diffusion. Damage to a particular channel can affect one substance without making the membrane equally permeable to everything."],["Osmosis across a partially permeable membrane","Osmosis is net movement of water across a selectively permeable membrane from higher water potential to lower water potential. At introductory level, when pressure conditions are comparable, this is often described as movement from a more dilute solution toward a solution with more effective non-penetrating solute. Water moves in both directions, with unequal rates giving a net transfer. The membrane must allow water movement while restricting relevant solutes for a sustained osmotic effect. Solute identity, membrane permeability and pressure can affect the result, so comparing total solute numbers without considering the membrane can mislead. State that water moves, name the direction relative to the two environments and explain how this changes cell volume or mass under the specified conditions."],["Animal cells and tonicity","Tonicity describes the tendency of a solution to change a cell's volume through the effects of non-penetrating solutes. In a hypotonic solution, an animal cell may gain water, swell and lyse. In a hypertonic solution, it may lose water and shrink. An isotonic solution produces no sustained net osmotic change in volume under those conditions, though molecules continue moving. Animal cells lack a rigid external wall that could oppose swelling. The terms are relative to the cell and membrane, so a solution is not inherently hypertonic without a comparison. Explain the physical consequence rather than equating dilute with harmless. The same external concentration can affect different cells differently when their internal conditions or membrane permeabilities differ."],["Active transport against a gradient","Active transport uses an energy source to move substances against a concentration or electrochemical gradient. Membrane pumps can use ATP directly, while other transporters can use energy stored in an ion gradient created by earlier pumping. For the core comparison, identify the movement against the gradient, the involvement of specific transport proteins and the requirement for energy. Root cells can accumulate mineral ions even when their concentration outside is lower than inside. A decline in respiration can limit ATP-dependent transport. Do not identify active transport solely because a substance moves into a cell: substances can enter by diffusion when their gradient favours entry. State which substance, which direction and what supplies the energy in the example."],["Endocytosis and exocytosis","Endocytosis brings material into a cell through membrane invagination and vesicle formation. Phagocytosis engulfs relatively large particles, while other forms take in fluid or selected materials. Exocytosis releases material when intracellular vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane. These processes require cellular energy and machinery, and can transport material that cannot pass directly through the bilayer or a simple channel. A secretory cell can export a protein made through the rough ER and Golgi pathway by exocytosis. Bulk transport is distinct from moving single small solutes through a carrier, although all involve membrane function. Explain the membrane change and destination of the material, rather than simply saying active processes move anything in or out."],["Surface area to volume and exchange distance","For a cube with side length a, surface area is 6a² and volume is a³, so the surface-area-to-volume ratio is 6/a. As a similar-shaped cell becomes larger, its volume grows faster than its surface area. Exchange capacity may therefore become insufficient relative to the metabolic needs of the contents, particularly when diffusion distances also increase. A 1-unit cube has a ratio of 6:1, while a 3-unit cube has 54:27 or 2:1. Thin, flattened or folded shapes can provide more surface relative to volume than a compact shape of similar volume. A high ratio does not guarantee unlimited exchange because membrane properties, gradients and transport proteins also matter. State the actual dimensions and units before comparing calculations."],["Choosing the transport mechanism","Ask first whether the substance crosses the lipid bilayer, uses a transport protein or travels in a vesicle. Then determine the direction relative to its concentration or electrochemical gradient and whether cellular energy is needed. Oxygen is small and nonpolar, so it crosses by simple diffusion. Glucose generally requires proteins. A large secreted protein requires vesicular transport. Water can cross through aquaporins as well as the bilayer. The membrane’s properties and the substance’s chemistry determine the available route."]],"mis":"Particles continue moving at equilibrium; there is no net diffusion, rather than no molecular movement.","worked":["Predict and explain the response of human erythrocytes in hypotonic, isotonic and hypertonic solutions.","Hypotonic surroundings cause net water entry; cells swell and may lyse because they lack a rigid cell wall. Isotonic surroundings produce no sustained net water movement and little volume change, although water moves both ways. Hypertonic surroundings cause net water loss and shrinking; osmosis concerns water moving across a selectively permeable membrane."],"workedMore":[["Compare simple diffusion of oxygen with facilitated diffusion of glucose.","Both are passive net movement down the relevant gradient and do not directly consume ATP. Oxygen can cross the lipid bilayer; glucose generally moves via specific membrane transport proteins. Protein availability and saturation can limit facilitated diffusion; both processes depend on maintained gradients and membrane properties."],["For cube-shaped model cells with side lengths 1.00 mm and 3.00 mm, calculate SA:V and explain the exchange consequence.","SA = 6l² and V = l³, giving SA:V = 6/l. The ratios are 6.00 mm⁻¹ and 2.00 mm⁻¹ respectively; the larger cell has less surface area per unit volume. Its relative membrane capacity is lower and internal diffusion distances are longer. Actual human cells use shape, specialised surfaces and transport systems rather than being cubes."]],"practice":[["A kidney epithelial cell returns an ion from low concentration to high concentration. Explain why simple diffusion alone cannot produce this sustained net movement.","Passive diffusion favours net movement down the relevant gradient. Uphill movement requires a suitable transport mechanism coupled to an energy source, such as an ATP-driven pump or a secondary gradient. The transport protein is selective; the energy coupling must be maintained for sustained transport.",6],["Compare endocytosis by a phagocyte with exocytosis from a secretory cell.","Endocytosis encloses extracellular material in a vesicle formed from the plasma membrane. Exocytosis fuses an intracellular vesicle with the membrane, releasing cargo outside the cell. Both involve membrane rearrangement and cellular energy, and move material that cannot simply diffuse through the lipid bilayer.",6],["Predict two effects of damaging transport proteins while the lipid bilayer remains intact.","Transport depending on the damaged carriers or channels is reduced, affecting specific solutes or ion gradients. Simple diffusion of appropriate small nonpolar molecules may remain possible through the bilayer. Effects depend on the proteins damaged: impaired ion pumps, glucose carriers or aquaporins have different consequences.",6]],"quiz":["Which phospholipid arrangement is appropriate?",["Hydrophilic heads outward, tails inward","All heads inside the core","All tails exposed to water","A solid wall with no proteins"],0,"Amphipathic phospholipids form a bilayer with hydrophobic tails sheltered from aqueous fluids."],"quizzes":[["What is facilitated diffusion?",["Protein-mediated movement down a gradient","Always ATP-driven uphill pumping","Vesicle fusion only","Movement without any membrane"],0,"Channels/carriers assist passive movement; protein involvement alone does not make transport active."],["For a cube of side 2, what is SA:V?",["3:1","2:1","6:1","1:6"],0,"SA=6×4=24 and V=8, giving ratio 3:1; SA:V declines with increasing side length."],["Which process releases large secreted cargo through vesicle fusion?",["Exocytosis","Simple diffusion","DNA translation","Denitrification"],0,"Exocytosis merges a vesicle membrane with the cell membrane and releases its contents outside."],["A red blood cell placed in a strongly hypertonic solution will generally:",["Lose water and shrink.","Gain water and burst.","Make a cellulose wall.","Immediately double its chromosome number."],0,"The outside has lower water potential, causing net osmotic water loss."]],"images":["membrane","osmosis"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"tissues","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"03 · Human tissues, organisation and exchange surfaces","goal":"Identify four tissue types and link organisation and exchange geometry to body functioning.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["Cells, tissues, organs and systems","A cell is the smallest living structural and functional unit. A tissue contains cells and associated material organised for a shared function. An organ contains several tissue types working together, and an organ system coordinates organs. The heart includes cardiac muscle, connective tissue, nervous tissue and epithelial lining. A label such as “muscle organ” therefore does not mean every cell in that organ is muscle. Systems depend on one another: circulation transports substances exchanged by respiratory and digestive surfaces."],["Epithelial tissue","Epithelial cells form closely packed sheets covering surfaces, lining cavities and forming glands. Their shape and number of layers suit their task. A thin simple squamous layer shortens diffusion distance in alveoli and capillaries; a multilayered surface provides greater resistance to abrasion. Intestinal epithelial cells have microvilli that increase membrane area for absorption. A tissue description should connect arrangement to function rather than only naming a shape."],["Connective tissue","Connective tissue supports, binds and transports. Cells are distributed within an extracellular matrix whose fibres and ground substance differ between tissues. Bone has a mineralised matrix for support; cartilage has a firm flexible matrix; tendons have strongly aligned collagen for transmitting tension. Blood is connective tissue with a fluid matrix, plasma. Matrix properties explain why different connective tissues behave differently despite belonging to the same broad category."],["Muscular and nervous tissue","Muscular tissue produces force by contractile proteins. Skeletal muscle supports voluntary movement, cardiac muscle contracts in the heart and smooth muscle changes the diameter or contents of hollow organs. Nervous tissue includes neurons that receive and transmit signals and glial cells that support their functioning. The nervous system coordinates muscle activity but is not itself the tissue that supplies most mechanical force in movement."],["Exchange geometry","A large exchange surface and a short diffusion path increase potential transfer rate. Folds, villi, microvilli and numerous alveoli increase area without requiring a proportionate increase in body size. Thin barriers reduce the path between two environments. Flow on each side replenishes materials and removes products, maintaining gradients. These adaptations work together: a large but dry, thick or poorly perfused surface can still exchange inefficiently."],["The internal environment","Most cells exchange with interstitial fluid surrounding them rather than directly with outside air or food. Blood transports materials to capillaries, which exchange with tissue fluid; cells then exchange across their membranes. Lymphatic vessels return excess interstitial fluid to the bloodstream. Distinguish the lumen of the gut, which is continuous with the external environment, from absorbed nutrients in the body’s internal environment."],["Epithelial arrangement changes exchange and protection","A thin simple squamous epithelium reduces diffusion distance at an alveolus or capillary, whereas multiple layers protect surfaces exposed to abrasion. Columnar intestinal cells can have microvilli that increase membrane area and transport capacity. Goblet cells secrete mucus, and ciliated epithelial cells move material across a surface. The basement membrane supports the epithelium and separates it from underlying connective tissue. Match the arrangement with the task: a thick protective surface cannot be assumed to exchange gases as rapidly as a very thin one under otherwise comparable conditions."],["Connective tissues differ through cells and matrix","Connective tissue includes loose supportive tissue, adipose tissue, cartilage, bone and blood. Its extracellular matrix varies from the fluid plasma of blood to collagen-rich or mineralised arrangements. Adipocytes store lipid and contribute to insulation; cartilage supports flexible or low-friction structures; bone provides rigid support. Fibres and ground substance are as important as the cell names. A tissue classification is therefore based on organisation and function, not whether it feels solid. Blood qualifies as connective tissue even though its matrix is fluid."]],"mis":"An organ usually contains several tissue types. Naming its main function does not make it one uniform tissue.","worked":["Explain how four tissue categories contribute to the function of a section of intestine.","Epithelial tissue lines the lumen and supports selective absorption and protection. Smooth muscle moves and mixes contents; nervous tissue coordinates activity. Connective tissue supports structures and contains blood or lymphatic vessels transporting absorbed substances."],"workedMore":[["Explain why adding villi and microvilli can improve absorption more effectively than simply making the intestinal wall thicker.","Villi and microvilli increase membrane surface area available for transport. A thin epithelial barrier keeps the movement path short. A thicker barrier increases diffusion distance and does not necessarily increase functional absorptive area."],["Trace oxygen from an alveolus to a muscle-cell mitochondrion, identifying the major exchange barriers.","Oxygen crosses alveolar and capillary walls into pulmonary blood and is carried largely by haemoglobin. Systemic capillaries exchange oxygen into tissue fluid down its gradient. Oxygen crosses the muscle-cell membrane and reaches mitochondria, where aerobic respiration helps maintain demand."]],"practice":[["Predict how a protective epithelium and a gas-exchange epithelium differ, and justify each structure.","A protective surface exposed to abrasion often has multiple layers, providing replacement and resistance to injury. Gas-exchange epithelium is thin, usually one cell layer, reducing diffusion distance. Compare the functions using the stated context; no single epithelial arrangement is best for every organ.",6],["Explain how tendon structure supports its role during movement.","Aligned collagen fibres provide high tensile strength along the direction of pull. The tendon connects muscle to bone and transmits the muscle’s contractile force. It does not contract like skeletal muscle; its connective matrix supports force transmission.",6],["Explain why effective ventilation alone cannot guarantee oxygen delivery to every cell.","Ventilation renews alveolar air but gases must also diffuse across exchange surfaces. Blood flow and haemoglobin transport oxygen to systemic tissues. Tissue perfusion and cell-membrane exchange must remain effective; failure in any link can limit delivery.",6],["Explain why a thin alveolar epithelium and a multilayered protective epithelium have different strengths.","A thin layer reduces gas diffusion distance; multiple layers resist abrasion and preserve a barrier. Each structure favours a different functional demand.",4]],"quiz":["Blood is classified as connective tissue because:",["Its cells are suspended in a fluid extracellular matrix.","It contains only muscle cells.","It has a cellulose wall.","Its cells all transmit nerve impulses."],0,"Its cells are suspended in a fluid extracellular matrix."],"quizzes":[["A thin alveolar epithelium primarily helps by:",["Reducing diffusion distance.","Reducing the exchange surface area.","Preventing all gas movement.","Increasing the length of the airway."],0,"Reducing diffusion distance."],["Which tissue provides contractile force in the heart?",["Cardiac muscle.","Simple squamous epithelium alone.","Bone.","Cartilage."],0,"Cardiac muscle."],["Which best describes an organ?",["Several tissue types cooperating in a structure.","One isolated cell only.","All organ systems combined.","A substance with no cells."],0,"Several tissue types cooperating in a structure."],["Most body cells receive exchanged substances immediately from:",["Surrounding interstitial fluid.","Food still inside the stomach.","Air directly outside the skin.","Urine in the bladder."],0,"Surrounding interstitial fluid."]],"images":["y11-tissues","y11-villi"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]],"tables":[{"title":"Four tissue classes: structure and function","headers":["Tissue","Useful structural feature","Functional example"],"rows":[["Epithelial","Closely arranged cells; specialised surface","Barrier, secretion or absorption"],["Connective","Cells within variable extracellular matrix","Support, transport, storage or connection"],["Muscular","Contractile protein organisation","Force and movement"],["Nervous","Excitable cells and supporting cells","Detection, integration and signalling"]]}]},{"id":"nutrients","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"04 · Biomolecules, nutrients and cell requirements","goal":"Explain the roles of carbohydrates, proteins, lipids, vitamins, minerals and water in metabolism.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["Nutrients support energy and construction","A nutrient is a material the body uses to support functioning. Nutrients can supply energy, building blocks or required chemical components. Oxygen supports efficient aerobic respiration but does not supply the stored chemical energy found in organic fuel molecules. Carbohydrates, lipids and proteins can provide metabolic substrates; vitamins and minerals support specific reactions or structures. Deficiency of one essential material can limit metabolism even when total food energy is adequate."],["Carbohydrates","Carbohydrates include monosaccharides such as glucose and polymers such as glycogen. Digestion breaks suitable dietary carbohydrates into smaller molecules that can be absorbed. Cells can oxidise glucose to transfer energy to ATP, while liver and muscle glycogen provide stores that can be mobilised. Building a polymer uses condensation reactions and breaking suitable bonds by hydrolysis allows digestion. Glycogen storage is not identical to storing ATP: the fuel must be metabolised before much of its energy becomes available for cellular work."],["Proteins","Proteins are polymers of amino acids joined by peptide bonds. Their amino-acid sequence influences folding, and their three-dimensional structure affects function. Enzymes, receptors, antibodies and many structural components are proteins. Digestion generally breaks dietary proteins into smaller peptides and amino acids before absorption; an eaten enzyme is not simply delivered intact to the matching body tissue. Amino acids can be used for new proteins or processed, with excess nitrogen contributing to urea formation."],["Lipids","Triglycerides contain glycerol and fatty acids and form concentrated long-term energy stores. Phospholipids form membrane bilayers, while cholesterol contributes to membrane properties and steroid synthesis. Lipids are not all the same compound and a phospholipid is not a typical triglyceride. Their limited water solubility affects transport and digestion: bile salts emulsify fats, and lipid-derived material follows special absorption and blood or lymph transport routes."],["Vitamins, minerals and cofactors","Vitamins are organic micronutrients and minerals are required inorganic elements or ions. Some contribute to enzyme cofactors or coenzymes, while others have structural or signalling roles. Iron contributes to haemoglobin and calcium supports bone and cellular functions. A cofactor can assist an enzyme without being the enzyme’s protein chain. Having more of a required nutrient does not imply an unlimited rise in reaction rate, because substrate availability, enzyme concentration and other factors may be limiting."],["Water and balanced supply","Water acts as a solvent, participates in reactions, supports transport and contributes to heat exchange. Its polarity helps explain why ions dissolve while many lipids do not. A balanced supply means meeting physiological requirements across different materials, rather than treating every nutrient as interchangeable calories. For an exam response, identify the specific shortage, connect it to a cellular mechanism and explain the likely consequence; avoid claiming that a single food prevents all dysfunction."],["Chemical building blocks explain different nutritional roles","Monosaccharides can supply substrates for respiration and larger carbohydrate synthesis. Amino acids are assembled into proteins, including enzymes, receptors and contractile proteins. Fatty acids and glycerol-related components contribute to energy stores and membrane lipids. An essential nutrient must be obtained in adequate amounts because the body cannot synthesise enough of it. Proteins are not simply a second name for muscle, and lipids are not only unwanted body mass. Digestion, absorption, transport and cellular use are distinct steps between eating food and supporting metabolism."],["Connect micronutrients with a defined mechanism","Iron contributes to haemoglobin function, while calcium supports skeletal structure and other cellular functions. Vitamins can contribute to coenzyme formation or other essential processes; a small required amount does not mean a small biological consequence of deficiency. Water acts as a solvent, transport medium and participant in reactions, and contributes to heat transfer. Explain a deficiency through a particular function rather than claiming every mineral directly supplies ATP. Excess intake can also cause harm; nutritional adequacy concerns appropriate balance and context, not maximising every nutrient concentration."]],"mis":"Digestion releases absorbable building blocks; cellular respiration transfers energy to ATP. They occur at different levels.","worked":["Explain how protein in a meal can contribute to a muscle protein in the body.","Proteases hydrolyse dietary protein into smaller peptides and amino acids. Absorbed amino acids enter transport pathways and become available to muscle cells. Ribosomes assemble a muscle protein using the cell’s genetic instructions; the original food protein is not simply moved intact into the muscle."],"workedMore":[["Compare a triglyceride’s main role with a phospholipid’s role, linking each to structure.","Triglycerides provide concentrated energy storage through their fatty-acid-rich structure. Phospholipids have hydrophilic heads and hydrophobic tails, favouring bilayer formation in water. Their roles overlap with wider metabolism but the two structures are not interchangeable."],["A person has adequate food energy but inadequate iron availability. Explain why cellular functioning may still be compromised.","Adequate total energy does not ensure availability of every required micronutrient. Reduced functional haemoglobin can limit oxygen carriage. Oxygen delivery supports aerobic metabolism, so nutrient roles and transport systems must both be considered."]],"practice":[["Compare glycogen storage with ATP availability during activity.","Glycogen stores glucose units and can be broken down when fuel is required. Cellular respiration transfers some fuel energy to ATP. ATP couples energy transfer to immediate cellular work and is continually regenerated; glycogen is not an ATP polymer.",6],["Explain why an enzyme’s activity may fall despite an unchanged amount of enzyme protein.","A necessary cofactor or coenzyme may be absent, preventing normal catalytic function. pH, temperature, inhibitors or substrate availability may also change enzyme activity. Enzyme quantity alone does not establish the functional reaction rate; identify the relevant factor from evidence.",6],["An advertisement says one vitamin “provides all the energy the body needs”. Evaluate the claim using metabolism.","Major energy-yielding substrates include carbohydrates, lipids and, in suitable conditions, amino acids. A vitamin may support reactions but is not a substitute for all energy-rich fuel, water or other nutrients. Evaluate controlled evidence and exact outcomes rather than accepting a broad claim from a single testimonial.",6],["Why can adequate energy intake coexist with a specific nutrient deficiency?","Energy supply and the supply of essential components are different requirements. A diet may provide enough energy while lacking a vitamin, mineral or other needed nutrient.",3]],"quiz":["What is a protein’s basic building block?",["An amino acid.","A fatty acid only.","A glucose molecule only.","A phosphate ion only."],0,"An amino acid."],"quizzes":[["Which lipid contributes directly to bilayer structure?",["A phospholipid.","Glycogen.","Haemoglobin.","Cellulose."],0,"A phospholipid."],["Which statement about a vitamin is correct?",["It can support metabolism without being an energy-rich fuel in the usual dietary sense.","It replaces the need for all enzymes.","It is always a metal ion.","It gives unlimited reaction rate at any dose."],0,"It can support metabolism without being an energy-rich fuel in the usual dietary sense."],["What is glycogen?",["A glucose-storage polysaccharide.","A DNA base.","An oxygen-transport protein.","A steroid receptor."],0,"A glucose-storage polysaccharide."],["Why can iron deficiency affect oxygen transport?",["Iron is required for functional haemoglobin.","Iron replaces all alveoli.","Iron is the gas being breathed.","Iron converts every lipid into DNA."],0,"Iron is required for functional haemoglobin."]],"images":["y11-carbohydrate-digestion","membrane"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]],"tables":[{"title":"Nutrients and specific biological roles","headers":["Nutrient","Useful component or property","Role"],"rows":[["Carbohydrate","Monosaccharides and larger polymers","Fuel substrates; storage and molecular components"],["Protein","Amino-acid sequence and folding","Enzymes, receptors and structural/contractile functions"],["Lipid","Hydrophobic components; energy-rich stores","Membranes, fuel storage and signalling-related roles"],["Vitamins/minerals","Small required quantities; specific chemistry","Coenzymes, transport proteins and other particular functions"],["Water","Solvent and high heat capacity","Transport, reactions and thermal roles"]]}]},{"id":"enzymes","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"05 · Metabolism, enzyme action and investigations","goal":"Explain enzyme specificity and factors controlling anabolic and catabolic reactions.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["Matter, energy and metabolism","Metabolism is the set of processes through which cells transform matter and energy to maintain life. Anabolic pathways build larger or more complex molecules and generally require energy input. Catabolic pathways break down molecules and can release energy that is captured for other cellular work. Energy and matter are conserved overall, but cells continually exchange both with their surroundings. ATP provides an immediate means of transferring usable chemical energy between reactions, movement and transport processes. The cell's internal organisation helps coordinate these pathways by positioning enzymes and separating incompatible conditions. A metabolic process should be explained through its inputs, products and purpose, not described as a substance disappearing because the cell needed energy."],["Enzymes as biological catalysts","Enzymes are biological catalysts, commonly proteins, that increase reaction rate by providing a pathway with lower activation energy. They do not supply the reaction's overall energy, change its equilibrium position or become permanently consumed in each reaction. A substrate binds at an active site whose properties support the reaction, and products are released so the enzyme can act again. Specificity depends on shape and chemical interactions. Some enzymes need additional non-protein components for activity. Explain how catalysis affects the number of successful reactions per time under the conditions, rather than saying the enzyme gives molecules intelligence or simply makes them move faster. The rate observed also depends on substrate, enzyme concentration and other limiting conditions."],["Lock-and-key and induced-fit models","The lock-and-key model emphasises a complementary fit between substrate and active site. It is useful for introducing specificity but can imply that the site is rigid. The induced-fit model represents a change in enzyme conformation when the substrate binds, helping position groups for catalysis and stabilise relevant interactions. Both are simplified models of molecular processes. A comparison should identify the common substrate–site relationship and the difference in flexibility. Use the model to explain why a changed active site may bind less effectively or why a suitable substrate is preferentially acted upon. Do not claim every binding event guarantees a reaction or that a model diagram shows molecules at their real sizes, positions and timescales."],["Temperature and enzyme activity","Increasing temperature can initially raise reaction rate because particles collide more frequently with sufficient energy. Above a suitable range, changes to enzyme structure can reduce the proportion of functional active sites and lower activity. The balance produces an apparent optimum under the stated assay conditions. Low temperature usually slows reactions without necessarily denaturing the enzyme, while prolonged or extreme heat can cause lasting loss of function. Different enzymes and organisms have different temperature responses, and assay duration can affect the observed optimum. Explain both sides of the curve with distinct mechanisms. A lower product amount at high temperature could also involve changed substrate or assay conditions, so a valid investigation controls relevant variables and measures initial rates where appropriate."],["pH and inhibitory effects","pH affects charges and interactions in an enzyme and its substrate, which can alter binding and catalytic activity. An enzyme's optimum pH depends on its properties and operating environment. Extreme pH can also disrupt structure. Competitive inhibitors can occupy the active site and reduce access by substrate; in a simple reversible model, additional substrate can reduce their relative effect. Other inhibitors act through different sites or mechanisms and may not be overcome in that way. Not all inhibition is reversible, and non-competitive terminology is a simplified model for particular kinetics. Explain the actual interaction stated in a question rather than assuming every inhibitor permanently denatures the enzyme. Cells can regulate metabolic pathways through changes in enzyme activity as well as changes in enzyme amount."],["Substrate, enzyme and product concentrations","When enzyme concentration is fixed, raising substrate concentration initially increases the chance of productive binding. At sufficiently high substrate concentration, most available active sites are occupied and the rate approaches a maximum under those conditions. Adding more enzyme can increase that maximum if substrate and other requirements are available. As a reaction proceeds, substrate depletion or product accumulation may reduce the observed rate, so a final product total is not necessarily equivalent to the initial rate. Some products can inhibit pathways or affect the reverse reaction. State what remains fixed when interpreting a graph. A plateau can indicate saturation, but evidence and controls are needed to distinguish this from limitations caused by another reagent or the measurement method."],["Designing and evaluating an enzyme investigation","A fictional experiment measures product formed per minute at several pH values while keeping enzyme and substrate concentrations, temperature, reaction volume and measurement timing constant. Use appropriate buffer solutions, blanks and independent replicate reactions. Specify how the dependent variable will be measured and express a rate in suitable units. Repeating a reading from the same reaction is not equivalent to several independent reactions. A colour change can indicate product formation only if the assay has been validated and other factors do not alter the colour. Draw conclusions supported by the tested range, such as highest measured activity at pH 7, rather than claiming an exact universal optimum between untested values. Suggest improvements that address identified sources of variation or bias."],["Cofactors and coenzymes","Some enzymes need a nonprotein component to function. A cofactor can be an inorganic ion, while a coenzyme is an organic assisting molecule, often related to a vitamin. These components can help bind a substrate or transfer chemical groups or electrons. Their absence can reduce reaction rate even if the enzyme protein remains present. They do not remove the need for a suitable enzyme active site, and different enzymes have different requirements."]],"mis":"Enzymes lower activation energy. They are not consumed as fuel and do not change the overall energy difference between reactants and products.","worked":["An enzyme yields 2, 5, 9 and 1 units of product per minute at 10, 20, 35 and 60 °C. Describe and explain the trend without claiming an exact optimum.","Rate rises from 2 to 9 units min⁻¹ between 10 and 35 °C, then falls to 1 at 60 °C. Warming initially increases molecular motion and effective collisions; sufficiently high temperature alters protein structure and active-site function. The largest measured value occurs at 35 °C, but measurements at more temperatures are needed to establish the true optimum."],"workedMore":[["Explain why increasing substrate concentration can eventually cease to raise initial reaction rate when enzyme concentration is fixed.","At low substrate concentration, more substrate increases opportunities for enzyme–substrate complexes. At sufficiently high concentration, available active sites are occupied for much of the time and enzyme processing capacity is limiting. Adding more enzyme under suitable conditions can increase maximum rate; adding substrate alone cannot remove that limit."],["Design a comparison testing the effect of pH on one digestive enzyme.","Change pH systematically using suitable buffers while keeping enzyme amount, substrate amount, volume, temperature and measurement duration comparable. Measure an appropriate initial rate or product formation with consistent units and calibrated methods; include an appropriate no-enzyme comparison. Use independent repeats, calculate summaries and report variability; avoid changing acid concentration in a way that also changes uncontrolled reaction conditions."]],"practice":[["Explain why cooling an enzyme and strongly heating it can both reduce activity but have different recovery consequences.","Cooling lowers molecular motion and collision frequency while often leaving enzyme structure intact. Strong heating can denature the protein, changing the active site and functional binding or catalysis. Rewarming after cooling can restore rate under suitable conditions; cooling a denatured enzyme does not necessarily restore its original structure.",6],["A molecule resembles a substrate and binds reversibly at an enzyme’s active site. Explain the effect and predict how raising substrate concentration can alter it.","The inhibitor competes with the substrate for active-site occupancy, reducing productive complexes at a given substrate concentration. More substrate can reduce the inhibitor’s relative occupancy in the simplified reversible competitive model. This does not establish that every inhibitor behaves this way; irreversible and noncompetitive mechanisms have different consequences.",6],["Compare building a protein with breaking down glucose, explaining how the pathways are linked energetically.","Protein construction is an anabolic process requiring building blocks and energy. Glucose breakdown is catabolic and can transfer some released energy to ATP. ATP hydrolysis can be coupled to energy-requiring synthesis; enzymes organise both pathways without creating energy.",6]],"quiz":["What does an enzyme lower?",["Activation energy barrier","Every product’s mass","The equilibrium energy difference","The number of conserved atoms"],0,"Catalysis changes the reaction pathway/barrier rather than the overall energy difference or stoichiometry."],"quizzes":[["What best describes induced fit?",["Binding can change enzyme conformation","The enzyme is always rigid","Any molecule is a substrate","The enzyme is consumed entirely"],0,"Induced fit represents flexible enzyme–substrate interaction and positioning supporting catalysis."],["Why does substrate-dependent rate plateau?",["Available enzyme becomes limiting","All products turn into enzymes","No encounters occur","Temperature must be zero"],0,"At high substrate, active sites are occupied much of the time, limiting further rate increase at fixed enzyme concentration."],["What links amino acids in a protein backbone?",["Peptide bonds","Only hydrogen bonds between DNA bases","Glycosidic bonds in starch","Cellulose walls"],0,"Peptide bonds join amino acids, while folding involves additional interactions that may be altered by denaturation."],["How does cooling usually differ from severe heating?",["It slows encounters without necessarily denaturing","It always destroys every peptide bond","It increases rate without limit","It converts protein into DNA"],0,"Low temperature commonly reduces kinetic activity reversibly; sufficiently high heat can disrupt functional conformation."]],"images":["y11-carbohydrate-digestion"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"respiration","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"06 · Cellular respiration and ATP","goal":"Locate glycolysis and mitochondrial stages and compare aerobic respiration with human anaerobic metabolism.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["ATP and coupled cellular work","ATP transfers usable chemical energy to cellular processes. Hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and inorganic phosphate can be coupled to reactions or movements requiring energy. Energy released through metabolism is used to regenerate ATP, allowing continual turnover rather than long-term stockpiling. The overall free-energy change of hydrolysis matters; merely breaking a chemical bond does not itself release energy, because bond breaking requires input and the complete reaction includes formation and stabilisation of products. At this course level, explain ATP as an immediate energy-transfer molecule connecting energy-releasing and energy-requiring processes. It supports active transport, biosynthesis and movement. A cell needs ongoing metabolic activity because ATP is continually used and its available pool is limited."],["Aerobic respiration and its equation","Aerobic cellular respiration transfers energy from organic compounds into ATP, with oxygen serving as the final electron acceptor in the main respiratory electron transport chain. A simplified balanced equation for glucose is C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O, with energy transferred to ATP and released as heat. The process consists of linked enzyme-controlled stages rather than a single combustion reaction. Respiration occurs in plants and animals, and photosynthetic cells still need ATP for cellular work. Breathing supplies and removes gases at organism level, while cellular respiration is a set of chemical reactions in cells. Keep these meanings separate when discussing an organism's ventilation or oxygen uptake."],["Glycolysis, mitochondrial stages and ATP synthesis","Glycolysis occurs in the cytosol and splits glucose into pyruvate, generating a small net ATP yield and reduced electron carriers. In aerobic eukaryotic respiration, pyruvate is processed in mitochondria and further oxidised through a cycle of reactions in the matrix, releasing carbon dioxide and transferring electrons to carriers. Electron transport on the inner mitochondrial membrane uses these electrons and oxygen, helping create a proton gradient for ATP synthase. Most ATP from aerobic glucose breakdown is associated with this final system. Exact ATP totals vary with conditions and assumptions, so do not treat one historical textbook total as universal unless an exam specifies it. Link each location to its function rather than assigning every respiratory step to mitochondria."],["Factors controlling respiration rate","Respiration rate depends on substrate availability, suitable temperature, enzyme activity and, for aerobic pathways, oxygen supply. Increasing temperature can initially raise metabolic rate, but excessive temperatures can disrupt proteins and membranes. Oxygen limitation can restrict the electron transport chain and alter the balance of pathways used. Different tissues and organisms have different energy demands, so rates should be standardised to mass or another appropriate measure for fair comparison. A higher rate of carbon dioxide release may indicate increased respiration, but the interpretation depends on other processes and the organism. Fermentation in yeast can produce carbon dioxide without aerobic respiration. Explain why the chosen measurement represents the process under the experimental conditions rather than treating any gas production as uniquely diagnostic."],["Investigating energy transformations safely","Respiration can be investigated using suitable sensors or respirometers with a teacher-approved non-pathogenic model, or by analysing appropriate secondary data. A respirometer measures gas-related changes, not every individual ATP-producing reaction. Temperature, leaks, organism amount and any gas-absorption arrangement affect interpretation. Include an appropriate comparison and independent replicates. Human breathing measurements concern whole-body gas exchange and must not be confused with direct measurements of mitochondrial ATP production. Explain what the apparatus measures, how that signal relates to respiration and which assumptions or safety controls the method requires."],["Glycolysis in the cytosol","Glycolysis splits a six-carbon glucose molecule into two three-carbon pyruvate molecules in the cytosol. Energy is initially invested, and subsequent steps give a net gain of two ATP per glucose and reduced electron carriers. Oxygen is not used directly in glycolysis. A lack of mitochondria does not therefore mean a human cell cannot produce any ATP. Sustained glycolysis still requires regeneration of oxidised carriers so that the pathway can continue."],["Mitochondrial processing","When conditions permit aerobic respiration, pyruvate enters mitochondria and is converted to acetyl-CoA, releasing carbon dioxide and transferring electrons to carriers. Acetyl-CoA enters the citric-acid cycle in the matrix, releasing further carbon dioxide and producing more reduced carriers. The inner-membrane electron-transport chain uses transferred electrons to support a proton gradient that drives ATP synthesis. Oxygen is the final electron acceptor, forming water; it is not the direct substrate for every step."],["Human lactate production","If oxidative processing cannot meet demand, pyruvate can be reduced to lactate, regenerating NAD⁺ so glycolysis can continue. The direct ATP yield remains the two net ATP of glycolysis; lactate formation does not add a large new ATP yield. Lactate can later be used as a fuel or contribute to glucose formation through interorgan pathways. It is not simply a useless poison, and delayed muscle soreness should not be attributed solely to lactate remaining in muscle."],["Recovery and model limits","Post-exercise oxygen consumption supports several recovery tasks, including replenishing energy stores, restoring oxygen stores and processing metabolic changes. “Repaying an oxygen debt” is a useful school-level summary but does not mean every litre is used only to remove lactate. Total ATP yield from aerobic glucose metabolism varies with shuttle routes, coupling efficiency and conditions; do not insist that one memorised historical total is universal. The important comparison is a much greater potential ATP yield under aerobic conditions."]],"mis":"Breathing is ventilation. Cellular respiration consists of reactions within cells; oxygen delivery alone does not make ATP.","worked":["Outline the pathway of one glucose molecule through aerobic respiration, stating major locations and products.","Glycolysis in the cytosol produces two pyruvate, a net two ATP and reduced carriers. Pyruvate conversion and the citric-acid cycle in the mitochondrial matrix release carbon dioxide and produce reduced carriers. The inner-membrane electron-transport chain and ATP synthase generate much of the ATP; oxygen receives electrons and contributes to water formation."],"workedMore":[["A mature erythrocyte has no mitochondria. Explain how it can still obtain ATP and why it does not perform the full aerobic pathway.","Glycolysis occurs in the cytosol and can produce a net two ATP per glucose. Lactate formation regenerates NAD⁺ to support continuing glycolysis. Without mitochondria the cell lacks the full citric-acid-cycle and mitochondrial electron-transport machinery."],["In a simplified glycolytic model, a sample metabolises 0.500 mmol glucose. Calculate the net ATP amount from glycolysis alone.","The supplied basic glycolytic yield is two ATP per glucose. Net ATP amount = 2 × 0.500 = 1.00 mmol. This calculates ATP amount, not energy in joules, and excludes additional aerobic ATP production and actual physiological rates."]],"practice":[["Compare glucose metabolism during inadequate oxidative supply and during adequate aerobic processing.","Both can begin with glycolysis in the cytosol. Human lactate formation regenerates NAD⁺ with only the glycolytic net ATP yield, whereas mitochondrial aerobic processing offers a much greater potential ATP yield. Oxygen availability, mitochondrial capacity and demand affect the balance; cells do not necessarily switch instantaneously between completely separate all-or-none states.",6],["Predict why blocking the mitochondrial electron-transport chain can reduce ATP availability even when glucose is present.","Electron transfer and the associated proton-gradient maintenance are impaired. ATP synthase has less usable driving gradient, so oxidative ATP production falls. Glycolysis may supply some ATP if carrier regeneration is possible, but glucose alone does not guarantee normal aerobic energy supply.",6],["Evaluate: “Lactate is a waste that causes all muscle soreness, and recovery oxygen is used only to remove it.”","Lactate can be transported and used in other metabolic pathways, including oxidation and glucose formation. Delayed soreness cannot be explained solely as retained lactate. Recovery oxygen supports several restorative processes; the claim overextends a simplified oxygen-debt model.",6]],"quiz":["Where does human glycolysis occur?",["In the cytosol.","Only in the mitochondrial matrix.","In a chloroplast.","Only inside the nucleus."],0,"In the cytosol."],"quizzes":[["What is oxygen’s direct role in electron transport?",["It acts as the final electron acceptor.","It is the enzyme making glucose.","It supplies all glucose carbon atoms.","It replaces every proton gradient."],0,"It acts as the final electron acceptor."],["Why does lactate formation support glycolysis?",["It regenerates NAD⁺.","It adds thirty ATP directly.","It stores oxygen in DNA.","It converts mitochondria into nuclei."],0,"It regenerates NAD⁺."],["What is the net ATP yield of glycolysis per glucose in the basic model?",["Two ATP.","Zero ATP under every condition.","One hundred ATP.","Thirty-eight ATP from glycolysis alone."],0,"Two ATP."],["Which statement about ATP yield is most accurate?",["Aerobic processing has a much greater potential yield, with actual totals depending on conditions.","Every human cell must always make exactly 38 ATP per glucose.","Anaerobic lactate formation has the highest ATP yield.","Respiration creates energy from nothing."],0,"Aerobic processing has a much greater potential yield, with actual totals depending on conditions."]],"images":["y11-mitochondria","y11-alveoli"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"ventilation","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"07 · Respiratory anatomy and ventilation","goal":"Explain the pathway of air and how pressure changes ventilate human lungs.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["The airway pathway","Air normally enters through the nose or mouth, then travels through the pharynx, larynx and trachea. The trachea branches into two main bronchi, which divide repeatedly into smaller bronchi and bronchioles inside the lungs. Their branching distributes air towards alveoli, the tiny exchange spaces. Distinguish an airway from a blood vessel: air stays within the respiratory passage, while blood remains in neighbouring capillaries. Oxygen must cross an exchange surface before it can enter the blood."],["Protecting delicate exchange surfaces","Nasal passages help warm and humidify incoming air. Mucus traps some inhaled particles, while cilia in conducting airways move mucus towards the throat for removal. Cartilage helps support larger airways and prevent collapse; smaller bronchioles have smooth muscle that changes their diameter. These features serve different functions: cartilage provides support, cilia transport material and mucus traps it. A diagram showing a large bronchus should not be assumed to describe every feature of an alveolus."],["The thorax and pleural membranes","The lungs occupy the thoracic cavity and are surrounded by pleural membranes with a thin lubricating fluid layer between them. The chest wall, diaphragm and associated pressure relationships allow normal lung movement to follow thoracic expansion. The diaphragm is a sheet of skeletal muscle separating thorax and abdomen. Pleural fluid reduces friction; it is not a reservoir of inhaled air. Introducing air into the pleural space disrupts normal pressure coupling and can cause lung collapse."],["Inspiration: volume changes first","During quiet inspiration, the diaphragm contracts and flattens, and the external intercostal muscles help raise the ribs. Thoracic volume increases, the lungs expand and alveolar pressure briefly falls below atmospheric pressure. Air then flows down this pressure difference into the lungs. The causal sequence is muscle contraction → increased volume → lower alveolar pressure → inward airflow. The lungs do not pull air in by actively contracting like skeletal muscle, and oxygen concentration alone does not drive bulk ventilation."],["Expiration and elastic recoil","During quiet expiration the inspiratory muscles relax. Elastic recoil reduces lung and thoracic volume, briefly raising alveolar pressure above atmospheric pressure so air flows out. Quiet expiration is largely passive, although forced expiration recruits muscles such as abdominal muscles and internal intercostals. Airflow stops when the pressure difference disappears, even though the lungs still contain air. Residual air helps prevent complete emptying; a simple breath-volume measurement does not measure every lung-volume compartment."],["Ventilation is different from respiration","Ventilation moves air into and out of lungs. Gas exchange transfers oxygen and carbon dioxide across surfaces, whereas cellular respiration comprises metabolic reactions that transfer energy to ATP. These processes are linked but occur at different locations and scales. A student who states that “respiration occurs in the lungs” may mean breathing, but this is imprecise when a question asks about ATP production. Use the requested biological process explicitly in an exam answer."],["Interpreting breathing measurements","Minute ventilation is tidal volume multiplied by breathing frequency. A tidal volume of 0.50 L with 12 breaths per minute gives 6.0 L min⁻¹. This is total ventilated volume, not necessarily the volume reaching functioning alveoli: conducting passages contain anatomical dead space. Faster, shallow breathing can therefore have different exchange consequences from slower, deeper breathing at the same total minute ventilation. Compare values using consistent units and state which quantity the calculation represents."],["Airway narrowing and treatment mechanisms","In asthma, airway inflammation and variable smooth-muscle constriction narrow conducting passages, increasing resistance to airflow. A bronchodilator reduces smooth-muscle constriction; anti-inflammatory treatment addresses inflammation through a different mechanism. These are explanations of treatment principles, not instructions for choosing medication. A student should link the affected structure to reduced airflow rather than claim that asthma destroys all alveoli. Compare this with emphysema, where damage to alveolar walls reduces exchange area and elastic recoil."]],"mis":"Thoracic expansion lowers pressure and air then moves in. Incoming air does not cause the initial muscle-driven expansion.","worked":["Describe the causal sequence during quiet inspiration.","The diaphragm contracts and flattens, while external intercostal activity raises the ribs. Thoracic and lung volumes increase, reducing alveolar pressure below atmospheric pressure. Air moves into the lungs down the pressure gradient until pressures approach equality."],"workedMore":[["A student has a tidal volume of 450 mL and a breathing rate of 16 min⁻¹. Calculate minute ventilation and state a limitation.","Minute ventilation = tidal volume × frequency. 450 × 16 = 7200 mL min⁻¹ = 7.20 L min⁻¹. The value includes conducting-airway dead-space ventilation, so it is not a direct measurement of alveolar oxygen uptake."],["Explain how asthma and emphysema can both impair breathing through different structural changes.","Asthma involves airway inflammation and variable bronchiolar smooth-muscle constriction, reducing airway diameter. Emphysema damages alveolar walls, reducing exchange area and elastic recoil. Both can impair effective ventilation or exchange, but airway narrowing and alveolar destruction require different explanations."]],"practice":[["Evaluate: “Air enters because the lungs become high pressure when the diaphragm contracts.”","Diaphragm contraction increases thoracic volume. Expansion briefly lowers alveolar pressure below atmospheric pressure. Air enters down that pressure gradient; the quoted direction of the pressure change is incorrect.",6],["Two breathing patterns have the same minute ventilation. Explain why they may not have the same effective alveolar ventilation.","Conducting airways contain dead-space air that does not directly exchange with blood. With very small tidal volumes, a greater fraction of each breath remains in conducting passages. Identical total minute ventilation therefore does not establish identical alveolar ventilation or oxygen uptake.",6],["Explain why air entering the pleural space can interfere with ventilation.","Normal pressure relationships couple lung movement to chest-wall expansion. Air in the pleural space can disrupt that coupling and allow elastic recoil to collapse part or all of the affected lung. The affected lung region then ventilates poorly despite attempted inspiratory muscle contraction.",6]],"quiz":["What directly drives air into lungs during inspiration?",["Alveolar pressure becomes lower than atmospheric pressure.","Blood pressure becomes zero.","Oxygen actively transports air.","The diaphragm relaxes upwards."],0,"Alveolar pressure becomes lower than atmospheric pressure."],"quizzes":[["What contracts during quiet inspiration?",["The diaphragm.","Every alveolus.","Pleural fluid.","The lung tissue as a single skeletal muscle."],0,"The diaphragm."],["What is a principal role of airway cilia?",["Moving mucus and trapped material towards the throat.","Making haemoglobin.","Pumping blood.","Digesting inhaled oxygen."],0,"Moving mucus and trapped material towards the throat."],["Which process produces ATP from glucose?",["Cellular respiration.","Ventilation.","Airway humidification.","Pleural lubrication."],0,"Cellular respiration."],["What mainly produces quiet expiration?",["Relaxation of inspiratory muscles and elastic recoil.","Continuous contraction of the diaphragm.","A complete emptying of all lung air.","Active oxygen transport through the trachea."],0,"Relaxation of inspiratory muscles and elastic recoil."]],"images":["y11-respiratory-tract","y11-ventilation"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"gas-exchange","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"08 · Alveolar exchange and gas transport","goal":"Link alveolar structure, diffusion gradients, haemoglobin and carbon dioxide transport.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["An alveolus is an exchange unit","Alveoli provide a large combined surface area in contact with pulmonary capillaries. Their walls and capillary walls are thin, giving a short diffusion distance. A moist surface allows respiratory gases to dissolve before crossing the barrier. Ventilation replenishes alveolar air, while blood flow replenishes capillary blood; together they help maintain gas gradients. Listing “thin, moist, large surface” earns little explanatory credit unless each feature is connected to its effect on diffusion."],["Opposite oxygen and carbon dioxide gradients","In a functioning alveolus, oxygen partial pressure is generally higher in alveolar air than in the arriving venous blood, so oxygen diffuses into blood. Carbon dioxide partial pressure is generally higher in the arriving blood than in alveolar air, so carbon dioxide diffuses in the opposite direction. These are separate gradients. Neither gas needs to push the other across, and both movements are passive. Partial pressure is a useful way to describe the diffusion tendency of a gas within a mixture."],["Haemoglobin and oxygen transport","Most transported oxygen binds reversibly to haemoglobin in erythrocytes; a smaller fraction dissolves in plasma. Oxygen-rich conditions at the lungs favour loading, while conditions in actively respiring tissues favour unloading. Binding increases blood oxygen-carrying capacity compared with dissolved oxygen alone. Oxygen crosses the alveolar barrier by diffusion before it binds haemoglobin: haemoglobin does not reach out through the wall to collect air. Haemoglobin saturation and the total amount of haemoglobin are related but different quantities."],["Exchange at tissues","At systemic tissue capillaries, oxygen moves from blood towards tissues that consume it during metabolism. Carbon dioxide produced by cells moves towards blood. Blood flow supplies reactants and removes products, helping sustain these gradients. A cell’s ATP demand can influence local oxygen use, but a diffusion gradient remains essential to passive movement. In an answer, identify the exchange location: describing oxygen entering blood at the lungs is not sufficient when the question asks how oxygen reaches a muscle cell."],["Carbon dioxide transport","Carbon dioxide travels in several forms: dissolved in plasma, bound to proteins including haemoglobin, and predominantly as bicarbonate ions. Much bicarbonate formation is supported by reactions within erythrocytes. At the lungs, these processes favour regeneration of carbon dioxide for diffusion into alveoli and expiration. Oxygen and carbon dioxide therefore do not have identical dominant transport forms. Detailed enzyme and ion-exchange mechanisms are useful extension explanations, but a correct core answer identifies bicarbonate as the major form."],["Factors affecting diffusion rate","Exchange rate depends on effective surface area, diffusion distance and the relevant gas gradient, as well as properties of the gas and tissue. Destruction of alveolar walls reduces area; fluid accumulation can increase the distance gases must cross; poorly ventilated regions may have reduced oxygen gradients. A thick exchange barrier is therefore not an improvement merely because it is “stronger”. Effective exchange also requires blood flow, so normal-looking alveoli alone do not guarantee normal whole-body oxygen delivery."],["Interpreting blood-gas evidence","Pulmonary arteries carry blood from the heart towards lungs and are relatively oxygen-poor; pulmonary veins return relatively oxygen-rich blood. Artery and vein names describe direction relative to the heart, not an absolute oxygen-content rule. Anaemia can reduce total oxygen-carrying capacity despite a normal percentage haemoglobin saturation. Avoid treating one measurement as proof of every component of oxygen delivery: ventilation, exchange, haemoglobin amount and circulation contribute separately."],["An exchange investigation","A valid comparison of gas exchange should identify what is measured and control important influences such as activity level, temperature and sampling method. A before-and-after observation with one individual can suggest an effect but provides weak evidence for a general causal claim. Use supplied clinical or simulated datasets rather than deliberately exposing classmates to smoke or hypoxia. Explain uncertainty through sample variation and measurement limitations, and distinguish a correlation from a demonstrated biological mechanism."]],"mis":"Oxygen and carbon dioxide have separate partial-pressure gradients. They do not swap places by an active one-for-one exchange pump.","worked":["Explain how large area, thin walls and continuous ventilation support oxygen uptake.","Large combined area provides more surface across which diffusion can occur. Thin alveolar and capillary walls shorten the diffusion distance. Ventilation replenishes oxygen in alveolar air and, with blood flow, helps sustain an oxygen gradient into blood."],"workedMore":[["Compare the directions of oxygen and carbon dioxide movement at lung and muscle capillaries.","At lungs, oxygen moves from alveoli to blood while carbon dioxide moves from blood to alveoli. At muscle tissues, oxygen moves from blood towards cells while carbon dioxide moves from cells towards blood. Each gas moves passively down its own relevant gradient, maintained by ventilation, circulation and metabolism."],["Two samples have similar haemoglobin oxygen saturation, but one contains substantially less haemoglobin per litre. Explain why saturation alone cannot establish equal oxygen delivery.","Saturation describes the proportion of available haemoglobin oxygen-binding sites occupied. Less haemoglobin per litre means fewer total binding sites and potentially less oxygen carried per litre at the same saturation. Delivery also depends on blood flow and tissue exchange, so percentage saturation alone is insufficient."]],"practice":[["Explain why destruction of alveolar walls can reduce oxygen uptake.","Destruction reduces effective alveolar surface area. Reduced area lowers potential diffusion across the exchange surface for otherwise comparable conditions. Loss of elastic recoil can also impair ventilation, further reducing effective exchange.",6],["Correct: “All carbon dioxide is transported on haemoglobin in exactly the same way as oxygen.”","Carbon dioxide has dissolved, protein-bound and bicarbonate transport forms. Most is transported as bicarbonate, whereas most oxygen is carried bound to haemoglobin. At the lungs carbon dioxide is regenerated for diffusion into alveoli and expiration.",6],["Predict the effect of a thicker alveolar exchange barrier when area and gradients are otherwise equal.","The diffusion distance increases. Gas diffusion is slower under otherwise equal conditions. Lower oxygen uptake can result; increasing structural thickness is not automatically an improvement in exchange.",6]],"quiz":["Why does a thin alveolar wall favour exchange?",["It provides a short diffusion distance.","It actively pumps all gases.","It removes the need for blood flow.","It changes oxygen into carbon dioxide."],0,"It provides a short diffusion distance."],"quizzes":[["What carries most oxygen in normal blood?",["Haemoglobin in erythrocytes.","Platelet DNA.","Lymphatic valves.","Bile salts."],0,"Haemoglobin in erythrocytes."],["What is the principal transported form of carbon dioxide?",["Bicarbonate ions.","Oxygenated glucose.","Solid carbon.","ATP."],0,"Bicarbonate ions."],["What defines an artery?",["It carries blood away from the heart.","It always carries oxygen-rich blood.","It has no wall.","It transports air."],0,"It carries blood away from the heart."],["What can reduce total oxygen capacity despite normal saturation?",["Reduced haemoglobin concentration.","More functional haemoglobin with otherwise equal conditions.","A normal thin exchange barrier.","Normal tidal volume alone."],0,"Reduced haemoglobin concentration."]],"images":["y11-alveoli","y11-circulation"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"circulation","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"09 · Heart, blood vessels and double circulation","goal":"Trace blood through the heart and relate cardiac and vessel structures to pressure and flow.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["Two linked circuits","Human circulation is double circulation: blood passes through the heart between the pulmonary and systemic circuits. The pulmonary circuit moves blood from the right heart to the lungs and back to the left heart. The systemic circuit moves blood from the left heart to body tissues and back to the right heart. This arrangement supports effective pulmonary exchange while maintaining a separate high-pressure systemic supply. The two circuits are connected in series rather than operating as independent supplies of unrelated blood."],["Following a complete pathway","Blood returns from the body through the venae cavae to the right atrium, crosses the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, and leaves through the pulmonary valve and pulmonary arteries. After lung exchange it returns through pulmonary veins to the left atrium, crosses the mitral valve into the left ventricle, then passes through the aortic valve into the aorta. Trace arrows in sequence instead of memorising chambers as an unconnected list. The septum separates left and right sides."],["Chamber walls reflect workload","Atria receive blood and move it into adjacent ventricles, so their walls are relatively thin. Ventricles generate pressure to drive blood out of the heart. The left ventricle has a thicker muscular wall than the right because it normally generates higher pressure for systemic circulation; the right ventricle pumps through the lower-pressure pulmonary circuit. The left and right ventricles normally pump approximately equal volumes over time. A thicker wall therefore does not imply that the left side normally pumps twice the blood volume."],["Valves respond to pressure differences","Heart valves open or close as pressure differences across them change. Atrioventricular valves permit atrium-to-ventricle flow and prevent substantial backflow when ventricles contract. Semilunar valves permit outward flow during ventricular ejection and prevent arterial backflow during ventricular relaxation. Chordae tendineae and papillary muscles help prevent atrioventricular valve prolapse; they do not pull the valves open. Valve failure can impair net forward flow even when the heart muscle continues contracting."],["The cardiac cycle","During filling, the ventricles relax and blood enters through open atrioventricular valves. Atrial contraction adds to ventricular filling. Ventricular contraction raises pressure, closes atrioventricular valves and, once ventricular pressure exceeds arterial pressure, opens the semilunar valves for ejection. Ventricular relaxation then reduces pressure, closes semilunar valves and eventually permits filling again. Systole means contraction and diastole means relaxation; identify which chamber is being described rather than treating every part of the heart as always contracting together."],["Arteries and arterioles","Arteries carry blood away from the heart and generally experience pulsatile pressure. Their muscular and elastic walls withstand pressure and help smooth pressure changes through stretch and recoil. Arterioles regulate distribution of flow through changes in smooth-muscle tone and diameter. A thicker muscular wall is distinct from a larger lumen: narrowing a vessel can increase resistance. Do not describe artery walls as thick because blood is oxygenated; pulmonary arteries demonstrate why that explanation fails."],["Capillaries and veins","Capillary walls consist mainly of a thin endothelial layer that supports exchange with tissues. Their networks provide extensive contact with surrounding cells. Veins return blood towards the heart at comparatively low pressure; many limb veins have valves that reduce backflow. Skeletal-muscle contractions and respiratory pressure changes assist venous return. Veins are not simply passive pipes in which blood returns because oxygen has been used up. Exchange characteristics, pressure and direction each explain a different aspect of vessel structure."],["Cardiac output and coronary supply","Cardiac output is heart rate multiplied by stroke volume, the volume ejected by a ventricle per beat. A rate of 70 beats min⁻¹ and stroke volume of 70 mL give 4900 mL min⁻¹, or 4.9 L min⁻¹. The myocardium itself receives oxygen and nutrients through coronary vessels, rather than obtaining enough oxygen directly from blood inside the chambers. Coronary narrowing can restrict this supply, impairing cardiac muscle function and potentially damaging tissue."],["Interpreting circulatory evidence","A pulse reflects a pressure wave associated with ventricular ejection; it is not a single parcel of blood moving at the same speed as that wave. Blood pressure measurements depend on technique and physiological conditions. A higher heart rate alone does not prove a higher cardiac output because stroke volume can also change. In data questions, calculate the requested quantity, compare like conditions and connect observed differences to a plausible mechanism without claiming that one measurement diagnoses a specific disease."]],"mis":"Arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins return it. Oxygen concentration does not define either vessel type.","worked":["Trace an erythrocyte from the vena cava through lungs to the aorta.","Vena cava → right atrium → tricuspid valve → right ventricle → pulmonary valve → pulmonary arteries. Pulmonary capillaries → pulmonary veins → left atrium. Mitral valve → left ventricle → aortic valve → aorta; the sequence distinguishes circuits and valves."],"workedMore":[["Heart rate increases from 72 to 110 beats min⁻¹, while stroke volume changes from 65 to 85 mL beat⁻¹. Calculate both outputs and the change.","Resting output = 72 × 65 = 4680 mL min⁻¹ = 4.68 L min⁻¹. Later output = 110 × 85 = 9350 mL min⁻¹ = 9.35 L min⁻¹. Increase = 9.35 − 4.68 = 4.67 L min⁻¹; both frequency and volume per beat contribute."],["Explain how arterial, capillary and venous structures suit their functions.","Arterial elastic and muscular walls withstand and modulate relatively high, pulsatile pressures. Thin capillary walls and extensive networks favour exchange with tissues. Low-pressure venous return is assisted by a relatively large lumen, valves in many veins and skeletal-muscle pumping."]],"practice":[["Explain why an incompetent atrioventricular valve can reduce effective forward output.","Ventricular contraction raises pressure while the atrioventricular valve should prevent backflow. An incompetent valve permits some blood to return into the atrium. Part of ventricular ejection therefore contributes to backward flow rather than effective onward circulation.",6],["Explain why ventricular wall thickness does not prove the left ventricle normally pumps more blood than the right.","The circuits are linked in series, so their average outputs must be approximately balanced. The left ventricle faces greater systemic resistance and generates greater pressure. Wall thickness reflects pressure-generating workload rather than a normally greater long-term flow volume.",6],["A student observes a higher pulse rate and concludes cardiac output must have increased. Evaluate the claim.","Cardiac output depends on both heart rate and stroke volume. An increased rate can coexist with reduced stroke volume, so rate alone is insufficient. Measure or estimate stroke volume under comparable conditions before establishing the change in output.",6]],"quiz":["What is the correct route immediately after the right ventricle?",["Pulmonary valve and pulmonary arteries.","Aorta and body tissues.","Mitral valve and left atrium.","Vena cava and liver only."],0,"Pulmonary valve and pulmonary arteries."],"quizzes":[["Why is the left ventricular wall thicker?",["It generates higher pressure for systemic circulation.","It always pumps twice the volume.","It stores oxygen in bone.","It receives only lymph."],0,"It generates higher pressure for systemic circulation."],["What opens a semilunar valve during ejection?",["Ventricular pressure exceeding downstream arterial pressure.","Papillary muscles pulling it open.","The disappearance of haemoglobin.","Atrial pressure falling to zero."],0,"Ventricular pressure exceeding downstream arterial pressure."],["What is cardiac output?",["Heart rate multiplied by stroke volume.","Heart rate divided by blood volume.","The number of red cells in one capillary.","Tidal volume multiplied by breathing rate."],0,"Heart rate multiplied by stroke volume."],["What favours capillary exchange?",["A thin wall and an extensive tissue-contact network.","A thick impermeable cartilage wall.","The absence of plasma.","Valves in every exchange segment."],0,"A thin wall and an extensive tissue-contact network."]],"images":["y11-heart-anatomy","y11-circulation","y11-arteries"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"blood","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"10 · Blood components, ABO and Rh transfusion","goal":"Relate blood components to transport and defence and explain antigen–antibody transfusion compatibility.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["Plasma as a transport medium","Plasma is the liquid portion of blood, consisting largely of water with dissolved ions, proteins, nutrients, wastes and signalling molecules. Albumin contributes to osmotic balance and transports some substances; other plasma proteins contribute to clotting and immunity. Blood is a tissue containing suspended cells and cell fragments within this fluid matrix. Plasma and serum are different: serum is the fluid remaining after clotting has occurred and lacks some clotting components. Do not describe plasma as pure water or as containing only oxygen."],["Erythrocyte specialisation","Mature human erythrocytes have a biconcave form, are flexible and contain abundant haemoglobin. Their shape provides a relatively large surface area and short internal diffusion distances, while flexibility assists passage through narrow capillaries. They lack a nucleus and mitochondria, leaving space for haemoglobin but limiting cellular maintenance and division. Haemoglobin reversibly binds oxygen and contributes to some carbon dioxide transport. A red cell transports oxygen; it does not generate oxygen through respiration."],["Leukocytes and defence","Leukocytes include several cell types with distinct defensive roles. Some ingest microorganisms or debris; lymphocytes contribute to targeted immune responses and immune memory. Unlike mature erythrocytes, leukocytes have nuclei, and many can leave blood vessels to function in tissues. A raised leukocyte count can be associated with several conditions and is not by itself proof of a particular infection. Detailed innate and adaptive pathways are developed in Year 12, but Year 11 requires a clear transport-versus-defence distinction."],["Platelets and haemostasis","Platelets are fragments derived from large bone-marrow cells. At vessel injury they adhere, become activated and help form a platelet plug. A coagulation cascade leads to fibrin formation, stabilising a clot. This reduces blood loss while repair occurs. Platelets are not complete nucleated cells, and a clot is not made simply by red cells sticking together. Distinguish helpful clotting at an injury from an unwanted thrombus that can obstruct blood flow or travel as an embolus."],["ABO antigens and antibodies","In the basic ABO model, group A erythrocytes carry A antigen, group B carry B antigen, group AB carry both, and group O carry neither A nor B antigen. Plasma normally contains antibodies against absent A or B antigens: group A has anti-B, B has anti-A, O has both and AB has neither. Antigens are surface identifiers and antibodies bind specific targets. ABO grouping does not mean cells lack all other antigens; many additional blood-group systems exist."],["Red-cell transfusion compatibility","For a red-cell transfusion, donated erythrocyte antigens must not be attacked by the recipient’s antibodies. A group A recipient can generally receive ABO-compatible A or O red cells, whereas B or AB red cells expose B antigen. Incompatible binding can cause agglutination and haemolysis. “Universal donor” and “universal recipient” are simplified labels applying to particular component and compatibility contexts, not a guarantee that any whole-blood transfusion is safe. Crossmatching and clinical testing remain essential."],["Rh(D) and sensitisation","Rh positive commonly indicates the presence of the D antigen, while Rh negative indicates its absence. Anti-D antibodies are not normally present in an unexposed Rh-negative person in the same way that ABO antibodies are normally present. Exposure to D-positive cells through transfusion or pregnancy can lead to sensitisation. In a subsequent relevant pregnancy, maternal IgG anti-D may cross the placenta and damage fetal erythrocytes. Preventive anti-D treatment is a clinical strategy, not something students should equate with an inherited change in fetal blood group."],["Compatibility depends on the component","Red-cell and plasma compatibility require different reasoning. With red cells, consider donor-cell antigens meeting recipient antibodies. With plasma, consider donor antibodies meeting recipient-cell antigens. Group AB plasma lacks anti-A and anti-B, whereas group O plasma contains both in the basic model. This reverses familiar simplified red-cell compatibility patterns. In an exam, first identify the component and the specified ABO/Rh assumptions; do not apply an unexplained memorised arrow diagram to every transfusion question."],["Reading blood data responsibly","Cell counts, haemoglobin concentration and haematocrit describe related but distinct measurements. A lower erythrocyte count can accompany lower oxygen-carrying capacity, while a platelet deficit can impair haemostasis. Neither conclusion establishes the underlying cause without more evidence. Use units and reference information supplied in a question, compare equivalent samples and identify uncertainties. Students should interpret supplied anonymised data rather than collect or exchange blood themselves. Biological understanding supports explanation, not independent diagnosis."],["Blood components have different treatment aims","Red-cell components can restore oxygen-carrying capacity in appropriate cases of major blood loss or severe red-cell deficiency. Platelet components support clotting when relevant platelet deficits or dysfunction create bleeding risk. Plasma supplies soluble proteins, including clotting factors, for appropriate indications. These are not identical treatments, and compatibility depends on the component and clinical testing. A red-cell ABO teaching table cannot be applied unchanged to plasma. Match the deficient function, the component supplied and a relevant limitation rather than describing every transfusion as adding more red blood cells."]],"mis":"Compatibility depends on the blood component and clinically relevant testing. A simplified red-cell ABO table is not a universal transfusion rule.","worked":["Using only the basic ABO model, explain why group O red cells may be compatible with a group A recipient but group B red cells are not.","A recipient normally has anti-B antibodies. Group B donor erythrocytes carry B antigen, which those antibodies can bind, potentially causing agglutination and haemolysis. Group O erythrocytes lack A and B antigens; this ABO conclusion still does not replace Rh assessment, other compatibility tests or clinical crossmatching."],"workedMore":[["Connect three mature erythrocyte features with their transport function.","Biconcave shape offers relatively large area and short diffusion distances. Flexibility assists passage through small capillaries. Loss of nucleus and many organelles makes more space for haemoglobin, increasing capacity for reversible oxygen binding."],["Explain why the statement “group O is always the universal donor” is unreliable for a plasma transfusion.","Group O plasma normally contains anti-A and anti-B antibodies in the basic ABO model. Those donor antibodies may bind A or B antigens on recipient erythrocytes. Compatibility must consider the transferred component; simplified red-cell donor rules do not apply unchanged to plasma."]],"practice":[["Explain how Rh(D) sensitisation can create a risk in a later relevant pregnancy.","An Rh-negative mother can encounter D-positive fetal erythrocytes and form anti-D following sensitisation. Maternal IgG anti-D can cross the placenta in a subsequent pregnancy with a D-positive fetus. Binding to fetal erythrocytes can cause haemolysis; the risk depends on exposure, sensitisation and fetal antigen status.",6],["Compare the expected consequences of substantially reduced platelets and impaired leukocyte function.","Platelet reduction can impair formation of an initial haemostatic plug and stable clot, increasing bleeding risk. Impaired leukocyte function can reduce effective defence against pathogens. These components have different primary roles; neither observation alone identifies its underlying cause.",6],["Evaluate: “A red cell produces oxygen in its mitochondria and then carries it to tissues.”","Mature human erythrocytes lack mitochondria. They obtain oxygen from lung exchange and carry most of it reversibly bound to haemoglobin. Cellular respiration consumes oxygen in aerobic pathways rather than producing it as the transported product.",6]],"quiz":["Which blood component carries most oxygen?",["Erythrocytes containing haemoglobin.","Platelet nuclei.","Dissolved glucose alone.","Bile droplets."],0,"Erythrocytes containing haemoglobin."],"quizzes":[["What is a platelet?",["A cell fragment involved in haemostasis.","A complete red cell with a nucleus.","An antibody antigen.","A respiratory alveolus."],0,"A cell fragment involved in haemostasis."],["Which antibodies normally occur in group A plasma?",["Anti-B.","Anti-A only.","Both anti-A and anti-B.","Neither anti-A nor anti-B."],0,"Anti-B."],["What best explains ABO-incompatible red-cell transfusion harm?",["Recipient antibodies bind donor-cell antigens.","Donor oxygen changes into glucose.","All donor cells lack membranes.","Recipient cells make bile."],0,"Recipient antibodies bind donor-cell antigens."],["How does Rh sensitisation differ from a simple ABO rule?",["Anti-D can develop following exposure to D-positive erythrocytes.","All Rh-negative people are born with high anti-D levels.","D antigen is found only in plasma water.","Rh status prevents all ABO reactions."],0,"Anti-D can develop following exposure to D-positive erythrocytes."]],"images":["y11-blood-components","y11-abo"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"],["Australian Red Cross Lifeblood: blood components","https://www.lifeblood.com.au/health-professionals/products/blood-components"]]},{"id":"lymph","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"11 · Lymph formation, return and defence","goal":"Explain how the lymphatic system links fluid balance, lipid absorption and defence.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["From plasma to tissue fluid","Fluid exchange across capillary walls supplies the interstitial environment around cells. Water and small solutes can move across the wall, while blood cells and most large plasma proteins normally remain within the circulation. The interstitial fluid connects capillary exchange with cells; blood does not need to flow directly into every cell. Some fluid and escaped proteins enter lymphatic capillaries and are eventually returned to the bloodstream. This prevents continual accumulation of interstitial fluid under normal conditions."],["Formation of lymph","Lymph is interstitial fluid after it enters lymphatic vessels. Initial lymphatic capillaries have structures that permit entry of fluid and larger material, with overlapping endothelial arrangements acting as one-way entry pathways. Lymph moves into larger collecting vessels and ultimately returns to venous circulation near the base of the neck. It is not identical to whole blood: normal lymph does not contain the same erythrocyte concentration and its composition varies with the drained tissue."],["Returning fluid without a central pump","The lymphatic system does not have a central pump equivalent to the heart. Skeletal-muscle movement, respiratory pressure changes and contraction of lymphatic vessel smooth muscle help move lymph. Valves reduce backward flow. Obstruction or damage can reduce drainage and contribute to swelling, called lymphoedema. State the causal chain: impaired drainage → retained interstitial fluid → tissue swelling. Do not explain every swollen tissue as a proven lymphatic blockage, because other mechanisms also cause oedema."],["Lymph nodes as defensive meeting points","Lymph passes through nodes containing immune cells that encounter material draining from tissues. Macrophages and other cells can capture material, while lymphocyte interactions support immune responses. A lymph node is not a gland that produces digestive enzymes or a pump that forces all blood through the lungs. Node enlargement can accompany several immune or other conditions and is not a diagnosis by itself. The Year 12 course develops specific cell interactions and immune memory in detail."],["Lacteals and lipid absorption","Lacteals are lymphatic capillaries within intestinal villi. Many absorbed long-chain lipid products are reassembled and packaged into chylomicrons by intestinal cells, then enter lacteals. This lipid-rich intestinal lymph eventually reaches the bloodstream through lymphatic return. Water-soluble nutrients such as many sugars and amino acids mainly enter blood capillaries and travel towards the liver in portal circulation. A villus contains both pathways, so saying that all nutrients enter either blood or lymph is inaccurate."],["A linked-system explanation","The lymphatic system helps maintain circulating fluid, transports absorbed lipid and provides routes for immune surveillance. These roles link cardiovascular, digestive and immune systems rather than forming three unrelated lists. In an exam, identify which role the question tests and name the relevant structure: a lacteal for intestinal lipid transport, a valve-bearing collecting vessel for one-way return, or a node for defensive interactions. A single generic statement that lymph “cleans the body” does not explain these mechanisms."],["Follow a fluid and protein return pathway","Fluid in an interstitial space can enter blind-ended lymphatic capillaries, pass into collecting vessels with valves, traverse nodes and eventually join larger ducts returning to venous blood near the neck. Overlapping endothelial arrangements assist entry when local pressure conditions favour it, while vessel valves reduce backflow. Returning escaped proteins helps maintain fluid distribution as well as conserving material. Do not draw all lymph returning directly into an artery or label a lymph node as the structure pumping blood through a capillary. The cardiovascular and lymphatic pathways are connected but have different flow organisation."],["Use a three-system explanation after a meal","Digestive enzymes generate lipid products; intestinal epithelial cells absorb and package many long-chain lipids; lacteals receive the resulting chylomicrons. Lymphatic return eventually introduces these products into blood, which transports them to tissues. At the same time, blood capillary exchange supports the intestinal cells’ oxygen and nutrient needs. This traces digestive processing, lymphatic transport and cardiovascular distribution through distinct structures. A damaged villus can therefore affect more than one transport route, while an obstructed lymph vessel can impair lipid transport without directly stopping every glucose carrier in the epithelium."]],"mis":"Lymph is tissue fluid after entry into lymphatic vessels. It is not a second circulation pumped by a separate heart.","worked":["Explain how damaged lymph drainage can contribute to limb swelling.","Fluid normally moves into interstitial spaces during capillary exchange. Lymphatic vessels return excess fluid and escaped proteins towards venous blood. Reduced drainage allows material and fluid to accumulate, increasing interstitial volume and producing swelling."],"workedMore":[["Trace an absorbed long-chain lipid from intestinal processing towards blood.","Digested lipid products enter intestinal epithelial cells and are commonly reassembled into triglycerides. They are packaged into chylomicrons, which enter villus lacteals. Lymphatic transport ultimately delivers them into venous circulation, rather than directly into the initial villus blood capillaries."],["Explain how lymph can return despite lacking a central heart-like pump.","Muscle contraction and respiratory pressure changes help move fluid through lymph vessels. Some collecting vessels contribute smooth-muscle contraction. Valves limit backflow, supporting net movement towards venous return."]],"practice":[["Give two differences and one functional connection between blood and lymph.","Blood normally has abundant erythrocytes and circulates through a heart-driven circuit. Lymph derives from interstitial fluid, varies by drainage site and moves through a one-way return network. Lymph returns fluid and proteins to blood, helping maintain circulating volume.",6],["Explain why a villus needs both blood capillaries and a lacteal.","Blood capillaries receive many water-soluble absorbed nutrients, including sugars and amino acids. Lacteals receive many packaged long-chain lipids as chylomicrons. The two routes connect digestion with different initial transport pathways before materials ultimately reach circulation.",6],["Why does an enlarged lymph node not independently prove a specific bacterial infection?","Nodes contain immune cells responding to material and signals from drained tissues. Several infectious and noninfectious causes can produce enlargement. A specific cause requires further appropriate evidence rather than inference from size alone.",6]],"quiz":["What is lymph initially derived from?",["Interstitial fluid entering lymphatic capillaries.","Air inside bronchi.","Bone mineral dissolved in bile.","Urine flowing backwards."],0,"Interstitial fluid entering lymphatic capillaries."],"quizzes":[["What helps lymph move without a central heart-like pump?",["Muscle movement and one-way valves.","Only red-cell contraction.","A separate lymph heart in the skull.","Conversion of fluid to air."],0,"Muscle movement and one-way valves."],["What enters lacteals after intestinal processing?",["Many chylomicrons carrying long-chain lipids.","All glucose instead of blood.","Undigested bone.","All intact dietary proteins."],0,"Many chylomicrons carrying long-chain lipids."],["What can impaired lymph drainage cause?",["Accumulation of interstitial fluid and swelling.","Immediate removal of all tissue fluid.","Universal elevation of oxygen content.","Conversion of lymph nodes into alveoli."],0,"Accumulation of interstitial fluid and swelling."],["What occurs in lymph nodes?",["Immune cells encounter material draining from tissues.","All blood oxygenation.","Urine filtration.","Enzymatic digestion of all stomach contents."],0,"Immune cells encounter material draining from tissues."]],"images":["y11-lymph-capillaries","y11-lipid-absorption"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"mechanical-digestion","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"12 · Digestive anatomy and mechanical processing","goal":"Trace the alimentary pathway and distinguish mechanical processing, propulsion and chemical digestion.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["An alimentary pathway","Food passes through mouth, pharynx, oesophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, rectum and anus. The lumen is continuous with the external environment; nutrients must cross an epithelial barrier to enter internal tissues. Accessory organs contribute secretions without food passing through their tissue: salivary glands, liver, gallbladder and pancreas connect to the pathway through ducts. Drawing the liver directly within the food tube is therefore a structural error."],["Chewing increases access","Teeth cut, tear and grind food while the tongue positions it and mixes it with saliva. Mechanical fragmentation increases exposed surface area, allowing digestive enzymes more access to suitable substrates. Chewing does not by itself break every protein into amino acids; chemical bonds require chemical digestion. Tooth shapes reflect different mechanical roles, and saliva lubricates as well as supplying digestive substances. A bolus is the formed mass prepared for swallowing."],["Swallowing and airway separation","Swallowing moves a bolus from mouth through pharynx into the oesophagus. Coordinated protective movements reduce entry into the airway. The oesophagus leads to the stomach and is distinct from the trachea, which leads towards the lungs. Normal swallowing is not simply food falling down by gravity: coordinated muscular activity contributes. A labelled diagram should keep these pathways separate and make clear that nutrients are not absorbed by routing the bolus through bronchi."],["Peristalsis and segmentation","Peristalsis involves coordinated waves of smooth-muscle activity that propel luminal contents along the tract. Segmentation-type contractions mix material, especially in the intestine, improving contact with secretions and surfaces. These patterns can coexist; propulsion and mixing are functions rather than exclusive definitions of every contraction. The process uses muscular movement, not cilia carrying the bolus. If an exam asks how food progresses through the oesophagus, explicitly link contraction patterns with movement towards the stomach."],["The stomach as a mixer","Stomach muscular movements mix swallowed material with gastric secretions, producing chyme and regulating delivery to the small intestine. A mucus-containing protective barrier helps separate the epithelium from the acidic, enzyme-containing lumen. Acid provides a suitable environment for certain gastric processes and can alter protein structure, but acid is not itself a protein enzyme. Mechanical churning improves contact and mixing; it must be distinguished from enzymatic hydrolysis of substrates."],["Bile and emulsification","The liver produces bile, and the gallbladder stores and concentrates it before release into the small intestine. Bile salts help disperse lipid into small droplets, increasing accessible surface area for lipase and helping lipid processing. Emulsification is physical dispersion rather than enzymatic cleavage of triglyceride bonds. Bile is not manufactured by the gallbladder, is not a digestive enzyme and does not produce amino acids from protein. Its physical role complements subsequent chemical digestion."],["Integrating structure and process","A meal is progressively mixed, fragmented, chemically digested and transported, with most nutrient absorption occurring in the small intestine. The stomach is an important processing chamber but does not absorb all nutrients into blood. Mechanical actions change food size or distribution; chemical digestion changes molecular structure. In an extended answer, trace the location, action and purpose of each process rather than claim that any movement counts as complete digestion."],["Trace the anatomical sequence and local functions","Food passes through mouth, pharynx, oesophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, rectum and anus. Teeth and tongue process and position food; swallowing transfers a bolus while coordinated airway protection reduces entry into the trachea. Peristaltic contractions move contents through the muscular tract. The stomach stores and mixes, the small intestine combines chemical processing with major nutrient absorption, and the large intestine recovers water and ions and forms faecal material. Accessory organs deliver secretions through ducts; food does not normally pass through the liver or pancreas themselves."],["Distinguish propulsion from mixing","Peristalsis uses coordinated contraction and relaxation along a tract segment to move contents. Segmentation mixes intestinal contents and renews their contact with secretions and absorptive surfaces. Gastric churning mixes food with secretions to form chyme. Mechanical processing increases access or redistributes material without necessarily hydrolysing its molecules. Bile salts emulsify lipid droplets, increasing the surface available to lipase. In a prediction task, explain which process is impaired: poor mixing, reduced onward transport and reduced chemical hydrolysis are related consequences but are not interchangeable descriptions of one mechanism."]],"mis":"Emulsification separates lipid into droplets; it does not break the chemical bonds in triglycerides.","worked":["Compare chewing, peristalsis and enzymatic digestion.","Chewing mechanically fragments food and increases exposed surface area. Peristalsis uses coordinated contractions to propel contents through the tract. Enzymatic digestion catalyses chemical breakdown of suitable substrates into smaller molecules."],"workedMore":[["Correct: “The gallbladder makes an enzyme that digests fat into amino acids.”","The liver produces bile and the gallbladder stores and concentrates it. Bile salts help emulsify lipid physically; bile is not a digestive enzyme. Lipases catalyse lipid hydrolysis; amino acids are protein-digestion products rather than fat products."],["Trace food from mouth to small intestine and identify accessory contributions.","Mouth → pharynx → oesophagus → stomach → small intestine. Salivary glands contribute saliva to the mouth. Pancreatic secretions and bile reach the small intestine through ducts; the food does not pass through the pancreas or liver itself."]],"practice":[["Explain how stomach churning supports digestion without being identical to chemical digestion.","Muscular movements mix contents with gastric secretions. Mixing increases contact between food and relevant luminal substances. Movement is mechanical processing; enzyme-catalysed molecular hydrolysis is chemical digestion.",6],["A student diagram places the trachea between mouth and stomach. Correct the pathway and explain the functional distinction.","The oesophagus is the food passage from pharynx towards stomach. The trachea is an airway leading towards bronchi and lungs. Swallowing requires coordinated airway protection; food should not normally travel through the respiratory passage.",6],["Two otherwise equal food samples contain large pieces or finely chewed particles. Predict an initial difference in digestion rate under equal enzyme conditions.","Finely divided particles expose a greater combined accessible surface area. More substrate surface can contact enzymes, potentially increasing the initial rate. The prediction assumes comparable composition and conditions and does not imply every particle is fully digested instantly.",6]],"quiz":["Which is an accessory digestive organ?",["The pancreas.","The oesophagus lumen.","The trachea.","The aorta."],0,"The pancreas."],"quizzes":[["What does emulsification do?",["Disperses lipid into smaller droplets without itself enzymatically cleaving it.","Converts all protein to glucose.","Manufactures red cells.","Removes all need for lipase."],0,"Disperses lipid into smaller droplets without itself enzymatically cleaving it."],["Where is bile produced?",["The liver.","The gallbladder only.","The alveoli.","The kidney."],0,"The liver."],["What is a principal role of peristalsis?",["Propelling contents through coordinated muscular activity.","Transcribing dietary DNA.","Pumping oxygen into blood.","Filtering urine."],0,"Propelling contents through coordinated muscular activity."],["Why does chewing help enzymatic digestion?",["It increases exposed food surface area.","It makes every digestive enzyme unnecessary.","It changes amino acids into teeth.","It actively absorbs all nutrients through enamel."],0,"It increases exposed food surface area."]],"images":["y11-digestive-tract","y11-peristalsis"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]],"tables":[{"title":"Production, storage and action are separate","headers":["Source","Secretion / function","Delivery or action"],"rows":[["Salivary glands","Saliva and amylase","Mouth"],["Liver","Bile production","Delivered to small intestine via biliary pathways"],["Gall bladder","Bile storage and concentration","Releases stored bile into a delivery pathway"],["Pancreas","Digestive enzymes and bicarbonate","Small intestine"],["Stomach glands","Acid and pepsin-related secretions","Stomach lumen"]]}]},{"id":"chemical-digestion","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"13 · Digestive enzymes and accessory secretions","goal":"Explain substrate-specific digestion in changing gastrointestinal conditions.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["Hydrolysis makes absorbable products","Chemical digestion breaks large food molecules into smaller products through enzyme-catalysed hydrolysis. Carbohydrates are processed towards monosaccharides, proteins towards amino acids and small peptides, and triglycerides towards fatty acids and monoglycerides. The products are not interchangeable: lipase does not normally turn triglycerides into amino acids. Enzymes have appropriate substrates and conditions. Digestion supplies material for absorption; cellular respiration later transfers energy from suitable fuels to ATP in cells."],["Carbohydrate digestion","Salivary amylase begins starch breakdown in the mouth. Its activity changes as the bolus reaches the acidic stomach. Pancreatic amylase acts in the small intestine, and enzymes associated with the intestinal brush border complete processing of appropriate disaccharides and other carbohydrate products to monosaccharides. Amylase does not convert every dietary carbohydrate directly into glucose. Lactase processes lactose; reduced lactase activity can leave lactose in the lumen, influencing osmotic balance and microbial fermentation."],["Protein digestion","Gastric pepsin acts in acidic conditions on proteins, producing smaller peptide material. Pancreatic proteases and intestinal peptidases continue processing in the small intestine. Some proteases are secreted as inactive precursors, reducing the risk of digesting the cells and ducts that produce them before activation in the appropriate location. Hydrochloric acid supports the gastric environment and protein denaturation but should not be labelled a protease. Different enzymes work sequentially rather than one enzyme doing every step."],["Lipid digestion","Bile-mediated emulsification increases the surface area of lipid droplets available to enzymes. Pancreatic lipase is a major contributor to triglyceride hydrolysis in the small intestine, producing fatty acids and monoglycerides in the basic model. Bile salts help form structures that deliver lipid products towards the intestinal surface. Physical emulsification and chemical hydrolysis are complementary processes, not competing answers. The final absorption and transport route differs from that of many water-soluble nutrients."],["Neutralising gastric chyme","Pancreatic and other intestinal secretions provide bicarbonate that helps neutralise acidic chyme entering the small intestine. This protects tissue and establishes conditions suitable for many intestinal digestive enzymes. Enzyme activity depends on pH because protein shape and interacting chemical groups can change. A low stomach pH suits pepsin but should not be generalised as the ideal pH for every enzyme. Secretions therefore do more than simply add water to food."],["Digestive dysfunction and evidence","Reduced enzyme production, a blocked duct or damaged intestinal surfaces can impair different stages of nutrient processing. A blocked pancreatic duct may reduce delivery of digestive enzymes and bicarbonate, whereas reduced lactase specifically affects lactose processing. Match the affected structure or enzyme to its consequence. Symptoms alone cannot establish one diagnosis; supplied investigation data and mechanisms support a reasoned explanation. Avoid treating all intolerance, allergy and malabsorption as one identical condition."],["Locate secretion sources and their delivery routes","Salivary glands release saliva containing amylase into the mouth. Gastric glands contribute acid and pepsin-related secretions to the stomach. The pancreas supplies enzymes and bicarbonate through ducts into the small intestine. The liver produces bile, while the gall bladder stores and concentrates it before delivery. Brush-border enzymes operate at the intestinal epithelial surface. This separates production, storage and site of action. A substance can be made in one organ and act in another, so identify both locations when explaining a duct obstruction or interpreting an organ-system diagram."],["Explain operating conditions through the enzyme model","Pepsin is suited to an acidic gastric environment; many pancreatic enzymes operate effectively after acidic chyme is neutralised in the small intestine. Changes in pH alter the ionisation and arrangement of chemical groups involved in substrate binding and catalysis. Severe conditions can disrupt protein structure, while smaller changes may alter activity without complete permanent denaturation. Temperature, available substrate, enzyme concentration and inhibition also affect reaction rate. A digestion answer should therefore name substrate, enzyme, products, location and suitable conditions rather than memorise a list of enzyme names alone."]],"mis":"Bile is not a digestive enzyme, and stomach acid is not a protease. Mechanical and chemical actions complement one another.","worked":["Explain why acidic stomach contents require a different environment for many pancreatic digestive enzymes.","Gastric acid supports acidic processes such as pepsin activity. Bicarbonate-containing secretions help neutralise chyme entering the small intestine. The changed pH supports relevant intestinal enzymes and protects tissue; enzymes do not all share one optimum."],"workedMore":[["Trace chemical protein digestion through stomach and small intestine.","Pepsin acts in the acidic stomach to break proteins into smaller peptide material. Pancreatic proteases and intestinal peptidases continue hydrolysis in the small intestine. Amino acids and absorbable small peptides result; acid itself is not a protease."],["Explain their complementary roles in lipid digestion.","Bile salts disperse lipid into smaller droplets, increasing accessible surface area. Lipase catalyses hydrolysis of triglycerides to smaller lipid products. Physical emulsification assists enzymatic action but does not replace chemical bond breakdown."]],"practice":[["Predict digestive consequences of reduced pancreatic secretion delivery.","Less enzyme delivery can impair digestion of several nutrient classes. Less bicarbonate delivery can impair neutralisation and suitable intestinal conditions. Effects depend on severity and compensation; the problem is secretion delivery rather than direct absorption by the pancreas.",6],["Explain why reduced lactase can affect lactose digestion without eliminating all carbohydrate digestion.","Lactase acts on lactose specifically. Other enzymes still process their appropriate carbohydrate substrates. Unprocessed lactose can affect luminal water balance and fermentation, but this does not mean every carbohydrate pathway fails.",6],["Evaluate: “Stomach acid is an enzyme that finishes all protein digestion.”","Acid is not a protein catalyst such as pepsin. It helps create the gastric environment and alter protein structure. Further pancreatic and intestinal enzyme action normally continues protein digestion in the small intestine.",6],["A duct is blocked between pancreas and intestine. Explain two effects beyond simply less digestion.","Reduced enzyme delivery impairs specific substrate hydrolysis. Reduced bicarbonate delivery can leave less suitable intestinal pH and compromise relevant enzyme activity and tissue protection.",4]],"quiz":["Which enzyme acts on starch?",["Amylase.","Lipase.","Pepsin acting as a starch enzyme.","DNA polymerase."],0,"Amylase."],"quizzes":[["What is a major role of intestinal bicarbonate?",["Helping neutralise acidic chyme.","Making the intestine more acidic than the stomach in every case.","Emulsifying oxygen.","Making haemoglobin."],0,"Helping neutralise acidic chyme."],["What are basic products of triglyceride digestion?",["Fatty acids and monoglycerides.","Only amino acids.","Only nucleotides.","Intact bone cells."],0,"Fatty acids and monoglycerides."],["Why are some proteases secreted as inactive precursors?",["To help limit premature digestion of producing tissues.","To ensure they never become active.","To replace every digestive enzyme.","To allow oxygen production."],0,"To help limit premature digestion of producing tissues."],["Where does much pancreatic enzyme action occur?",["In the small-intestinal lumen.","Inside alveoli.","Inside the gallbladder only.","In the urinary bladder."],0,"In the small-intestinal lumen."]],"images":["y11-carbohydrate-digestion","y11-digestive-tract"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]],"tables":[{"title":"Digestive enzymes and operating environments","headers":["Region / source","Substrate and enzyme example","Products / conditions"],"rows":[["Mouth / salivary glands","Starch / salivary amylase","Smaller carbohydrates; activity changes after acid exposure"],["Stomach / gastric glands","Protein / pepsin","Peptides; acidic environment"],["Small intestine / pancreas","Starch / pancreatic amylase","Smaller carbohydrates; bicarbonate-supported environment"],["Small intestine / pancreas","Protein/peptides / proteases","Smaller peptides; subsequent peptidase action"],["Small intestine / brush border","Disaccharides / specific disaccharidases","Monosaccharides for absorption"],["Small intestine / pancreas","Triglycerides / lipase","Fatty acids and monoglycerides; emulsification assists access"]]}]},{"id":"absorption","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"14 · Villi, nutrient absorption and elimination","goal":"Explain intestinal transport routes and distinguish egestion from excretion.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["Villi increase exchange area","The small intestine has folds, villi and epithelial microvilli that increase absorptive surface area. A villus contains blood capillaries and a lymphatic lacteal beneath a thin epithelial barrier. Large area and relatively short transport distances support efficient uptake. These are biological structures rather than tiny teeth grinding food. Absorption means transferring substances across the intestinal lining into internal transport pathways; it is distinct from the prior chemical breakdown that makes many substrates absorbable."],["Sugar and amino-acid uptake","Monosaccharides and amino acids cross intestinal epithelial cells through appropriate transport proteins and then commonly enter blood capillaries. Sodium-coupled transport can move some substrates into cells using an ion gradient maintained indirectly by ATP-dependent pumps. Other steps use facilitated diffusion through carriers. It is inaccurate to say all glucose moves through a simple open channel or that every stage is directly ATP-powered. The portal vein carries much absorbed water-soluble material towards the liver."],["Lipid uptake and chylomicrons","Lipid digestion products are delivered towards the intestinal surface with help from bile-associated micelles. Many long-chain products enter epithelial cells, are reassembled into triglycerides and packaged into chylomicrons. These particles enter lacteals and travel through lymph before reaching blood. Shorter-chain lipid products can follow different routes, including portal transport. The school-level lacteal pathway is valuable but should not be extended into the claim that every lipid molecule follows an identical route."],["Water, ions and the large intestine","Water is absorbed in both small and large intestine; the large intestine is not the only site of water absorption. Ion transport and osmotic gradients influence water movement. The colon also handles remaining contents and supports a microbial community, with composition influenced by several factors. Water retention or excessive loss changes stool consistency, but a single stool observation cannot identify a unique cause. Link altered water balance to physical consequences without treating every intestinal problem as an enzyme defect."],["Egestion and excretion differ","Faeces contain undigested or unabsorbed material, microorganisms and shed cells, among other components. Removal through rectum and anus is egestion, also called elimination of intestinal contents. Excretion removes metabolic wastes produced by cellular processes, such as urea through kidneys and carbon dioxide through lungs. Some excreted substances enter the digestive tract through bile, showing that pathways can overlap. The defining distinction is the origin of the material, not simply which body opening it uses."],["Damage to absorptive surfaces","Damage or flattening of villi reduces effective absorptive area and may impair nutrient uptake even if food has been chemically digested. Conversely, enzyme failure can leave fewer absorbable products despite an intact surface. An explanation should identify which stage is disrupted and how this changes transfer. Use supplied evidence to distinguish proposed mechanisms; symptoms such as weight change alone do not prove villus damage. Different digestive conditions require different causal chains."],["Follow glucose across a villus and towards tissues","Intestinal folds, villi and microvilli increase exchange area at different organisational levels. Many monosaccharides and amino acids cross an epithelial layer through specific transport mechanisms and enter villus blood capillaries. Sodium-linked uptake and other carriers illustrate why some absorption depends indirectly on energy-maintained ion gradients. Blood from much of the digestive tract reaches the liver through hepatic portal circulation before general distribution. Distinguish the lumen, epithelial cell, tissue fluid and capillary rather than drawing food molecules jumping directly from the gut into every body cell."],["Follow lipid products through a different route","Bile-associated structures help deliver fatty acids and monoglycerides to the epithelial surface. Many long-chain lipid products enter cells, are reassembled into triglycerides and are packaged with other components into chylomicrons. These enter lacteals and then lymphatic return before reaching blood. Shorter lipid products can follow different routes, so this is a useful major pathway rather than a rule that every fat-related molecule always enters lymph. Water and ions are also absorbed along the tract; the large intestine contributes recovery and faecal formation after much small-intestinal absorption has already occurred."]],"mis":"Not all nutrients take the same transport route. Many sugars enter blood capillaries; chylomicrons enter lacteals.","worked":["Explain how a villus supports nutrient absorption.","Villi and microvilli increase surface area. A thin epithelium gives a relatively short route across the barrier. Blood capillaries and lacteals receive different absorbed materials and support onward transport."],"workedMore":[["Compare initial transport of glucose and many long-chain dietary lipids.","Glucose crosses epithelium using appropriate carriers and commonly enters blood capillaries and portal circulation. Many long-chain lipid products are reassembled and packaged into chylomicrons within epithelial cells. Chylomicrons enter lacteals and lymph before venous blood, so initial routes differ."],["Classify faecal removal and urinary urea removal and justify each.","Faecal elimination primarily removes remaining intestinal contents and is egestion. Urea arises from metabolic nitrogen processing and its removal is excretion. The classification depends on material origin, not simply on whether it is unwanted."]],"practice":[["Explain why flattened villi can reduce nutrient uptake even when digestive enzymes remain active.","The effective absorptive surface area decreases. Fewer absorbable products may cross into blood or lymph per unit time. Chemical digestion and absorption are distinct stages, so normal enzyme action does not guarantee normal uptake.",6],["Explain why sodium-coupled glucose uptake can depend indirectly on ATP.","A maintained sodium gradient provides a driving force for coupled uptake. ATP-dependent pumps help maintain that ion gradient. The coupled carrier need not itself split ATP at every glucose-transfer step; direct and indirect energy dependence differ.",6],["Correct: “The small intestine absorbs food but no water; all water absorption happens in the colon.”","The small intestine absorbs substantial water as well as nutrients. The large intestine also absorbs water and ions from remaining contents. Relative amounts depend on conditions; assigning all water uptake to the colon is inaccurate.",6],["Trace a long-chain dietary lipid from hydrolysis to general blood circulation.","Lipase generates products; intestinal cells absorb and reassemble many long-chain lipids, package them into chylomicrons and deliver them to lacteals. Lymphatic return eventually introduces them into blood.",5]],"quiz":["What is the principal benefit of intestinal microvilli?",["Increased absorptive surface area.","Blood pumping.","Production of teeth.","Complete prevention of all pathogens."],0,"Increased absorptive surface area."],"quizzes":[["Where do many absorbed long-chain lipids initially enter?",["Lacteals as packaged chylomicrons.","The trachea.","Only red-cell nuclei.","The urine directly."],0,"Lacteals as packaged chylomicrons."],["What carries much water-soluble absorbed material towards the liver?",["Hepatic portal blood.","Only the pulmonary artery.","The ureter.","Airway mucus."],0,"Hepatic portal blood."],["What distinguishes egestion from excretion?",["Egestion removes intestinal contents; excretion removes metabolic wastes.","Egestion produces all ATP.","Excretion refers only to faeces.","They are defined solely by colour."],0,"Egestion removes intestinal contents; excretion removes metabolic wastes."],["Where is water absorbed?",["In both small and large intestine.","Only in the mouth.","Only in the large intestine.","Only in alveoli after a meal."],0,"In both small and large intestine."]],"images":["y11-villi","y11-lipid-absorption"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"excretion","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"15 · Liver, kidneys and nephron processing","goal":"Explain metabolic nitrogen disposal and distinguish filtration, reabsorption and secretion.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["Metabolic waste and deamination","Excretion removes products of metabolism that must not accumulate excessively. Carbon dioxide leaves through lungs, while kidneys remove urea and regulate the composition of urine. In the liver, amino-acid nitrogen can be removed during deamination. Toxic ammonia-related nitrogen is converted into urea, which enters blood and is transported to the kidneys. The remaining carbon skeleton can enter different metabolic pathways; it is not invariably converted straight into carbohydrate. Urinary urea is not simply undigested protein that travelled directly from intestine to bladder."],["The urinary pathway","Each kidney receives blood through a renal artery and returns it through a renal vein. Urine produced within the kidney drains through the renal pelvis and ureter to the bladder, then leaves through the urethra. The ureter and urethra have different locations and functions. The kidney cortex and medulla contain regions of nephrons and associated vessels. Urine and blood remain separate fluid pathways, with selected transfers across barriers rather than whole blood flowing into the bladder."],["Nephron structure","A nephron includes a renal corpuscle containing the glomerulus and Bowman’s capsule, followed by tubular regions including proximal tubule, loop of Henle and distal tubule. Collecting ducts receive fluid from several nephrons and contribute to its final processing. Capillaries around tubules support exchange with blood. A labelled nephron model should distinguish the vascular route from the tubular route. The glomerulus is a capillary network, not a hollow urine-storage sac."],["Pressure filtration","Pressure drives movement of water and small solutes from glomerular blood across a filtration barrier into Bowman’s space. Glucose, amino acids, ions and urea can enter the initial filtrate, while blood cells and most large proteins are normally retained. Filtration is not a perfect decision-making sieve that selects only waste molecules. Useful small substances are filtered too, making subsequent selective reabsorption essential. Abnormal cells or substantial protein in urine can indicate a disrupted process but do not independently establish a specific diagnosis."],["Selective reabsorption","Tubular cells return useful substances from filtrate towards blood through appropriate transport mechanisms. Normally, nearly all filtered glucose and amino acids are reclaimed under ordinary conditions, along with large amounts of water and selected ions. Movement can involve active transport, coupled transport, facilitated diffusion and osmosis at different steps. Reabsorption does not mean that the kidney swallowed urine back into the stomach. Identify direction clearly: tubular fluid → cells/interstitium → blood."],["Tubular secretion","Secretion transfers selected substances from blood or tubular cells into tubular fluid, contributing to removal of some ions, drugs and other materials and to acid–base regulation. Its direction differs from reabsorption. A substance’s final urinary amount reflects filtration, reabsorption and secretion together. If a question supplies quantities, use excreted = filtered − reabsorbed + secreted with compatible units and the stated assumptions. Do not identify all material entering a tubule as having arrived solely through glomerular filtration."],["Final urine and course boundaries","Final urine normally contains water, urea and varying amounts of ions and other small wastes, with composition depending on physiological conditions. Bladder storage occurs after kidney processing; the bladder is not the principal filtration organ. This Year 11 module explains structures and transfers. Detailed ADH, aldosterone, thirst and feedback regulation belong to the Year 12 homeostasis course. Knowing where those later mechanisms act helps integration without replacing the required structural explanation."],["Interpreting clearance-style data","Suppose a simplified dataset states that 100 mg of a substance is filtered, 70 mg reabsorbed and 5 mg secreted in a given period. Excreted amount is 100 − 70 + 5 = 35 mg. This is an amount for that period, not automatically a concentration; concentration also depends on urine volume. Keep mass, volume and time units distinct. A single abnormal measurement needs contextual evidence, because production rate, transport capacity, hydration and sampling can all influence observations."],["Separate filtration, reabsorption and secretion by direction","Filtration moves water and small dissolved substances from glomerular blood into the nephron under pressure across a selective barrier. Reabsorption moves useful substances from tubular fluid towards surrounding tissue and blood. Secretion moves selected substances from blood or surrounding tissue into the tubule. Blood cells and most large proteins normally remain in blood during filtration. A molecule found in final urine may have entered by filtration or secretion; its final amount also depends on reabsorption. Use arrows and named compartments to explain the process rather than treating the kidney as a single sieve."],["Connect liver metabolism with renal removal","Amino acids cannot be stored indefinitely as a dedicated excess amino-acid reserve. Deamination removes nitrogen-containing groups during processing in the liver, and nitrogen is converted into urea for transport and renal excretion. The remaining carbon skeleton can enter other metabolic pathways. Urea is produced mainly through liver processes and removed through kidneys; those are different organ roles. Carbon dioxide is another metabolic waste removed largely by the lungs. This shows why the excretory system is a functional grouping and why removing undigested food from the gut is a different process."]],"mis":"Excretion removes metabolic wastes; egestion removes unabsorbed digestive material. Passing faeces is not identical to kidney excretion.","worked":["Distinguish filtration, reabsorption and secretion by location or direction.","Filtration transfers water and small solutes from glomerular blood into Bowman’s space under pressure. Reabsorption returns selected material from tubular fluid towards blood. Secretion transfers selected material from blood or tubular cells into tubular fluid."],"workedMore":[["During one period, 160 mg of a substance is filtered, 125 mg reabsorbed and 8 mg secreted. Calculate the excreted amount.","Excreted = filtered − reabsorbed + secreted. 160 − 125 + 8 = 43 mg in the stated period. The result is an amount, not a concentration; urine volume would be needed to calculate concentration."],["Explain how amino-acid metabolism links liver function to urinary urea.","Deamination removes amino-acid nitrogen and can generate toxic ammonia-related material. The liver converts nitrogen into urea, which enters blood. Kidneys process this circulating urea and remove a portion in urine; protein does not simply flow intact from intestine to bladder."]],"practice":[["Explain why finding glucose in initial filtrate is normal but substantial glucose in final urine may need investigation.","Glucose is small enough to enter ordinary glomerular filtrate. Tubules normally reabsorb nearly all filtered glucose under ordinary conditions. Final urine therefore depends on filtered load and reabsorptive capacity; an abnormal finding is not itself a complete diagnosis.",6],["Correct a diagram that labels the tube from kidney to bladder as urethra and the bladder as the filtration organ.","The kidney-to-bladder tube is the ureter. The urethra carries urine from bladder towards the exterior. Filtration occurs in renal corpuscles within kidneys; the bladder chiefly stores urine.",6],["Why is “the kidney filters only toxins” an inaccurate explanation?","The initial barrier permits many small useful substances, including glucose and amino acids, as well as wastes. Selective tubular reabsorption recovers much useful material. Final urine results from multiple processes rather than a waste-only first filter.",6]],"quiz":["Where is urea primarily formed?",["The liver from metabolic nitrogen processing.","The bladder by chewing protein.","Alveoli from oxygen.","Platelets from bile."],0,"The liver from metabolic nitrogen processing."],"quizzes":[["What normally remains outside initial filtrate?",["Blood cells and most large proteins.","All water.","All glucose.","All urea."],0,"Blood cells and most large proteins."],["What is reabsorption?",["Movement from tubular fluid back towards blood.","Movement of urine from bladder into mouth.","Only blood entering a kidney.","Production of all new alleles."],0,"Movement from tubular fluid back towards blood."],["What is secretion?",["Transfer of selected substances into tubular fluid from blood or tubular cells.","Only urine leaving the urethra.","Only oxygen entering haemoglobin.","Blood cells dividing in Bowman’s space."],0,"Transfer of selected substances into tubular fluid from blood or tubular cells."],["What connects a kidney to the bladder?",["A ureter.","The urethra.","The trachea.","The aorta lumen directly."],0,"A ureter."]],"images":["nephron"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]],"tables":[{"title":"Nephron processes: identify direction","headers":["Process","Direction","Important distinction"],"rows":[["Filtration","Blood → nephron lumen","Pressure-driven; cells and most large proteins retained"],["Reabsorption","Tubular fluid → tissue/blood","Recovers useful filtered material"],["Secretion","Blood/tissue → tubular fluid","Adds selected substances to fluid destined for excretion"]]}]},{"id":"skeleton","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"16 · Bone, cartilage and the human skeleton","goal":"Relate skeletal organisation and living bone tissues to support, movement and health.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["Axial and appendicular organisation","The axial skeleton includes the skull, vertebral column and thoracic cage; the appendicular skeleton includes limbs and their supporting girdles. Together they support body shape, protect organs and provide attachment points and levers for movement. The skull protects the brain, vertebrae protect the spinal cord and the rib cage helps protect thoracic organs. Bone also participates in mineral storage and houses marrow involved in blood-cell formation. It is a living tissue, not simply an inert frame."],["Bone matrix and strength","Bone contains cells embedded in a matrix with collagen and mineral components. Collagen contributes tensile properties, while mineral contributes rigidity and resistance to compression. Their combination supports strength without making bone completely unbreakable. Compact bone forms dense regions, while spongy bone contains organised trabeculae and spaces. A porous internal structure does not mean a bone is empty or useless; arrangement and distribution of material matter to strength, mass and function."],["Bone cells and remodelling","Osteoblasts contribute new bone matrix, osteocytes maintain and sense aspects of the surrounding tissue, and osteoclasts resorb bone. Bone remodelling balances formation and resorption through life, allowing repair and adaptation to loading. The cells do not all perform the same job. A net loss of bone can occur when resorption exceeds formation over time, but its cause requires context. Name both the cellular process and its tissue consequence when explaining an altered-bone dataset."],["Cartilage has different properties","Cartilage contains chondrocytes in a matrix suited to supportive flexibility or smooth joint surfaces depending on its type. Articular cartilage covers many synovial joint surfaces, reducing friction and spreading loads. Compared with bone, cartilage has limited direct blood supply, which contributes to slow or restricted repair in some locations. Cartilage is not soft bone with identical cells and vascularity. A model should distinguish articular cartilage, compact bone and the surrounding joint capsule."],["Levers and protection","Bones act as levers moved by skeletal muscles through tendons, while joints provide movement axes or constraints. A long bone has structural features that distribute force without requiring solid dense material throughout its entire volume. Protection and movement can impose different demands: the skull encloses delicate organs whereas a limb arrangement permits varied movement. Explain a specific structure–function relationship rather than state that every skeletal part exists solely for support."],["Osteoporosis and osteoarthritis","Osteoporosis involves reduced bone strength associated with loss of bone mass and changes in architecture, increasing fracture risk. Osteoarthritis primarily affects joints through changes including articular cartilage damage and associated tissue responses. They are not identical conditions, and neither should be explained as merely “less calcium” without further mechanism. Ageing, activity, nutrition and other factors influence musculoskeletal health; course examples explain mechanisms and evidence rather than prescribe an individual treatment."],["Interpreting skeletal evidence","Bone density measurements inform assessment but do not capture every feature of fracture risk, which can also depend on architecture, loading and falls. Compare measurements using the same method and suitable reference information. A diagram of a denser or thinner trabecular network supports a structural interpretation, not a universal diagnosis from appearance alone. Safe classroom study uses models and supplied data, with conclusions scaled to the evidence and its uncertainties."],["A long bone combines strength, supply and repair","A long bone has a shaft with a strong compact-bone region and ends containing organised spongy bone, with articular cartilage at relevant joint surfaces. Marrow occupies internal spaces, while blood vessels supply living cells. A periosteal covering supports attachment, supply and repair processes. Osteons in compact bone arrange matrix around vascular channels; trabeculae provide internal support without a solid dense block throughout the bone. Connect these arrangements to loading, mass, cell survival and repair rather than describing the skeleton as a uniform collection of calcium rods."],["Compare bone disease and joint disease through the affected tissue","Osteoporosis increases fracture susceptibility through reduced bone mass and altered architecture. Osteoarthritis involves changes in the joint, including articular cartilage and associated tissues, affecting movement and pain. Their prevention and management considerations differ because the affected structures and processes differ. Loading, muscle strength, nutrition, falls risk and appropriate clinical treatment can all matter in context. A bone-density result does not directly measure cartilage wear, and a painful joint does not establish osteoporosis. Explain the evidence and mechanism before considering what a proposed intervention could change."],["Connect management principles with the affected structure","Adequate calcium supply and vitamin-D-related absorption support mineral availability for bone, while appropriate mechanical loading can stimulate maintenance and adaptation. Muscle strength and balance can reduce falls-related forces and fracture opportunities. These mechanisms complement, rather than replace, context-specific clinical treatment. For osteoarthritis, suitable physical therapy and muscle support can improve function and joint control without guaranteeing regrowth of lost articular cartilage. Joint replacement changes the articulating arrangement when appropriate. Distinguish supporting bone strength, reducing injury risk and improving a damaged joint’s function; they are different biological aims."]],"mis":"Bone is living tissue with cells and a remodelled matrix. Cartilage is a distinct tissue, not merely immature bone.","worked":["Explain why bone is a living tissue rather than an inert mineral rod.","Bone contains cells with formation, maintenance and resorption functions. Its collagen–mineral matrix is remodelled in response to biological conditions and loading. It supports repair, mineral storage and marrow functions as well as mechanical support."],"workedMore":[["Explain why both collagen and mineral contribute to bone function.","Collagen contributes tensile properties and some flexibility. Mineral contributes rigidity and resistance to compression. Their combined matrix supports mechanical performance; either component alone does not fully model normal bone."],["Compare osteoporosis and osteoarthritis using affected structures and consequences.","Osteoporosis reduces bone strength through changes in mass and architecture, increasing fracture risk. Osteoarthritis involves joint changes including articular cartilage damage, potentially causing pain and reduced function. Bone fragility and joint-surface dysfunction are different mechanisms despite possible coexistence."]],"practice":[["Predict an outcome if bone resorption persistently exceeds formation.","Osteoclast-mediated removal exceeds osteoblast-mediated deposition over time. Net bone material can decline and architecture may weaken. Mechanical strength can fall, although severity and causes require further evidence.",6],["Compare cartilage and bone in a synovial joint.","Articular cartilage provides a relatively smooth, load-distributing contact surface. Bone provides a rigid supporting structure beneath it. Differences in matrix, cells and blood supply mean their repair and mechanical properties are not identical.",6],["Why does a lower bone-density measurement not alone quantify every aspect of fracture risk?","Density describes one relevant property but not all architectural features. Loads, falls and other biological factors also affect whether a fracture occurs. Interpret the measurement with suitable reference values and context rather than as a complete mechanical prediction.",6]],"quiz":["Which bone cell resorbs bone?",["An osteoclast.","A chondrocyte acting as a neuron.","An erythrocyte.","A pancreatic beta cell."],0,"An osteoclast."],"quizzes":[["What contributes strongly to bone tensile properties?",["Collagen.","Air alone.","Bile salts.","DNA codons alone."],0,"Collagen."],["What is part of the axial skeleton?",["The vertebral column.","Only the fingers.","The shoulder girdle alone.","The femur alone."],0,"The vertebral column."],["How do osteoporosis and osteoarthritis differ?",["Osteoporosis affects bone strength; osteoarthritis primarily affects joints.","They are identical names for the same enzyme failure.","Both only destroy erythrocytes.","Neither involves tissue structure."],0,"Osteoporosis affects bone strength; osteoarthritis primarily affects joints."],["What helps explain limited articular cartilage repair?",["Its limited direct blood supply.","Its abundant coronary arteries.","The absence of any cells.","Its role in DNA replication."],0,"Its limited direct blood supply."]],"images":["y11-bone"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"joints","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"17 · Joint structure, movement and stability","goal":"Explain synovial joint structures and relate joint shape to permitted movement.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["Joint categories","A joint is a connection between skeletal elements. Fibrous joints, cartilaginous joints and synovial joints differ in connecting tissues and permitted movement. Some skull connections allow very little movement, whereas many limb synovial joints permit substantial movement. “Joint” does not mean every connection is freely movable. Classifying a joint requires attention to anatomy rather than simply whether a person reports pain there. Different structural categories support different balances of stability, protection and mobility."],["Synovial joint organisation","A typical synovial joint has articular cartilage covering opposing bone surfaces, a joint cavity containing synovial fluid and an enclosing capsule. The synovial lining contributes fluid, while the capsule and associated ligaments support containment and stability. The cavity separates the surfaces rather than fusing them into one bone. A simplified diagram should label cartilage and fluid separately: cartilage is a tissue attached to surfaces, whereas fluid lubricates the space between them."],["Lubrication and load distribution","Articular cartilage provides smooth surfaces and spreads loads. Synovial fluid contributes lubrication and supports transport of nutrients to articular cartilage. These functions reduce friction while maintaining useful movement. Neither means that a joint experiences no force or can never wear or be injured. A damaged contact surface can impair movement even if surrounding bone remains intact. Explain the relationship between surface condition and mechanical consequences rather than state only that cartilage is “soft”."],["Ligaments and tendons","Ligaments connect bone to bone and help constrain movement and maintain alignment. Tendons connect muscle to bone and transmit force generated by contraction. Both are connective tissues, but their typical attachment relationships differ. A tendon is not normally the structure carrying an antibody to a joint, and a ligament does not generate active contraction like muscle. Joint stability also depends on bone shape, capsule, muscle control and other structures, not ligaments alone."],["Hinge and ball-and-socket geometry","A hinge-type joint primarily supports flexion and extension around a dominant axis, as in a simplified elbow model. Ball-and-socket joints such as shoulder and hip permit movement in several directions, including rotation. The shoulder has extensive mobility while the hip provides substantial load-bearing stability. These are structure–function comparisons rather than absolute statements that a hinge has no other small movement or that every ball-and-socket joint has identical range. State the main allowed movements and relevant constraints."],["Movement terminology","Flexion generally decreases the angle between connected parts, while extension increases it. Abduction moves a limb away from the body’s midline and adduction moves it towards that reference. Rotation turns a structure around its axis. Use the anatomical reference frame in a diagram rather than assuming “up” always means flexion. The movement name describes a change in position; the muscles and joint shape explain how that movement occurs."],["Injury and evidence","Ligament damage can reduce passive joint restraint, whereas tendon damage can interrupt transmission of muscle force. Arthritis affects joint tissues through different mechanisms, so the label alone is not one universal causal explanation. Imaging and functional assessment provide complementary evidence: a structural image does not directly measure every movement or pain experience. Student activities should use safe models or supplied data rather than force joints beyond a normal comfortable range."],["Structural and functional classifications answer different questions","Fibrous joints connect bones through fibrous tissue, cartilaginous joints use cartilage, and synovial joints contain an organised cavity with supporting tissues. Functional categories describe permitted movement, ranging from little or none to freely movable. A structural name therefore does not merely restate an action such as flexion. Skull sutures illustrate restricted movement; intervertebral arrangements permit limited coordinated movement; many limb joints allow substantial movement. The precise movement depends on articular shape, capsule, ligaments and muscular control, so classify the joint from its actual structure and function."],["Stability and mobility involve a trade-off","A synovial capsule encloses the joint, synovial fluid assists lubrication, and articular cartilage helps distribute load. Ligaments constrain excessive displacement, while surrounding muscles and tendons contribute dynamic stability. A ball-and-socket joint permits movement in several planes, whereas a hinge predominantly permits flexion and extension. Greater range does not guarantee greater resistance to displacement. During an injury explanation, identify the damaged tissue and the lost mechanical role rather than stating that every joint injury is torn cartilage or that ligaments actively contract to move bones."]],"mis":"A tendon connects muscle to bone; a ligament usually links bones. Neither is the articular cartilage covering a joint surface.","worked":["Explain the roles of cartilage, fluid and ligaments in a synovial joint.","Articular cartilage provides smooth contact surfaces and distributes loads. Synovial fluid lubricates and supports nutrient transfer to articular cartilage. Ligaments connect bones and constrain excessive movement, contributing to alignment and stability."],"workedMore":[["Compare a hinge and ball-and-socket joint in terms of movement.","A hinge primarily permits flexion and extension around a dominant axis. A ball-and-socket arrangement permits movement in several directions, including rotation. Range also depends on surrounding tissues and geometry; all examples do not have identical mobility."],["Predict a primary mechanical consequence of severing a tendon compared with damaging a stabilising ligament.","A tendon transmits muscle force to bone, so interruption can impair active movement. A ligament constrains bone relationships, so damage can reduce stability or allow excess movement. Actual effects depend on the particular structure and other supporting tissues."]],"practice":[["A diagram shows a straight elbow bending so the angle between upper arm and forearm decreases. Name and explain the movement.","The movement is elbow flexion in the stated reference. The angle between the connected segments decreases. Joint geometry permits the motion while muscle-generated force is transmitted through tendons.",6],["Explain why two ball-and-socket joints can differ in mobility and stability.","The depth and geometry of the socket differ. Capsules, ligaments and surrounding muscle support differ. Ball-and-socket classification permits multi-axis movement but does not prescribe one identical range or stability.",6],["Evaluate: “Synovial fluid is the same thing as cartilage and contracts to bend the joint.”","Cartilage is a tissue covering articular surfaces, while synovial fluid occupies the joint space. Fluid contributes lubrication and transport, not active skeletal-muscle contraction. Muscles generate force transmitted through tendons, with the joint structures guiding and supporting movement.",6]],"quiz":["What connects muscle to bone?",["A tendon.","A ligament in the basic attachment definition.","A lacteal.","A bronchus."],0,"A tendon."],"quizzes":[["What commonly connects bone to bone?",["A ligament.","A tRNA.","An alveolus.","A ureter."],0,"A ligament."],["What characterises a synovial joint?",["A cavity containing synovial fluid between articular surfaces.","Complete fusion of all opposing bones.","A cavity containing only air.","The absence of cartilage and connective tissue."],0,"A cavity containing synovial fluid between articular surfaces."],["What is abduction?",["Movement away from the body’s midline reference.","Any reduction in a joint angle.","Only breathing in.","Any movement of glucose."],0,"Movement away from the body’s midline reference."],["Which joint shape generally permits movement around several axes?",["Ball-and-socket.","A simplified hinge only.","An immovable skull connection.","A DNA double helix."],0,"Ball-and-socket."]],"images":["y11-synovial-joint","y11-joint-types"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]],"tables":[{"title":"Compare joint organisation","headers":["Structure","Example","Movement interpretation"],"rows":[["Fibrous","Skull sutures","Little movement in the useful adult model"],["Cartilaginous","Intervertebral arrangements","Limited movements can combine across several levels"],["Synovial hinge","Elbow","Predominantly flexion/extension"],["Synovial ball-and-socket","Hip / shoulder","Several movement planes; stability depends on arrangement"]]}]},{"id":"muscle","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"18 · Muscle fibres and the sliding-filament model","goal":"Explain sarcomere shortening, calcium regulation and ATP roles in contraction.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["Organisation from muscle to sarcomere","A skeletal muscle contains bundles of muscle fibres surrounded by connective tissues. A fibre is a long specialised cell containing many myofibrils. Myofibrils contain repeating sarcomeres, whose organised filament arrangements create a striated appearance. The hierarchy matters: a sarcomere is not the whole muscle and a myofibril is not the same scale as a muscle fibre. Connective tissue and tendons transmit combined forces towards skeletal attachments."],["Thin and thick filaments","Sarcomeres contain thin filaments including actin and thick filaments containing myosin. Myosin heads can interact with suitable actin sites to form cross-bridges. The filaments overlap in an organised arrangement between Z lines. During ordinary shortening, the actin and myosin filaments do not themselves become shorter; their relative positions change and overlap increases. A model showing shrinking filament lengths therefore misrepresents the central mechanism of sliding-filament contraction."],["Cross-bridge cycling","Under suitable activation conditions, myosin heads bind actin and undergo a force-producing change that moves thin filaments relative to thick filaments. ATP binding assists detachment of myosin from actin, and ATP hydrolysis supports re-energising the head for another cycle. Repeated cycles across many cross-bridges contribute to contraction. ATP is not used only after movement ends, and calcium is not the energy molecule replacing ATP. Distinguish attachment, force production, detachment and resetting in a causal explanation."],["Calcium regulates access","Activation leads to calcium release within the muscle fibre. Calcium interacts with regulatory proteins associated with the thin filament, changing the position of tropomyosin and exposing actin binding sites. This permits cross-bridge cycling when the other conditions are met. Removal of calcium from the cytosol supports relaxation. Calcium therefore has a regulatory role, while ATP supplies chemical energy and supports detachment and pumping. Detailed excitation pathways are developed through the nervous-system course."],["What changes during shortening","As thin filaments slide inwards, Z lines approach one another and the sarcomere shortens. The I band and H zone generally decrease, while the A band retains its length because thick filament length is unchanged. A labelled diagram should compare equivalent regions before and after contraction. The model does not imply every fibre always shortens when it develops force: muscle can generate tension while held at approximately constant length during an isometric action."],["Energy demand and relaxation","Cross-bridge activity and ion transport require ATP, supplied through interacting metabolic pathways. Mitochondrial capacity and oxygen supply support sustained aerobic production, while other pathways support changing demands. Relaxation is not simply the absence of all energy use: calcium pumping requires energy, and ATP enables cross-bridge detachment. Depleted ATP can therefore interfere with relaxation as well as contraction. Link muscle physiology with the earlier respiration module rather than treating ATP as an unrelated vocabulary term."],["Reading model limitations","A two-dimensional sarcomere sketch shows alignment and overlap but omits full three-dimensional arrangements, biochemical interactions and coordinated activation of whole fibres. It can explain band changes without predicting an individual’s maximum lifting force. Muscle strength depends on several factors including size, activation and mechanical leverage. Use the model for the mechanism it represents and avoid inferring that every drawn myosin head moves in perfect synchrony."],["Sequence the cross-bridge cycle with ATP roles","When binding sites become available, a myosin head can attach to actin. Release of products associated with its ATP cycle supports the power stroke, pulling the thin filament relative to the thick filament. Binding of a new ATP molecule permits detachment; ATP hydrolysis returns the head to a configuration ready for another cycle. Many heads work asynchronously, producing tension while filaments overlap. ATP is therefore required for detachment and preparation as well as overall muscle activity. Running out of ATP is not a normal controlled method of muscle relaxation."],["Explain activation, relaxation and visible sarcomere changes","Calcium released within the muscle fibre binds to regulatory proteins, changing the arrangement that normally blocks actin binding sites. When calcium is actively returned to storage, those regulatory arrangements reduce continued cross-bridge attachment. During shortening, Z lines approach, the I band and H zone generally decrease, and the A band remains approximately the same length because thick filaments retain their length. Whole-muscle tension also depends on how many fibres are activated and their loading conditions. A drawing should identify which dimension changes rather than shrinking every line on the page."]],"mis":"During sarcomere shortening, filaments slide past one another; the actin and myosin filaments themselves do not become shorter.","worked":["Explain sarcomere shortening without stating that actin and myosin filaments shrink.","Activated myosin heads interact with exposed actin sites and cycle to move thin filaments relative to thick filaments. Overlap increases and Z lines move closer. Filament lengths remain approximately unchanged; shortening occurs through relative sliding."],"workedMore":[["Distinguish their roles in muscle contraction and relaxation.","Calcium regulates exposure of actin sites through thin-filament regulatory proteins. ATP supports cross-bridge detachment and re-energising, rather than calcium serving as the energy source. ATP-dependent calcium handling supports relaxation, so relaxation also has energy requirements."],["Predict I-band, H-zone and A-band changes during sarcomere shortening.","The I band decreases as overlap increases. The H zone decreases as thin filaments move further into the central region. The A band remains approximately unchanged because thick-filament length is unchanged."]],"practice":[["Evaluate: “ATP is only needed to make the first contraction; relaxation requires no energy.”","Repeated cross-bridge cycling requires ATP-related steps, including detachment and resetting. ATP-dependent calcium pumping helps return the fibre towards relaxed conditions. Insufficient ATP can interfere with relaxation as well as repeated force production.",6],["A student holds a load still and says the muscles must be inactive because the limb is not moving. Evaluate.","Muscle can develop tension during an isometric action with little overall length change. Force can balance the load even when the joint angle remains stable. Cross-bridge and ion-handling processes can still consume ATP; lack of movement does not prove lack of muscle activity.",6],["State what a sarcomere diagram explains well and why it cannot alone predict maximum lifting force.","It represents filament arrangement, overlap and band changes during shortening. It omits whole-muscle size, activation patterns and limb leverage. Force performance requires those additional properties and conditions, not just counting lines in a simplified sketch.",6]],"quiz":["What shortens in the basic sliding-filament model?",["The sarcomere, as filament overlap changes.","The actin molecules into half-length molecules.","The myosin filament itself in every cycle.","The DNA backbone."],0,"The sarcomere, as filament overlap changes."],"quizzes":[["What remains approximately unchanged during normal sarcomere shortening?",["A-band length.","Distance between Z lines.","H-zone width in all conditions.","I-band width in all conditions."],0,"A-band length."],["What is an important role of ATP binding at myosin?",["Supporting detachment from actin.","Replacing calcium with haemoglobin.","Making every filament shorter.","Preventing all enzyme activity."],0,"Supporting detachment from actin."],["What does calcium help regulate?",["Exposure of suitable actin binding sites.","The production of bone marrow erythrocytes only.","Direct conversion of actin to ATP.","The number of chromosomes in an egg."],0,"Exposure of suitable actin binding sites."],["Can muscle develop tension without substantial shortening?",["Yes, during an isometric action.","No, tension is possible only if all bones shorten.","No, ATP prevents all static force.","Only if myosin is absent."],0,"Yes, during an isometric action."]],"images":["y11-muscle-fibre","y11-sarcomere","y11-sliding-filaments"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]],"tables":[{"title":"Sarcomere shortening: what changes?","headers":["Feature","During shortening","Reason"],"rows":[["Actin filament length","Approximately unchanged","Thin filaments slide rather than shrink"],["Myosin filament length / A band","Approximately unchanged","Thick-filament length retained"],["Distance between Z lines","Decreases","Sarcomere ends approach"],["I band / H zone","Generally decreases","Overlap changes as filaments slide"]]}]},{"id":"antagonists","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"19 · Antagonistic muscles, posture and movement","goal":"Explain force transmission, antagonistic actions and coordinated stability.","prior":"Recall cell structure, membrane transport and the meaning of a structure–function relationship.","sections":[["Skeletal muscle pulls through tendons","A contracting skeletal muscle generates tension transmitted through its connective tissues and tendons to attachment points. It generally pulls rather than actively pushing a bone away. Movement depends on how that pull acts around a joint and on opposing loads. Bone and muscle therefore form a mechanical system, not separate lists of body parts. The same muscle can contribute differently depending on joint position, load and which attachment is relatively stabilised."],["An elbow example","In a simplified elbow model, biceps brachii contributes to flexion and triceps brachii contributes to extension. Their actions oppose one another around the joint, so they are described as antagonists for this movement. When flexion is produced, flexor activity creates a net flexion moment; when extension is produced, extensor activity can create the opposing moment. Other muscles also contribute, so a two-muscle diagram is a useful teaching model rather than a complete account of every elbow action."],["Agonist and antagonist are task-dependent","The agonist is a principal contributor to a specified movement, while an antagonist can oppose or control it. These labels depend on the movement being discussed, rather than being permanent moral categories of helpful and harmful muscles. An antagonist need not be completely inactive: controlled activation can brake motion or stabilise a joint. When a question asks about a particular action, name the movement first, then explain the relevant muscle roles."],["Co-contraction and stability","Simultaneous activity of muscles with opposing actions can increase joint stiffness and stability. This co-contraction helps explain posture or controlled movement in which simply describing one muscle as fully relaxed is inadequate. It can also increase metabolic demand without producing a large visible displacement. A static hold involves balancing moments and controlling alignment, not necessarily complete muscular rest. The relative activation pattern varies with task and load."],["Concentric, eccentric and isometric action","During a concentric action, an active muscle shortens; during an eccentric action, it develops tension while lengthening under the load; during an isometric action, it develops tension with little overall length change. Lowering a load can therefore involve an active muscle controlling movement while lengthening rather than simply switching off. These terms describe length behaviour, not whether ATP is needed. Relate each to an explicit movement and avoid saying that only shortening muscles can produce force."],["Posture and mechanical leverage","Posture depends on coordinated muscle activity, skeletal alignment, sensory feedback and support forces. Muscle attachment distance from a joint affects its turning effect, so muscle force is not generally equal to the external load force. A diagram should show joint axis, force direction and relevant distances if calculations are required. The course requires biological structure–function reasoning; detailed engineering calculations are extension unless supplied in the task."],["Fatigue and safe interpretation","Reduced performance during repeated activity can reflect interacting metabolic, neural and mechanical influences. It should not be attributed solely to a single persistent store of “lactic acid”. Compare equivalent tasks and control relevant conditions before making causal claims. Students can analyse safe supplied movement data and models without testing extreme loads or intentionally provoking injury. Recovery and training involve more than simply making each myosin molecule longer."],["Follow force from a fibre to a joint movement","Contractile force is transmitted through muscle connective tissues to tendons and their skeletal attachments. The line of pull relative to a joint determines the turning effect. During elbow flexion, flexor activity can increase while extensor activity is adjusted; during controlled lowering, an active muscle can lengthen while resisting the load. Isometric activity develops tension with little overall length change, helping posture and stabilisation. Naming a muscle as the antagonist in one task does not assign it a permanent inactive role in every movement."],["Interpret posture and fatigue as system problems","Posture requires ongoing coordination of muscle activity around multiple joints and an appropriate base of support. Stabilising muscles constrain unwanted movement while other muscles create the intended action. Sustained activity depends on ATP supply, ion handling, neural drive and other factors; fatigue should not be attributed to one universal accumulation of lactic acid. Changes in breathing and circulation during movement support oxygen delivery and carbon dioxide removal. Explain a supplied observation through a plausible mechanism and acknowledge when the evidence cannot distinguish between several possible limits."]],"mis":"A muscle pulls when producing tension. Opposing movement requires another force or muscle action, rather than the same muscle pushing a bone back.","worked":["Explain flexion and extension using a simplified biceps–triceps model.","Biceps activity contributes to a flexion moment through tendon attachments around the elbow. Triceps activity contributes to an opposing extension moment. They form an antagonistic arrangement for these actions, with coordinated activation and other muscle contributions affecting actual movement."],"workedMore":[["Explain why lowering a held load does not necessarily mean the supporting muscle has become inactive.","An active muscle can lengthen while maintaining tension during eccentric action. Its force can control the rate and direction of the load’s movement. Activity and ATP demand therefore cannot be inferred solely from whether muscle length increases or decreases."],["Explain how muscles can support a static position.","Muscles can generate tension approximately isometrically. Their turning effects can balance external loads while maintaining joint alignment. Co-contraction and feedback can support stability, with continuing energy requirements despite little visible movement."]],"practice":[["Evaluate: “The antagonist must always relax completely whenever the agonist is active.”","Agonist and antagonist labels depend on the specified movement. Opposing muscles can both be active during controlled motion or stabilisation. The simple alternating-pair model is useful but does not describe every activation pattern.",6],["Explain why removing tendon force transmission can impair movement even if muscle fibres still contract.","Fibres generate tension that must be transmitted through connective tissue and tendon. Interrupted transmission reduces effective pull on the skeletal attachment. Contraction within the tissue does not by itself guarantee the required joint moment.",6],["Why is “all fatigue is caused by retained lactate” an inadequate conclusion?","Fatigue reflects interacting metabolic, neural and mechanical factors. Lactate can be transported and used metabolically rather than simply remaining as an inert poison. A causal explanation requires evidence appropriate to the particular task and physiological conditions.",6]],"quiz":["How does a skeletal muscle normally act on an attachment?",["By generating tension and pulling through connective tissues.","By actively pushing the tendon away with no tension.","By converting bone to air.","By directly pumping blood through a joint."],0,"By generating tension and pulling through connective tissues."],"quizzes":[["Which muscle contributes to elbow extension in a simplified model?",["Triceps brachii.","Only biceps brachii as an extensor.","The diaphragm alone.","A villus smooth muscle."],0,"Triceps brachii."],["Must an antagonist always be completely inactive?",["No; it can help control movement or stabilise a joint.","Yes, in every movement and posture.","Yes, because it contains no ATP.","Only if the joint has cartilage."],0,"No; it can help control movement or stabilise a joint."],["What is eccentric muscle action?",["Active tension while the muscle lengthens.","Complete inactivity while every fibre shortens.","Creation of a new allele.","Loss of all myosin."],0,"Active tension while the muscle lengthens."],["What can co-contraction support?",["Joint stability and controlled posture.","Only larger tidal volume.","Automatic elimination of all injury risk.","Removal of all energy demand."],0,"Joint stability and controlled posture."]],"images":["y11-muscle-fibre","y11-synovial-joint"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"lifestyle","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"20 · Lifestyle, nutrition and linked body systems","goal":"Explain short-term and long-term effects of activity, diet and drug exposure through named biological mechanisms.","prior":"Connect the relevant cell, tissue and organ structures with the process being investigated.","sections":[["Link a lifestyle factor with an actual process","A causal explanation identifies exposure, affected structure or process and the resulting functional change. During physical activity, skeletal muscles increase ATP demand, so ventilation and circulation help supply oxygen and substrates and remove carbon dioxide. With repeated suitable training, several cardiovascular and muscular adaptations can support performance. Sedentary behaviour changes patterns of demand and can contribute to long-term health risks alongside diet and other factors. Distinguish an immediate physiological response, a longer-term adaptation and an association that may have several causes."],["Energy balance and nutrient quality are different questions","Energy intake can exceed, match or fall below expenditure, affecting changes in stored energy over time. Nutrient quality concerns the supply of essential building blocks, vitamins, minerals, fibre and other dietary components. An energy-rich diet can still be inadequate in particular nutrients. Conversely, a food containing an essential nutrient is not unlimited evidence of a balanced whole diet. Explain how digestion and absorption supply cells before discussing cellular use. Percentages on a food label need a stated mass or serving denominator for a fair comparison."],["Smoking illustrates several separate mechanisms","Inhaled smoke exposes airway and exchange tissues to a mixture of substances. Damage to ciliated surfaces can impair mucus clearance; longer-term damage to alveolar structures can reduce effective exchange area. Carbon monoxide can interfere with oxygen transport through haemoglobin binding. Nicotine has physiological effects through receptor pathways and contributes to dependence. These are different mechanisms, so do not attribute every consequence to one substance or assume that one laboratory measure captures total harm. Evaluate specific evidence, duration and exposure context."],["Drug effects depend on targets, dose and context","A drug interacts with biological targets such as receptors, enzymes or transporters. The same broad category can contain substances with different effects and risks. A bronchodilator can improve airflow by altering airway smooth-muscle activity, whereas an antibiotic acts on an appropriate microbial target. Misuse or inappropriate exposure can compromise functioning rather than improve it. The course examines mechanisms and evidence; a familiar drug name alone does not establish suitability, effectiveness or safety in an individual case."],["Evaluate a lifestyle study before inferring causation","An observational comparison may find a relationship between activity and a health outcome while age, pre-existing health, income or other factors also differ. A plausible mechanism strengthens interpretation but does not remove those design limitations. Compare equivalent groups where possible, inspect sample size and outcome definitions, and distinguish relative from absolute differences. A sample in which 8 of 40 participants show an outcome has an observed proportion of 20%; it does not prove every population has the same probability."],["Build an integrated response from an unfamiliar case","For a person undertaking an unfamiliar physical task, trace muscle ATP demand to substrate and oxygen delivery, carbon dioxide removal and force production. Then explain how a specified impairment could limit one link: reduced haemoglobin affects transport, damaged alveoli affect exchange and restricted joints affect movement. These are interacting systems rather than isolated chapter names. A strong response uses the supplied evidence and identifies which conclusion remains uncertain, instead of diagnosing every impairment from a single symptom."]],"worked":["A teaching study records a mean pulse of 72 beats min⁻¹ and stroke volume of 70 mL at rest. Calculate cardiac output.","Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume = 72 × 70 = 5040 mL min⁻¹, or 5.04 L min⁻¹. This describes blood pumped per minute under the stated conditions, not the amount of oxygen carried, which also depends on blood composition."],"practice":[["Explain why more rapid breathing alone cannot fully compensate for a large reduction in functional haemoglobin.","Ventilation supplies alveolar air, but blood oxygen transport depends strongly on functional haemoglobin. Improving one stage does not automatically restore the capacity of the next stage.",3],["A study compares activity groups of very different ages. Explain a limitation and a suitable improvement.","Age-related differences may confound the relationship between activity and the outcome. Use appropriately comparable groups or account for age in a suitable design and analysis; a larger biased sample alone does not remove the confound.",4],["Distinguish the effects of impaired cilia and reduced alveolar area.","Impaired cilia reduce clearance of mucus and trapped material. Reduced alveolar area limits the available gas-exchange surface. They affect different steps of respiratory function.",4]],"quiz":["What most improves a causal explanation of lifestyle effects?",["Name the exposure, mechanism and supported outcome","List several diseases without mechanisms","Assume correlation proves causation","Use only a personal anecdote"],0,"Connect a defined exposure with biological processes and evidence."],"images":["y11-respiratory-tract","y11-alveoli","y11-circulation"],"mis":"A lifestyle association is not automatically proof of a single cause. Physiological mechanism and study design both matter.","references":[]},{"id":"interventions","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"21 · Diagnosis, medicines, physical therapy and organ replacement","goal":"Compare ways of treating organ dysfunction by identifying the process each intervention changes.","prior":"Connect the relevant cell, tissue and organ structures with the process being investigated.","sections":[["Early detection changes available decisions","Improved imaging, laboratory measurements and physiological tests can reveal dysfunction earlier or more precisely. Earlier detection may permit an intervention before extensive loss of function, but benefit depends on test quality, disease behaviour and whether effective treatment exists. A more sensitive test can also find changes whose significance is uncertain. Separate the measured signal, the interpretation and the clinical decision. Finding an abnormality is not identical to demonstrating that a particular treatment improves a meaningful outcome."],["Medicines modify particular biological processes","A medicine may alter receptor activation, enzyme activity, transport or the activity of a pathogen. A bronchodilator can reduce an airway smooth-muscle component of obstruction; it does not rebuild every damaged alveolus. A relevant antimicrobial can reduce a susceptible infection without automatically repairing all tissue damage. Explain the target and downstream effect, then identify a limitation. Improvements in drug design and delivery can change benefits and harms, so neither all medicines nor all organ disorders share one treatment pathway."],["Physical therapy acts through function and adaptation","Appropriately designed physical therapy can develop strength, range, coordination or functional capacity, with aims determined by the impairment. Stronger supporting muscles can improve control around a joint, while progressive movement can support recovery of useful function. Therapy cannot be assumed to regrow every lost tissue or reverse every disease mechanism. Its effects are evaluated using relevant outcomes such as movement, strength and daily function, rather than only whether an exercise was performed. Biological loading and recovery processes connect the intervention to its intended benefit."],["Removal can eliminate a problem but sacrifices tissue","Removing a severely affected structure can reduce a source of disease or dysfunction, but the consequence depends on the remaining tissue and any lost function. A removed organ does not continue performing its original role automatically. Surgical decisions therefore balance disease control, procedural harms, functional loss and alternative approaches. In an educational case, explain which function would need compensation and which outcome is being targeted. Avoid inferring that a procedure successful for one local problem is suitable for every disorder with a similar symptom."],["Replacement and external support solve different problems","Joint replacement substitutes an artificial articulating arrangement for damaged surfaces and can improve selected movement-related outcomes. Organ transplantation replaces biological function but introduces issues including compatibility, immune rejection, long-term management and donor availability. Dialysis provides external removal and exchange functions in kidney failure but does not reproduce every endocrine and regulatory function of a healthy kidney. These examples compare tissue replacement, organ replacement and external support through the actual functions restored and those that remain limited."],["Evaluate treatment advances using comparable evidence","Compare the same condition, outcome and time interval between appropriate groups. Benefits may involve survival, symptoms or function; harms may include procedural risk, adverse effects and treatment burden. Cost, access, consent and patient priorities affect real-world decisions. A mechanistic explanation supports plausibility, while controlled evidence tests whether the benefit occurs. Improvements over time may reflect better diagnosis, selection, technique and follow-up together, so do not attribute every historical change to one newly named technology."]],"worked":["Explain why dialysis is not identical to a complete healthy kidney.","Dialysis can remove selected wastes and alter fluid or solute balance by exchange with a controlled external fluid. A healthy kidney also performs ongoing selective nephron processing and other functions; dialysis does not automatically reproduce every renal endocrine or regulatory role."],"practice":[["Compare physical therapy and joint replacement through their immediate biological aims.","Physical therapy develops or maintains movement, muscular support and coordination. Replacement substitutes an articulating arrangement for damaged joint structures. Both may improve function, but they act through different mechanisms and have different limits.",4],["Why is the discovery of an earlier detectable marker insufficient to prove improved survival?","The marker must identify relevant disease accurately, and earlier intervention must improve outcomes. Earlier detection alone can increase time measured since diagnosis without necessarily delaying death; comparable outcome evidence is required.",4],["A drug improves airway diameter but not damaged gas-exchange area. Predict a remaining limitation.","Airflow may improve, while reduced exchange area can continue limiting oxygen uptake and carbon dioxide transfer. Treatment of one component does not imply complete restoration of the system.",3]],"quiz":["Which comparison correctly distinguishes interventions?",["Dialysis supports selected exchange functions; transplantation supplies a biological organ","Dialysis creates a new kidney","All drugs replace organs","Physical therapy is the same as surgical removal"],0,"Compare the actual function supported or replaced."],"images":["nephron","y11-synovial-joint","y11-alveoli"],"mis":"A treatment can improve one function without restoring every component of a healthy organ.","references":[]},{"id":"unit1-synthesis","year":"11","unit":"1","title":"22 · Unit 1 synthesis: systems supporting metabolism","goal":"Integrate several mechanisms in a structured response using original supplied data and explicit reasoning.","prior":"Connect the relevant cell, tissue and organ structures with the process being investigated.","sections":[["Start with the process asked for","Read the command term and define the system or relationship being tested. A description names relevant events; an explanation connects causes and consequences; a comparison pairs corresponding features; an evaluation judges evidence and limitations. Begin with the supplied case rather than writing everything remembered about the topic. Select relevant structures, establish the direction of movement or information and connect the observation with a biological mechanism. Use labelled diagrams or calculations when they add explanatory information rather than decoration."],["Connect organisational levels","A complete response can move from molecules to cells, tissues and organs. Reduced alveolar area affects diffusion; altered haemoglobin affects transport; impaired mitochondrial processes affect ATP supply; muscle performance depends on the integrated pathway. These are related levels, not interchangeable labels. Explain the missing link when a question moves from a molecular observation to an organ-level or offspring-level result."],["Use quantities with the correct denominator","State the measured variable, unit and reference group. Cardiac output multiplies rate by volume per beat; a percentage change compares a difference with a stated starting value; an exchange gradient describes a directional difference. Show working and distinguish a mathematical prediction from a guarantee for one observed sample."],["Evaluate alternative explanations","A proposed mechanism can be plausible while the supplied evidence remains incomplete. Identify a specific alternative or procedural limitation, explain its effect and suggest a relevant improvement. For an exercise comparison, baseline fitness, age and measurement procedure can influence results. Avoid the empty phrase more research is needed unless the proposed work resolves a stated uncertainty."],["Construct the final scientific argument","Answer directly, organise relevant evidence and explain why it supports the conclusion. Use correct scientific names, figure labels, units and relationship arrows. Distinguish original observations from synthetic teaching examples and secondary sources. A suggested marking response identifies useful scientific ideas; identical phrasing is not required. After comparing, rewrite one weak link from memory and test the explanation on a changed condition. This retrieval step checks understanding beyond recognition of the model answer."]],"worked":["A synthetic result changes cardiac output from 5.0 to 10.0 L min⁻¹. Calculate percentage increase and explain a limit.","Increase = 5.0 L min⁻¹; percentage increase = 5.0/5.0 × 100 = 100%. The result describes volume flow, not by itself oxygen consumption or training adaptation."],"practice":[["Explain how reduced small-intestinal absorption could affect muscle function despite normal breathing.","Less absorbable substrate may reach blood and tissues. Oxygen delivery alone does not provide all substrates required for metabolism and ATP-supported muscle activity.",4],["Compare filtration in a nephron with diffusion across an alveolar surface.","Glomerular filtration is pressure-driven bulk movement across a selective barrier into tubular fluid. Alveolar gas exchange is diffusion down gas-specific gradients across a thin exchange surface. Name compartments and driving conditions.",4],["Explain why low haemoglobin and reduced alveolar area are different limitations.","One reduces effective oxygen-carrying capacity in blood; the other reduces available exchange surface. They affect different steps, although both may compromise oxygen supply to tissues.",4]],"quiz":["Which answer best demonstrates scientific explanation?",["A causal chain supported by the supplied evidence","An unconnected list of key words","A copied general essay ignoring the case","A conclusion with no stated denominator"],0,"Connect the mechanism with the actual evidence and question."],"images":["y11-alveoli","y11-heart-anatomy","nephron"],"mis":"A worked answer is a model of reasoning, not a substitute for applying the idea to a changed case.","references":[]},{"id":"dna","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"01 · DNA structure and replication","goal":"Explain DNA organisation, complementary pairing and semiconservative copying.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["Genes, alleles and genetic information","DNA carries inherited information through its nucleotide sequence. A gene is a DNA sequence contributing to a functional product, often a protein or a functional RNA. An allele is a variant of a gene at a particular locus. Chromosomes contain many genes and other DNA sequences, so gene and chromosome are not interchangeable. The genome is the full genetic material of an organism in the relevant context. Human nuclear chromosomes associate DNA with proteins, while mitochondria also contain DNA. A mitochondrial genome is distinct from the much larger nuclear genome. Accurate terminology matters when explaining heredity: an offspring receives particular alleles, not a different basic gene for every version of a trait."],["Nucleotides, pairing and antiparallel strands","A DNA nucleotide contains a phosphate group, deoxyribose sugar and one nitrogenous base: adenine, thymine, cytosine or guanine. Covalent bonds form each sugar–phosphate backbone, while hydrogen bonds connect complementary bases between strands. Adenine pairs with thymine and cytosine with guanine. The strands run in opposite orientations, described as antiparallel, and form a double helix. Complementary pairing allows one strand to provide information for rebuilding the other. Do not confuse the weaker hydrogen bonds between bases with the covalent backbone bonds. A simplified ladder diagram is useful for showing pairing but omits the three-dimensional helix and many molecular interactions. When writing sequences, include orientation when the question supplies or requires it."],["Semiconservative DNA replication","Before a cell divides, DNA must be copied so inherited information can be transferred. The two strands separate, and each acts as a template for synthesis of a complementary strand. DNA polymerase catalyses addition of appropriate nucleotides and extends a new strand in its permitted direction. Each completed DNA double helix contains one original strand and one newly synthesised strand, so replication is semiconservative. Complementary pairing supports accurate copying, while proofreading and repair reduce errors without eliminating them completely. The process occurs before chromosome separation in mitosis or meiosis, not as a new full replication between meiotic divisions. Explain how pairing and strand separation make copying possible rather than simply saying DNA doubles because the cell needs more."],["Replication direction and model limits","New DNA is synthesised in the 5′ to 3′ direction. Because template strands are antiparallel, synthesis at a replication fork proceeds differently on the two templates: one new strand can be extended more continuously, while the other is assembled from segments that are later joined. This extension explains the direction constraint without requiring every molecular component to be memorised for the syllabus. A drawing should distinguish old and new strands and show the consequence of complementarity. Replication is a regulated enzyme-mediated process, not random bases permanently sticking wherever they touch. A simplified model can represent the central mechanism while omitting multiple replication origins, detailed proofreading and chromatin organisation. Identify what is included and what remains beyond the diagram's purpose."],["Nuclear and mitochondrial DNA","Most human DNA is located in the nucleus, associated with histone proteins and organised into chromosomes. Mitochondria also contain a small genome, commonly represented as circular DNA without the same histone-based nuclear packaging. Nuclear genes contribute to many mitochondrial proteins, so a mitochondrial genome does not make the organelle genetically independent. A chromosome contains many genes and regulatory sequences; a gene is not an entire chromosome. Distinguish a DNA molecule, a gene region and a variant allele when interpreting diagrams."],["The backbone and information","Each DNA nucleotide contains a phosphate group, deoxyribose sugar and a nitrogenous base. Adjacent nucleotides form a sugar–phosphate backbone through covalent bonds, while complementary bases across the two strands associate through hydrogen bonding. Adenine pairs with thymine and guanine with cytosine. Base order stores genetic information; the backbone is not a four-letter protein code. The strands run in opposite directions, so any supplied sequence question must identify orientation before a complementary strand is written."],["Replication serves division","Before normal cell division, DNA is copied during the synthesis phase of the cell cycle. Each original strand provides a template for complementary nucleotides, producing two double-stranded molecules containing one original and one newly synthesised strand. This is semiconservative replication, not a mixing of random halves. DNA polymerases and associated enzymes support copying and checking. Errors can still occur, making mutation possible; a normal copied sequence is not a newly invented allele merely because the molecule is physically new."],["Scale and limitations of DNA models","A flat ladder diagram shows complementary pairing but not the full three-dimensional helix, chromatin packaging or enzyme machinery. A chromosome drawing often shows condensed duplicated chromosomes, not the appearance of DNA throughout interphase. Use the model to explain the particular relationship represented, then state its limitations. If a diagram contains four bases, it does not mean the human genome has only four nucleotides: these are four nucleotide types repeated in enormous sequences."]],"mis":"Replication produces DNA from DNA; transcription produces RNA from a DNA template. They are distinct processes.","worked":["Write the complementary strand aligned beneath 5′-ATGCCAT-3′ and explain the orientation.","Complementary bases give 3′-TACGGTA-5′ beneath the supplied strand. A pairs with T and G with C. The strands are antiparallel; writing both left-to-right as 5′ to 3′ without reversing order misrepresents their alignment."],"workedMore":[["Describe the DNA composition of each molecule after one normal replication of a labelled original double helix.","The two original strands separate and each acts as a template. Complementary new nucleotides are assembled into a new strand for each original strand. Each product has one original labelled strand and one new strand, rather than one entirely old and one entirely new helix."],["A double-stranded DNA sample contains 28% adenine. Calculate the percentages of the other bases under the standard pairing model.","Thymine equals adenine, so T = 28%. A + T = 56%, leaving G + C = 44%. G = C = 22%; this inference assumes an ordinary complete double-stranded sample."]],"practice":[["Compare nuclear and mitochondrial DNA organisation in a human cell.","Nuclear DNA is organised into chromosomes associated with histone proteins. Mitochondria contain a small genome commonly represented as circular DNA without the same nuclear histone packaging. Both store genetic information, while many mitochondrial proteins are encoded by nuclear genes.",6],["Explain two useful features and one limitation of a DNA ladder diagram.","It can show two nucleotide strands and complementary A–T/G–C pairing. It can distinguish backbone from base-pair connections and represent opposite strand directions. A flat ladder does not accurately display the three-dimensional helix or all packaging and replication machinery.",6],["Correct: “Each chromosome is one allele that makes one protein.”","A chromosome is a long DNA molecule with many gene and regulatory regions. An allele is a variant at a particular locus, not the whole chromosome. Genes can encode functional products, and gene-to-protein relationships are more complex than one chromosome making one protein.",6]],"quiz":["What is semiconservative replication?",["Each product has one old and one new strand","Both products contain only old strands","One product is entirely old and the other entirely new","Every base is converted to RNA"],0,"Original strands each template new complementary strands, conserving one in each daughter double helix."],"quizzes":[["Where is most human DNA found?",["In nuclear chromosomes associated with histones.","Only in erythrocyte mitochondria.","Only dissolved in plasma.","In the lipid bilayer as amino acids."],0,"In nuclear chromosomes associated with histones."],["Which base pairs with guanine in DNA?",["Cytosine.","Uracil.","Adenine.","Thymine."],0,"Cytosine."],["What distinguishes an allele from a gene?",["An allele is a sequence variant at a gene locus.","An allele is always a whole chromosome.","A gene contains no DNA.","An allele is necessarily dominant."],0,"An allele is a sequence variant at a gene locus."],["What bonds form the DNA backbone?",["Covalent bonds connecting sugar and phosphate groups.","Only hydrogen bonds between complementary bases.","Peptide bonds between sugars.","Ionic bonds between whole cells."],0,"Covalent bonds connecting sugar and phosphate groups."]],"images":["dna","replication"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"protein-synthesis","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"02 · Transcription, translation and proteins","goal":"Use DNA–RNA sequence conventions to explain human protein synthesis.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["DNA, RNA and the triplet code","RNA usually contains a single strand with ribose sugar and uracil in place of DNA's thymine. Messenger RNA carries a sequence that can be translated at ribosomes. The genetic code is read in base triplets, with an mRNA triplet called a codon. Most codons specify an amino acid, while some signal termination. Several codons can specify the same amino acid, making the code redundant or degenerate. This does not mean one codon specifies any amino acid arbitrarily. Use an mRNA codon table unless the question states another convention, and convert a DNA sequence appropriately first. A reading-frame shift changes how subsequent triplets are grouped and can therefore alter more than one amino acid."],["Transcription in eukaryotic cells","Transcription synthesises RNA using a DNA template. In eukaryotic cells, transcription of nuclear genes occurs in the nucleus. RNA polymerase uses complementary base pairing, with RNA uracil pairing to template adenine. The DNA coding strand has the same base sequence as the corresponding RNA segment except for thymine in place of uracil, when both are written in the same direction. Processing can modify the initial RNA transcript before export, including removal of relevant intervening sequences in many eukaryotic genes. Coding and non-coding DNA should not be confused with coding versus template strands: these describe different distinctions. Explain which strand provides the template, what molecule is produced and where the process occurs in the stated organism."],["Translation at ribosomes","Translation uses the mRNA codon sequence to assemble an amino acid chain at a ribosome. Transfer RNA molecules carry amino acids and contain anticodons that pair with suitable codons. The ribosome coordinates recognition and peptide-bond formation, advancing through the message until a stop signal is reached. The resulting polypeptide may need folding and modification before it functions as a protein. The DNA does not normally leave the nucleus to be directly read by a cytoplasmic ribosome in a eukaryotic cell. Distinguish transcription from translation by template, product and location. A diagram that includes mRNA, tRNA, amino acids and ribosome should label each role rather than showing protein appearing merely because RNA is present."],["A worked sequence convention","Suppose the DNA template is written 3′–TAC GGA CTT–5′. The complementary mRNA is 5′–AUG CCU GAA–3′, using U rather than T. With a standard mRNA codon table, AUG codes for methionine, CCU for proline and GAA for glutamate. The corresponding DNA coding strand is 5′–ATG CCT GAA–3′. This example is a short teaching sequence, not a complete gene or functional protein. If the question gives a strand in the opposite direction, orientation must be considered before using the codon table. Show the conversion clearly to reduce avoidable errors. A tRNA anticodon is complementary to the codon and should not simply be copied as the same triplet."],["Proteins connect genotype and cellular function","Proteins contribute to structure, transport, signalling and catalysis. A DNA sequence can influence a protein's amino acid sequence, which can influence folding and activity. Changes in a transporter or enzyme can then affect cellular processes and contribute to an observable phenotype. The connection is not always one gene producing one visible trait: many traits depend on multiple genes and environmental influences, and some DNA sequences produce functional RNA rather than protein. Non-coding sequences can have regulatory or structural roles, so they should not all be called useless. A strong explanation traces the relevant steps from genetic information through a functional product to the particular trait, while acknowledging what evidence would be needed to establish the effect of a variant."],["From nucleus to cytoplasmic ribosomes","In human nucleated cells, transcription uses a DNA template to produce RNA. For a protein-coding gene, processing produces mature mRNA that can leave the nucleus through nuclear pores. Ribosomes translate the mRNA sequence into an amino-acid sequence with help from tRNA. DNA normally remains in the nucleus during this process; the whole chromosome does not travel to a ribosome. Some translation occurs on ribosomes associated with rough ER, supporting production of many membrane and secreted proteins."],["Template, coding strand and codons","An mRNA sequence is complementary and antiparallel to its DNA template, and matches the coding strand in the same orientation except that RNA uses U instead of T. A codon is a three-base mRNA unit read in the 5′ to 3′ direction. A tRNA anticodon pairs with the corresponding codon, while its attached amino acid is incorporated into the growing chain. Stop codons signal termination rather than specifying an additional amino acid. Always use the supplied codon table and stated strand identity."],["Protein function requires more than a sequence","A polypeptide folds into a shape influenced by its amino-acid sequence and cellular conditions; some proteins are modified or assembled with other subunits. Shape supports functions such as enzyme catalysis, transport or signalling. A sequence change may alter function, but not every substitution changes the amino acid, and not every amino-acid change destroys a protein. Distinguish transcription of information, translation into a chain and subsequent folding or processing when explaining a biological consequence."]],"mis":"Codons are read on mRNA; anticodons are on tRNA. The amino acid is attached to tRNA, not to an mRNA codon.","worked":["A DNA template segment is 3′-TAC GGA CTT-5′. Write mRNA and use the supplied information AUG = methionine, CCU = proline and GAA = glutamate to name the encoded amino acids.","mRNA is 5′-AUG CCU GAA-3′ through complementary pairing and RNA U. Reading mRNA codons gives methionine–proline–glutamate. The supplied strand is the template; copying its letters directly would give the wrong mRNA."],"workedMore":[["Explain how a nuclear gene contributes to a protein secreted from a human cell.","Transcription and RNA processing produce mRNA that leaves the nucleus. Ribosomes associated with rough ER translate mRNA using tRNA-carried amino acids; folding and processing begin. Golgi sorting and vesicle transport lead to exocytosis of the secreted product."],["Two different mRNA codons specify the same amino acid. Explain why a corresponding DNA substitution may not alter the protein sequence.","The code is redundant: more than one codon can specify an amino acid. A substitution can therefore change a codon without changing the incorporated amino acid. This is a synonymous coding change, although sequence changes can sometimes affect other expression processes."]],"practice":[["Compare transcription and translation by location, template and product in a typical nucleated human cell.","Transcription occurs in the nucleus using DNA as a template and producing RNA. Translation occurs at ribosomes using mRNA codons to assemble a polypeptide with tRNA assistance. DNA does not need to leave the nucleus; RNA connects stored information to protein synthesis.",6],["An mRNA codon is 5′-GCU-3′. Write its aligned tRNA anticodon and identify the role of tRNA.","The aligned anticodon is 3′-CGA-5′. Complementary antiparallel pairing connects the tRNA with the codon. The tRNA delivers the corresponding amino acid; the codon table is normally read using mRNA, not by looking up the anticodon directly.",6],["Explain a possible causal chain from an amino-acid substitution to altered enzyme activity, while stating a limit.","A coding change can alter an amino acid in the polypeptide. Changed side-chain interactions may affect folding or the active-site properties, changing activity. The effect depends on the position and substitution; not every sequence change abolishes function.",6]],"quiz":["For template 3′-TAC-5′, what is mRNA?",["5′-AUG-3′","5′-TAC-3′","3′-ATG-5′","5′-UAC-3′"],0,"Complementary antiparallel RNA pairs A with U and C with G; template TAC gives AUG."],"quizzes":[["What does code redundancy mean?",["Several codons can encode one amino acid","Each codon must encode four amino acids","Proteins contain no information","All DNA changes are harmful"],0,"Synonymous codons can leave the amino-acid sequence unchanged, though other effects can still depend on context."],["Which molecule brings amino acids to translation machinery?",["tRNA","A chromosome wall","Nitrate","Phospholipid"],0,"Transfer RNA matches anticodon/codon information and delivers the corresponding amino acid to a ribosome."],["What matches mRNA except for T being replaced by U?",["The coding DNA strand written in the same direction.","The template strand without complementary pairing.","The amino-acid chain.","The phospholipid tail."],0,"The coding DNA strand written in the same direction."],["What occurs at a stop codon?",["Translation terminates rather than adding a stop amino acid.","DNA replication must begin.","The ribosome manufactures a new chromosome.","A nucleotide becomes a lipid."],0,"Translation terminates rather than adding a stop amino acid."]],"images":["protein"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"epigenetics","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"03 · Gene expression and epigenetics","goal":"Explain cell-specific expression and environmental influences without confusing epigenetic regulation with DNA mutation.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["Genes, alleles and genetic information","DNA carries inherited information through its nucleotide sequence. A gene is a DNA sequence contributing to a functional product, often a protein or a functional RNA. An allele is a variant of a gene at a particular locus. Chromosomes contain many genes and other DNA sequences, so gene and chromosome are not interchangeable. The genome is the full genetic material of an organism in the relevant context. Human nuclear chromosomes associate DNA with proteins, while mitochondria also contain DNA. A mitochondrial genome is distinct from the much larger nuclear genome. Accurate terminology matters when explaining heredity: an offspring receives particular alleles, not a different basic gene for every version of a trait."],["Proteins connect genotype and cellular function","Proteins contribute to structure, transport, signalling and catalysis. A DNA sequence can influence a protein's amino acid sequence, which can influence folding and activity. Changes in a transporter or enzyme can then affect cellular processes and contribute to an observable phenotype. The connection is not always one gene producing one visible trait: many traits depend on multiple genes and environmental influences, and some DNA sequences produce functional RNA rather than protein. Non-coding sequences can have regulatory or structural roles, so they should not all be called useless. A strong explanation traces the relevant steps from genetic information through a functional product to the particular trait, while acknowledging what evidence would be needed to establish the effect of a variant."],["Gene expression, environment and phenotype","Phenotype results from interactions between genetic information and the environment. Cells can express different genes or different amounts of their products, contributing to specialisation and responses to conditions. Nutrition, temperature or light can influence traits without necessarily changing the inherited DNA sequence. The Year 11 syllabus includes epigenetics: phenotypic expression depends on control of transcription and translation, products of other genes and the environment. Molecular examples below illustrate mechanisms without changing the underlying DNA sequence. Compare genotypic change with an environmental effect on expression or function. An environmental effect can alter the performance of an enzyme or development of a structure, and the same genotype can produce different outcomes under different conditions. Avoid interpreting every visible difference as a new allele or assuming genes determine traits independently of context."],["The same genome, different expression","A muscle cell and a neuron generally contain essentially the same inherited nuclear genome, yet differ in structure and function because they express different combinations and amounts of genes. Regulatory signals influence transcription and other expression steps. Expressing a gene means using its information to produce a functional product; it does not mean creating that gene for the first time. Specialised cells retain many unused genes, while mature erythrocytes are an important exception because they lack a nucleus."],["Chromatin access","DNA wraps around histone proteins and forms chromatin. Changes in packaging and associated regulatory proteins influence which DNA regions can be accessed by transcription machinery. Relatively open chromatin often permits transcription more readily than tightly packed regions, but expression also depends on specific regulators and cellular context. A model showing “open equals on” is a helpful simplification rather than a universal switch that works independently of every other factor."],["DNA methylation","DNA methylation adds chemical groups to particular DNA bases without replacing the underlying sequence of bases. Methylation at some regulatory regions is associated with reduced transcription, although its effects depend on location and context. It is therefore an epigenetic regulatory change rather than the same thing as a base substitution. Avoid claiming that every methyl group always silences every gene. A change in expression can occur while the DNA base sequence remains unchanged."],["Histone modifications","Chemical modifications to histone proteins can affect chromatin organisation and recruitment of regulatory proteins. Histone acetylation is often associated with more accessible chromatin. Histone methylation can be associated with activation or repression depending on the modified position and context. These tags do not ordinarily work by rewriting the histone amino-acid sequence. Distinguish a chemical modification of an existing protein from a mutation of the gene that encodes that protein."],["Environment and phenotype","Nutrition, hormones and other environmental influences can affect expression and measured phenotype through several mechanisms, some involving epigenetic regulation. Such influences do not mean that the organism intentionally rewrites genes to meet a need. Human evidence for transmission of particular acquired epigenetic effects across multiple generations requires careful interpretation, because shared environment, genetic variation and direct exposure can also explain patterns. Do not conclude that every acquired trait will be inherited."],["Evaluating an expression study","A study comparing methylation and expression must identify the tissue, developmental stage, measured DNA region and relevant environmental variables. A correlation between increased methylation and decreased expression does not by itself show that methylation caused the change. Stronger evidence may include controlled interventions, replication and consistent mechanistic results. Interpret group averages with attention to spread and confounding; two similar genomes do not ensure identical diets, cell compositions or life histories."],["Biotechnology changes the evidence available","DNA sequencing can identify differences in inherited or acquired base sequences, while RNA-based measurements can compare expression of particular genes in different samples. Two cell types can share essentially the same inherited genome yet show different RNA and protein patterns, supporting a regulatory explanation of specialisation rather than the idea that each tissue deletes unused genes. Methylation and chromatin measurements supply other kinds of evidence. A technique measures particular features; combining them can distinguish sequence change from altered expression. Tissue composition, sample quality and correlation-versus-causation limits remain important."]],"mis":"Different expression can occur without a changed DNA base sequence. An environmental association does not prove inherited epigenetic transmission.","worked":["Explain how a neuron and a muscle cell can have essentially the same inherited DNA yet different functions.","Different regulatory patterns lead to expression of different genes and amounts of products. Different proteins support different structures, such as neuronal communication processes and contractile machinery. The differences need not require removal of all unused genes or a different inherited genome."],"workedMore":[["Distinguish a base substitution from methylation-associated regulation.","A base substitution changes the DNA nucleotide sequence. DNA methylation adds chemical groups while ordinarily retaining that underlying base sequence. Methylation can influence transcription in a context-dependent way; the two changes should not be described as identical mechanisms."],["Evaluate: “Histone modification turns genes off by changing the histone amino-acid sequence.”","Common histone modifications add or remove chemical tags on existing proteins. They can change chromatin access or recruit regulators without rewriting the protein sequence. Some modifications favour activity and others repression depending on context, so a universal off-switch claim is inaccurate."]],"practice":[["A study finds different gene expression in two diet groups. Explain why this does not prove that diet created a new inherited allele.","Expression amount can change without altering the underlying gene sequence. Diet may influence expression through regulatory or other physiological mechanisms. Demonstrating a new inherited allele requires sequence and inheritance evidence; expression data alone are insufficient.",6],["Outline how to improve a study reporting an association between methylation and expression.","Control relevant tissue, developmental and environmental differences and use appropriate replication. Measure the same defined regulatory region and expression outcome with reliable methods. Use a suitable intervention or other mechanistic evidence where ethical and feasible to test causation, rather than relying only on correlation.",6],["Why must a claim about human transgenerational epigenetic inheritance be treated carefully?","Genetic variation and shared family environments can generate intergenerational associations. Direct exposure of developing offspring or their germ cells can complicate which generations are independently unexposed. Specific evidence across appropriately defined generations is needed; not every acquired trait is inherited.",6],["How can DNA sequencing and RNA measurement help distinguish two explanations of a cell difference?","Sequencing tests for base-sequence differences; RNA measurements examine expression. Similar inherited sequences with different RNA patterns support regulation as part of the explanation, while confounding and causal limits still require evaluation.",4]],"quiz":["What is an epigenetic change in this course context?",["A regulatory change affecting expression without replacing the DNA base sequence.","Any deliberate invention of a needed allele.","Loss of every gene in a specialised cell.","A change in blood group caused by transfusion."],0,"A regulatory change affecting expression without replacing the DNA base sequence."],"quizzes":[["Why do neurons and muscle cells differ?",["They express different combinations and amounts of genes.","Each has no shared inherited genes.","Only neurons contain proteins.","Only muscle cells have DNA."],0,"They express different combinations and amounts of genes."],["Which statement about histone methylation is most accurate?",["Its effects depend on the site and biological context.","It always increases every gene’s transcription.","It always changes the amino-acid sequence.","It makes DNA into RNA directly."],0,"Its effects depend on the site and biological context."],["What does a methylation–expression correlation establish alone?",["An association, without proving causation.","A universal causal mechanism in every tissue.","Inheritance across all generations.","The absence of any confounder."],0,"An association, without proving causation."],["What commonly accompanies histone acetylation?",["More accessible chromatin.","Conversion of a chromosome into glucose.","Removal of all nucleotides.","Mandatory permanent gene deletion."],0,"More accessible chromatin."]],"images":["protein","dna"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"mitosis","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"04 · Cell cycle and mitosis","goal":"Follow chromosome accounting through growth and mitotic division.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["Replication before division and chromosome accounting","A chromosome is counted by its centromere under the conventional school counting system. After DNA replication, one duplicated chromosome consists of two sister chromatids joined together, so DNA amount doubles without necessarily doubling chromosome number. Homologous chromosomes are corresponding maternal and paternal chromosomes with the same types of genes at corresponding loci, but they can carry different alleles. Sister chromatids are copies produced from one chromosome and are initially very similar. Distinguish these relationships when drawing meiosis. Ploidy describes the number of chromosome sets, not the number of DNA strands or chromatids. A diploid cell has two sets and a haploid cell one, while chromosomes in either cell may be duplicated or unduplicated depending on the stage."],["The cell cycle and mitotic purposes","The cell cycle includes periods of growth and preparation, DNA replication and division. Interphase is metabolically active and includes the DNA synthesis stage, so it should not be described simply as a resting phase. Mitosis distributes replicated chromosomes into daughter nuclei, and cytokinesis divides the cell's cytoplasm. The processes support growth, repair and replacement in many multicellular organisms and asexual reproduction in some eukaryotes. Regulation limits inappropriate division, and daughter cells can differentiate after division. Mitosis generally preserves chromosome set number, but it does not create genetic uniformity across every cell in an organism indefinitely because mutations and later expression differences can occur. State the role and outcome required by the particular biological context."],["Mitosis: chromosome movement","During mitosis, chromosomes condense, a spindle forms and nuclear envelope arrangements change. Duplicated chromosomes align with spindle attachments so that sister chromatids can separate toward opposite poles. New nuclear arrangements form around the separated sets before or alongside cytokinesis. The familiar stages are prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase, with more detailed classifications sometimes distinguishing intermediate events. The key mechanism is accurate separation of sister chromatids to preserve genetic information in daughter cells. A labelled model should show what separates and where it moves. Do not draw homologous pairing and crossing over as ordinary mitotic events. Cytokinesis differs structurally between many animal and plant cells, with a cleavage furrow or formation of a cell plate respectively."],["Interphase is active","Interphase comprises growth and preparation stages as well as DNA synthesis. In G1 a cell performs functions and prepares for possible replication; during S phase DNA is replicated; in G2 it prepares for division and checks readiness. Cells can also exit active cycling. Interphase is not a period in which a cell is biologically inactive, and chromosomes need not be condensed and visibly X-shaped throughout it. Each chromosome is counted by its centromere, not by the number of visible arms in a cartoon."],["Replicated chromosomes","After DNA replication, a typical human somatic cell still has 46 chromosomes, now represented by 92 sister chromatids. Sister chromatids are copies joined at a centromeric region; homologous chromosomes are the maternal and paternal members of a pair and need not carry identical alleles. At separation, each sister chromatid becomes a chromosome in its own right. Counting must therefore specify the stage and whether the question refers to the whole dividing cell or each future daughter nucleus."],["Chromosome movement","During mitosis chromosomes condense, the nuclear envelope breaks down and spindle attachments organise chromosomes at the cell equator. Sister chromatids then separate and move towards opposite poles. Nuclear structures re-form around the separated chromosome sets. Cytokinesis divides the cytoplasm, commonly by a cleavage furrow in animal cells. Phase names help organise this sequence, but an answer must identify what actually separates; saying “the chromosomes split” without distinguishing sisters from homologues can hide a misunderstanding."],["Growth, repair and limits","Mitosis supports growth and replacement of cells, producing daughter nuclei with the same chromosome complement in a normal division. The products are genetically very similar, although mutations can arise and later expression patterns may differ. Division and differentiation are not the same: a cell can divide while maintaining its type, or alter expression and specialise. Not every adult human cell divides readily, so damage to a tissue does not guarantee complete regeneration through ordinary mitosis."],["Reading a mitotic index","A mitotic index is the fraction of sampled cells observed in mitosis, often expressed as a percentage. If 18 of 120 cells are mitotic, the index is 15%. This is a snapshot rather than the complete division history of every cell. A high value can reflect many cells entering mitosis, a long mitotic duration or disrupted progression. Interpret it alongside tissue type, sampling and other evidence instead of diagnosing cancer from the percentage alone."]],"mis":"DNA amount doubles during S phase, but chromosome number is conventionally counted by centromeres. Chromatid number and chromosome number are not interchangeable.","worked":["State chromosome and chromatid counts in a typical human somatic cell after S phase but before chromatids separate.","There are 46 chromosomes, counted by centromeric units. Each consists of two sister chromatids, giving 92 chromatids. DNA replication doubles DNA content without immediately doubling the chromosome count."],"workedMore":[["A sample contains 240 cells, with 36 in mitosis. Calculate the mitotic index and state a limitation.","Mitotic index = mitotic cells/total cells × 100. 36/240 × 100 = 15%. It is a sampled snapshot influenced by mitotic duration and sampling, not a direct diagnosis or a count of all future divisions."],["Explain how mitosis can help replace damaged cells while distinguishing division from differentiation.","Normal mitosis produces daughter nuclei with the same chromosome complement. Cell proliferation increases the supply of replacement cells where suitable cells can divide. Differentiation changes expression and specialised function; it is a separate process and regeneration capacity varies by tissue."]],"practice":[["A diploid human somatic cell dividing for tissue repair shows duplicated chromosomes aligned singly, then sister chromatids moving to opposite poles. Identify the division process and explain.","The stated somatic growth/repair context supports mitosis. Sister chromatids separate and the normal daughter nuclei retain the starting chromosome complement. Single alignment and sister separation alone can also occur in meiosis II, so the context and chromosome accounting are important.",6],["Correct: “Interphase is resting because nothing occurs until the chromosomes become visible.”","Cells carry out metabolic and specialised activities during interphase. A cycling cell grows and replicates DNA during S phase, then prepares for division. Chromatin need not be highly condensed to be active or to carry genetic information.",6],["Distinguish sister chromatids and homologous chromosomes.","Sister chromatids are replicated copies of one chromosome joined before separation. Homologues form a corresponding maternal–paternal pair with the same gene loci but potentially different alleles. Ordinary mitosis separates sisters; meiosis I separates homologues.",6],["Give three paired differences between mitosis and meiosis.","Mitosis has one nuclear division versus two; normal products maintain chromosome set number versus haploid reduction; homologue pairing and crossing over are normal meiotic-I features rather than ordinary mitotic features.",6]],"quiz":["A 2n=8 cell has replicated DNA. How many chromosomes before separation?",["8","16","4","2"],0,"Chromosomes are counted by centromeres; replication produces sister chromatids without doubling chromosome number at this stage."],"quizzes":[["What separates in ordinary mitotic anaphase?",["Sister chromatids.","Only maternal homologues from paternal homologues.","All genes from DNA.","Amino acids from haemoglobin."],0,"Sister chromatids."],["How many chromosomes does a normal replicated human somatic cell have before separation?",["46 chromosomes.","92 chromosomes solely because DNA replicated.","23 chromosomes.","Four chromosomes."],0,"46 chromosomes."],["What is cytokinesis?",["Division of the cytoplasm.","Complementary DNA base pairing.","Loss of every organelle.","Transcription of an anticodon."],0,"Division of the cytoplasm."],["What does interphase include?",["Growth, cellular activity and DNA replication when cycling.","Only complete inactivity.","Only chromosome separation.","Fusion of haploid gametes."],0,"Growth, cellular activity and DNA replication when cycling."]],"images":["mitosis"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]],"tables":[{"title":"Paired comparison of mitosis and meiosis","headers":["Feature","Mitosis","Meiosis"],"rows":[["Primary role","Growth, repair and cell replacement","Gamete production and genetic variation"],["Replication","Once before division","Once before two nuclear divisions"],["Homologue pairing / crossing over","Not a normal feature of mitosis","Pairing and crossing over in meiosis I"],["First separation","Sister chromatids","Homologous chromosomes"],["Normal chromosome outcome","Maintained relative to starting nucleus","Reduced to haploid sets"],["Genetic products","Normally same inherited content, aside from errors","New combinations; errors can alter dosage"]]}]},{"id":"meiosis","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"05 · Meiosis, chromosome number and variation","goal":"Explain reduction division, recombination, independent assortment and nondisjunction.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["Replication before division and chromosome accounting","A chromosome is counted by its centromere under the conventional school counting system. After DNA replication, one duplicated chromosome consists of two sister chromatids joined together, so DNA amount doubles without necessarily doubling chromosome number. Homologous chromosomes are corresponding maternal and paternal chromosomes with the same types of genes at corresponding loci, but they can carry different alleles. Sister chromatids are copies produced from one chromosome and are initially very similar. Distinguish these relationships when drawing meiosis. Ploidy describes the number of chromosome sets, not the number of DNA strands or chromatids. A diploid cell has two sets and a haploid cell one, while chromosomes in either cell may be duplicated or unduplicated depending on the stage."],["Meiosis I: homologues and reduction","Meiosis begins after one round of DNA replication and includes two divisions. During meiosis I, homologous chromosomes pair, and non-sister chromatids can exchange corresponding segments through crossing over. Homologous pairs align at the equator, and the homologues then separate to opposite cells. Sister chromatids ordinarily remain together during this first separation. Reduction in chromosome set number occurs because each daughter receives one member of each homologous pair. The orientation of different pairs can vary independently, contributing to genetic variation. Clearly distinguish homologous chromosomes from sister chromatids when naming what separates. A cell entering meiosis with two sets can produce cells with one set after the first division even though each chromosome may still contain two chromatids."],["Meiosis II and haploid products","Meiosis II separates sister chromatids after the first division, without a new full round of DNA replication between the divisions. A typical model produces four haploid products from one original diploid cell. The products can differ genetically because of crossing over and chromosome assortment. In actual reproductive development, products can have different sizes or fates, as in animal egg formation, so four identical-looking circles should not imply four equivalent functioning eggs. The syllabus mechanism concerns transfer of genetic information and variation. In a species with diploid number 2n = 8, the final products ordinarily have n = 4 chromosomes, rather than two because there are two divisions. Count chromosome sets and identify which structures separate at each stage."],["Crossing over creates new combinations","Crossing over exchanges corresponding DNA segments between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes during meiosis I. This can create new combinations of existing alleles on a chromatid. It ordinarily does not create a new allele in the way mutation can, although unusual exchanges can cause changes. Show matching loci and exchanged segments in a drawing, keeping the homologues distinguishable. Crossing over can separate alleles at linked loci, with the frequency depending in part on their relative positions. The basic implication is increased genetic variety among meiotic products. An explanation should state the event, stage, structures involved and effect on allele combinations instead of using the word swapping without identifying what is exchanged."],["Independent assortment and fertilisation","Independent assortment arises from the alternative orientations of homologous pairs during meiosis I, producing different combinations of chromosomes in gametes when the loci or chromosomes under discussion assort independently. For n homologous pairs, the simple model allows 2ⁿ combinations from assortment alone, excluding crossing-over effects. Random fertilisation adds variation by combining gametes from two parents, restoring the diploid set number in a zygote. The product is not a complete copy of either parent. These processes recombine existing variation; mutation remains the ultimate source of new alleles. Use probabilities carefully, recognising assumptions about independent events and compatible gametes. A genetic prediction gives an expected distribution over many reproductive events, not a promise of a fixed outcome for a small family."],["Chromosome changes, inheritance and errors","Chromosomal mutations can involve deletion, duplication, inversion or translocation of segments. Errors in chromosome separation, including non-disjunction, can produce cells with an abnormal number of particular chromosomes. Such outcomes differ from a single base substitution. A mutation in a cell contributing to gametes can be transmitted to offspring, while a mutation in a typical somatic cell is not inherited through sexual reproduction in the same way. Some plants and asexually reproducing systems create additional contexts for transmission, so define the organism and route. Reduced fertility or altered development can follow certain changes, but effects vary. The comparison between mitosis and meiosis should include number of divisions, what separates, ploidy outcome, genetic variation and biological purpose."],["One replication, two divisions","A diploid germ-line precursor replicates DNA before meiosis I, then undergoes two nuclear divisions without another full DNA replication between them. Meiosis I separates homologous chromosomes and reduces the chromosome complement. Meiosis II separates sister chromatids. Human products normally contain 23 chromosomes rather than 46. The general four-haploid-product model describes chromosome outcomes; actual human oogenesis allocates cytoplasm unevenly and produces one large functional egg plus polar bodies rather than four equivalent eggs."],["Recombination is not directed adaptation","Crossing over between nonsister chromatids of paired homologues can exchange corresponding DNA regions, producing new allele combinations. It does not ordinarily create each allele from nothing and does not happen because an individual knows which trait will be useful. Independent assortment reflects the alternative orientations of homologous pairs during meiosis I. Fertilisation combines gametes from two parents, adding another source of genotype variation. Distinguish new combinations of existing alleles from mutation as a source of new sequence variants."],["Nondisjunction and evidence","Failure of homologues to separate properly in meiosis I, or sisters in meiosis II, can produce gametes with abnormal chromosome numbers. Following fertilisation, an embryo may have an extra or missing chromosome. The exact pattern depends on which division failed and which gamete participated. A karyotype can reveal number and large structural changes but cannot detect every small gene mutation. Avoid treating every genetic condition as either visible in a karyotype or caused by a single nondisjunction event."],["Read an actual karyotype at the appropriate resolution","A karyotype arranges photographed chromosomes into pairs using features such as size, centromere position and banding. In a typical diploid human somatic sample, identify 22 autosomal pairs and a sex-chromosome pair; the displayed preparation may show duplicated chromosomes because of the cell-division stage sampled. A karyotype can reveal selected number or large structural differences but not every single-base variant or every cause of a phenotype. Link an observed extra chromosome with a possible separation error while retaining the distinction between an observed pattern and its exact developmental cause."]],"mis":"DNA replicates once before two divisions. It does not replicate again between meiosis I and meiosis II.","worked":["Compare what separates in meiosis I and meiosis II and state the usual effect on chromosome complement.","Meiosis I separates homologous chromosomes, reducing a diploid complement to haploid sets. Meiosis II separates sister chromatids without another preceding full replication. Normal products are haploid; human gametes normally contain 23 chromosomes."],"workedMore":[["For a diploid organism with 2n = 8, calculate possible chromosome combinations from independent assortment alone and state excluded sources of variation.","The haploid number is n = 4. Independent-assortment combinations = 2ⁿ = 2⁴ = 16. This simplified count excludes crossing over, mutation and combinations created by fertilisation."],["Explain how crossing over contributes to variation without necessarily creating new alleles.","Paired homologous chromosomes can exchange corresponding DNA between nonsister chromatids. This creates new combinations of existing alleles along chromatids. Mutation changes DNA sequence and can originate new alleles; recombination and mutation are distinct mechanisms."]],"practice":[["Evaluate: “Meiosis replicates DNA twice so each egg retains 46 chromosomes.”","There is normally one DNA replication before the two divisions. No full replication occurs between meiosis I and II. The divisions produce haploid chromosome sets, normally 23 in a human functional gamete.",6],["Explain how meiotic nondisjunction can produce a trisomic embryo after fertilisation with a normal gamete.","Separation failure can produce a gamete containing an extra chromosome. Fusion with a normal haploid gamete combines the extra chromosome with the normal contribution. The resulting zygote has three copies of that chromosome; outcome depends on the abnormal gamete and chromosome involved.",6],["Why is a diagram of four equal meiotic products not a fully accurate model of human oogenesis?","It represents haploid nuclear chromosome outcomes. Human oogenesis divides cytoplasm unequally, producing one large functional egg and smaller polar bodies. Equivalent chromosome complements therefore do not mean four equivalent functional eggs.",6],["Why does a normal-looking karyotype not exclude every inherited disorder?","It examines chromosome-scale patterns at limited resolution. A small DNA-sequence variant can affect a gene without changing the visible chromosome number or large-scale arrangement.",3]],"quiz":["What separates at meiosis I?",["Homologous chromosomes","Normally all sister chromatids","Every nucleotide","Only ribosomes"],0,"Meiosis I segregates homologues, whereas meiosis II separates sister chromatids."],"quizzes":[["Between which chromatids does ordinary meiotic crossing over occur?",["Non-sister chromatids of homologues","Only sister chromatids of one chromosome","Only unrelated RNA strands","Only cell-wall fibres"],0,"Corresponding segments exchange between non-sister chromatids in paired homologous chromosomes."],["How many independent-assortment combinations for n=4, ignoring crossover?",["16","8","4","32"],0,"Two orientations for each of four pairs give 2⁴=16 whole-chromosome combinations in the simplified model."],["Which event restores diploidy after haploid gamete production?",["Fertilisation","Another meiosis II without fusion","Denaturation","Gel electrophoresis"],0,"Fusion of haploid gametes combines chromosome sets, restoring the diploid number in the standard sexual cycle."],["How many DNA replication rounds normally occur before the two meiotic divisions?",["One.","Two, one before each division.","None.","Four complete rounds."],0,"One."]],"images":["meiosis","crossing","y11-karyotype"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"stem-cells","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"06 · Stem cells, differentiation and cancer","goal":"Distinguish potency, cell division and differentiation and evaluate growth-control failures.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["Stem cells combine renewal and development","A stem cell can self-renew and generate cells that differentiate into one or more specialised types. Self-renewal maintains a supply of stem-like cells, while differentiation changes expression and function. These are related but distinct properties: a rapidly dividing cell is not automatically a pluripotent stem cell. Stem cells exist in development and in some adult tissues, where their normal roles vary. Avoid defining them only as “cells with no purpose”; their biological capacity supports maintenance and development."],["Potency describes possible outcomes","Totipotent cells can generate the embryonic tissues and the additional tissues required for development in the appropriate context. Pluripotent cells can generate the major body cell types but not by themselves the full extraembryonic support needed for a complete organism. Multipotent cells produce a more limited range, such as a family of blood-cell lineages. Potency is not a measure of cell size, current division rate or ethical value. State the possible developmental range rather than simply rank labels as better or worse."],["Differentiation through expression","As cells differentiate, changing regulatory patterns produce proteins supporting particular structures and functions. Most nucleated specialised cells retain essentially the same inherited genome rather than deleting all genes they do not currently use. Signals and local conditions help influence developmental pathways. The relationship links epigenetics, gene expression and tissue organisation. A neuron is not formed simply by mitosis making DNA contain a new list of neuronal genes."],["Cell-cycle regulation","Normal cell populations regulate progression, replication and division through interacting signals and checkpoints. Damaged cells may pause, repair or undergo programmed death. Cancer can develop when accumulated genetic and other changes disrupt growth regulation, survival and tissue behaviour. It is not one universal disease caused by every mitosis. A benign tumour and a malignant cancer differ in behaviour, including invasion and possible spread; high proliferation alone is not a complete diagnosis."],["Invasion and metastasis","Malignant cells can invade surrounding tissue and, in some cases, spread through blood or lymph to other sites, where they establish secondary growths. A cancer spreading to another organ is not necessarily a new cancer originating from that organ’s normal cells. These processes can compromise function through tissue damage and other mechanisms. Distinguish local growth, invasion and metastatic spread instead of using “large tumour” as the sole definition of malignancy."],["Potential and limitations of cell therapy","Cell-based therapies aim to restore or replace particular functions, but safe use requires suitable cell identity, integration, control and evidence of benefit. Some established procedures use blood-forming stem cells, while many other proposed applications remain research or have narrower evidence. Risks can include immune responses, inappropriate growth and poor functional integration. Do not claim that stem cells already cure every nervous-system disease. Year 12 develops the potential of neuro-cell therapies alongside their limitations."],["Screening versus diagnosis","Screening tests are used in defined populations to identify people who may need further assessment before symptoms. They are not perfect and can yield false positives or false negatives; further testing establishes diagnoses. Cervical screening in Australia focuses on relevant HPV evidence rather than being identical to the old routine Pap-only model. Breast and prostate examples also require distinguishing detection from diagnosis and considering benefits, harms and uncertainty. Course learning explains principles rather than recommending personal screening schedules."]],"mis":"Self-renewal and potency are different properties. Rapid division alone does not establish stem-cell identity or safe therapeutic use.","worked":["Compare pluripotent and multipotent cells using possible differentiation outcomes.","Pluripotent cells can generate the major body cell types under suitable conditions. Multipotent cells produce a more restricted related range of specialised cells. Potency describes developmental range, not division speed or guaranteed clinical safety."],"workedMore":[["Explain how loss of growth regulation can contribute to cancer without claiming all mitosis is cancer.","Normal division is regulated and supports growth and repair. Accumulated changes can disrupt checkpoints, survival controls and tissue behaviour. Malignant behaviour may involve invasion and spread; ordinary regulated division is not itself cancer."],["A screening test returns a positive result. Explain why further assessment is needed.","Screening identifies elevated likelihood or relevant evidence rather than automatically proving disease. False positives and other test limitations can occur. Appropriate follow-up investigates the result and informs diagnosis and decisions."]],"practice":[["Explain why a differentiated neuron need not lose every gene associated with other cell types.","Specialisation primarily involves altered expression and protein production. Most nucleated cells retain essentially the same inherited genome. Unused genes can remain present but differently regulated rather than physically deleted.",6],["State one potential benefit and two challenges of a proposed stem-cell therapy.","Suitable replacement cells may restore a missing or damaged function. Cells must have appropriate identity, integration and controlled growth. Immune compatibility and reliable evidence of benefit and safety are needed; potential does not equal a universal established cure.",6],["Evaluate: “A tumour is malignant only if it is very large.”","Size alone does not define malignant behaviour. Invasion and capacity for spread are important distinctions from benign growth. Assessment needs tissue and clinical evidence rather than a single size threshold.",6]],"quiz":["What distinguishes a stem cell?",["Self-renewal and capacity for appropriate differentiation.","Absence of any function or DNA.","Mandatory unlimited safe growth.","Only having many mitochondria."],0,"Self-renewal and capacity for appropriate differentiation."],"quizzes":[["What is pluripotency?",["Capacity to generate the major body cell types in the appropriate context.","A guarantee of generating a complete organism alone.","Production of only one mature blood cell.","The number of chromosomes in plasma."],0,"Capacity to generate the major body cell types in the appropriate context."],["What usually changes during differentiation?",["Gene expression and specialised products.","Removal of every unused inherited gene.","Conversion of every chromosome into protein.","Loss of all cell membranes."],0,"Gene expression and specialised products."],["What characterises metastasis?",["Spread and establishment of malignant growth at another site.","Normal replacement of an erythrocyte.","Any safe mitotic division.","Lipid absorption into lacteals."],0,"Spread and establishment of malignant growth at another site."],["What does a screening result identify?",["A possible need for further assessment, subject to test limitations.","A guaranteed definitive diagnosis in every case.","The absence of all uncertainty.","A replacement for informed consent."],0,"A possible need for further assessment, subject to test limitations."]],"images":["mitosis","y11-germ-layers"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"inheritance","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"07 · Autosomal inheritance and probability","goal":"Use explicit genetic models to explain autosomal dominant and recessive inheritance.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["Genotype, phenotype and a notation system","A genotype describes the allele combination at the relevant locus or loci, while a phenotype describes the observed or measured trait. A homozygote has two matching alleles at a diploid locus and a heterozygote has different alleles. In a simple dominance model, A can represent a dominant allele and a a recessive allele, but explain the symbols before using them. Dominance concerns the phenotype expressed in a heterozygote, not which allele is common, stronger or more evolutionarily advanced. Environmental effects and gene interactions can complicate the relationship. Identify parental genotypes from evidence, derive possible gametes and then combine them. A Punnett square displays possible combinations and expected probabilities under its assumptions rather than recording actual offspring automatically."],["Monohybrid crosses and expected ratios","For Aa × Aa, each parent produces A or a gametes with probability one half under normal segregation. The possible offspring combinations are AA, Aa, Aa and aa, giving expected genotype proportions of one quarter, one half and one quarter. With complete dominance, the expected dominant-to-recessive phenotype ratio is 3:1. The probabilities concern independent reproductive events, and a small actual sample may depart from the ratio through chance. A previously born recessive offspring does not make the next child more or less likely to be recessive in this simple model. Show the gametes, square and genotype–phenotype interpretation. Do not use the 3:1 phenotype ratio for a cross with different parental genotypes or a different pattern of dominance."],["Test crosses and inferring an unknown genotype","An individual showing a dominant phenotype could be homozygous dominant or heterozygous under a simple model. A test cross pairs it with a homozygous recessive individual. If the unknown is Aa, expected offspring are half Aa and half aa; if it is AA, all offspring are expected to be Aa. Observing a recessive offspring supports the presence of the recessive allele in the unknown parent, under the stated assumptions. Observing only dominant offspring in a small sample cannot prove homozygosity because chance may conceal a heterozygous cross. Distinguish evidence from certainty and explain what additional offspring or genetic testing would improve confidence. The method assumes a known inheritance pattern and that relevant environmental or scoring effects do not obscure the phenotypes."],["Dominant does not mean common","Under complete dominance, one copy of a dominant allele is sufficient for the defined dominant phenotype, while the recessive phenotype requires two recessive copies. This says nothing by itself about population frequency, severity or desirability. A rare dominant disease allele can remain rare. Use uppercase and lowercase notation only after defining the alleles and phenotype model; a letter is a symbol, not the mechanism of dominance."],["Huntington disease as a model","Huntington disease is used in the course as an autosomal dominant example. In a simplified heterozygous affected-parent and unaffected-parent cross, each child has a one-half probability of inheriting the disease-associated allele. Age of onset and other clinical complexities mean that an apparently unaffected younger person may not provide complete genotype information. Classroom crosses are probability models, not individual diagnoses. A pedigree question must state whether it assumes full penetrance and reliable phenotype classification."],["Phenylketonuria as a model","Phenylketonuria is commonly modelled as an autosomal recessive condition involving impaired phenylalanine metabolism. Two carriers can be unaffected yet each transmit the recessive allele, giving a one-quarter affected probability per child under the standard model. Screening can identify affected infants and management can influence outcomes, illustrating why inherited genotype and measured phenotype are connected but not identical. A dietary intervention changes metabolic consequences rather than replacing the inherited alleles."],["Each conception is a new probability event","A Punnett square gives expected proportions across many conceptions, not an order in which outcomes must occur. For two carriers, an unaffected first child does not make the next child “due” to be affected. Conditional questions can change the sample space: among unaffected children of two carriers, two-thirds are expected carriers under the complete recessive model. Identify whether the question asks about all children or only an already-defined subgroup."],["Test crosses and evidence","A dominant-phenotype individual may be homozygous or heterozygous in a simple complete-dominance model. A cross with a homozygous recessive individual can provide evidence: a recessive offspring demonstrates that the dominant parent can transmit the recessive allele. A small sample containing only dominant offspring does not prove homozygosity, because chance can produce such a sample from a heterozygote. Human inheritance analysis uses pedigrees and testing, not deliberately arranging reproductive test crosses."]],"mis":"Dominant describes expression in a heterozygote; it does not mean common, more beneficial or guaranteed to be inherited.","worked":["Two unaffected carriers of a recessive condition have genotypes Aa and Aa. Calculate genotype probabilities and affected risk.","Possible combinations are AA, Aa, Aa and aa. Expected genotype probabilities are 1/4 AA, 1/2 Aa and 1/4 aa. The recessive affected probability is 1/4 per conception; carriers are unaffected under the stated complete model."],"workedMore":[["For two unaffected carriers Aa × Aa of a fully recessive condition, find the carrier probability given that their child is unaffected.","Exclude aa from the unaffected sample space. The remaining expected categories are AA, Aa and Aa in the ratio 1:2. Conditional carrier probability is 2/3, rather than the unconditional 1/2."],["Under a simple autosomal dominant model, an affected heterozygote Dd and unaffected dd parent have children. Calculate risk and distinguish dominance from frequency.","The heterozygote produces D and d gametes in equal expected proportions; the other parent contributes d. Half the expected offspring are Dd and half dd, giving 1/2 dominant-phenotype risk under the assumptions. Dominance describes heterozygote expression and does not establish that D is common in the population."]],"practice":[["Explain why three unaffected children of carrier parents do not make an affected fourth child certain.","The cross has a one-quarter affected probability per conception under the model. Earlier outcomes do not remove alleles or change the parental genotypes. Independent probability events do not enforce the expected ratio within every family of four.",6],["A dominant individual crossed with aa has four dominant offspring. Does this prove the unknown individual is AA?","AA would produce dominant offspring under the simple model. Aa could also produce four dominant offspring by chance: (1/2)⁴ = 1/16. The sample supports a comparison but cannot prove AA solely from absence of observed recessive offspring.",6],["Explain why managing a recessive metabolic condition through diet does not mean the inherited genotype has been corrected.","Diet can alter substrate exposure and therefore the metabolic consequences of the impaired pathway. This can improve the measured phenotype or health outcome. The underlying inherited alleles ordinarily remain; environmental management and gene-sequence replacement are different.",6]],"quiz":["For Aa×Aa under complete dominance, what is recessive phenotype probability?",["1/4","1/2","3/4","1"],0,"The aa outcome occupies one of four equally probable combinations; observed small samples can differ from expectation."],"quizzes":[["What does an affected aa child imply about two unaffected parents under the stated simple recessive model?",["Both are Aa","Both must be AA","Both must be aa","Neither supplied a"],0,"Each parent supplied a and each remains unaffected, making both heterozygous under complete penetrance and no new mutation."],["Does a dominant allele have to be common?",["No, dominance concerns heterozygote phenotype","Yes, always over 50%","Yes, always over 90%","Only if it is on an autosome"],0,"Dominance describes expression relationships and does not by itself determine population frequency or fitness."],["For Aa × aa, what is the probability of an aa child?",["One-half.","One-quarter.","Three-quarters.","Zero."],0,"One-half."],["Among unaffected offspring of Aa × Aa under complete recessive inheritance, what proportion are carriers?",["Two-thirds.","One-quarter.","One-half.","All."],0,"Two-thirds."]],"images":["y11-recessive-inheritance"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]],"tables":[{"title":"Aa × Aa: allele combinations","headers":["Maternal gamete","Paternal A","Paternal a"],"rows":[["A","AA","Aa"],["a","aA","aa"]]}]},{"id":"codominance","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"08 · Codominance, multiple alleles and ABO inheritance","goal":"Use allele notation to distinguish codominance, incomplete dominance and multiple alleles.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["Genotype, phenotype and a notation system","A genotype describes the allele combination at the relevant locus or loci, while a phenotype describes the observed or measured trait. A homozygote has two matching alleles at a diploid locus and a heterozygote has different alleles. In a simple dominance model, A can represent a dominant allele and a a recessive allele, but explain the symbols before using them. Dominance concerns the phenotype expressed in a heterozygote, not which allele is common, stronger or more evolutionarily advanced. Environmental effects and gene interactions can complicate the relationship. Identify parental genotypes from evidence, derive possible gametes and then combine them. A Punnett square displays possible combinations and expected probabilities under its assumptions rather than recording actual offspring automatically."],["Incomplete dominance and codominance","In incomplete dominance, the heterozygous phenotype is distinguishable and often intermediate between the two homozygous phenotypes, such as an intermediate pigment level in a simplified flower example. In codominance, both allelic effects are detectably expressed in the heterozygote rather than combining into one intermediate effect. These patterns change genotype–phenotype relationships but do not invalidate the segregation of alleles during meiosis. A heterozygote cross can produce a 1:2:1 phenotype ratio as well as a 1:2:1 genotype ratio when all three phenotypes are distinct. Use an appropriate notation and define each genotype's phenotype. Saying the dominant allele is weaker in incomplete dominance is less useful than explaining the actual heterozygote outcome and the distinction from codominance."],["Multiple alleles and the ABO model","A population can contain more than two alleles at a locus even though a diploid individual carries only two there. The ABO blood-group model uses Iᴬ, Iᴮ and i. Iᴬ and Iᴮ are codominant, while each is dominant relative to i. Genotypes IᴬIᴬ or Iᴬi give phenotype A, IᴮIᴮ or Iᴮi give B, IᴬIᴮ gives AB and ii gives O in the simplified model. If Iᴬi and Iᴮi parents are crossed, the expected phenotypes A, B, AB and O each occur with probability one quarter. This is a genetic teaching model, not a complete guide to clinical transfusion. State the parental genotypes because a phenotype A or B alone may not uniquely determine the alleles."],["Polygenic traits and environmental interaction","Polygenic traits depend on contributions from several genes and often show continuous variation, with environmental influences adding to the distribution. Height, for example, is affected by many genetic variants and developmental conditions. A smooth trait distribution does not mean one allele has unlimited values, and a continuous trait cannot usually be analysed with one simple dominant–recessive Punnett square. Multiple alleles concerns several variants at one locus, while polygenic inheritance concerns contributions from several loci. Keep those concepts distinct. Family resemblance supports genetic influence but does not remove shared environmental explanations. A biological account should connect gene effects, environment and phenotype without claiming that a broad continuous trait is fully determined by one gene or always inherited in a fixed ratio."],["ABO genotypes","The basic ABO model uses Iᴬ, Iᴮ and i. Iᴬ and Iᴮ are codominant with each other and each is dominant to i for the corresponding antigen phenotype. Group A can be IᴬIᴬ or Iᴬi, group B can be IᴮIᴮ or Iᴮi, group AB is IᴬIᴮ and group O is ii. There are more than two alleles in the population, but each ordinary diploid individual has two alleles at this locus. “Multiple alleles” does not mean every individual carries every population variant."],["Expression versus blending","Codominance means that the specified effects of both alleles are detectable in the heterozygote, as with A and B antigens in group AB. Incomplete dominance refers to a heterozygote phenotype intermediate under the defined measurement. Neither changes the principle that alleles segregate into gametes. Do not call AB blood a mixture that turns A and B antigens into a new intermediate antigen, or assume every measured trait follows one of these simple single-gene models."],["Limits of blood-group inference","Known blood groups can exclude some parental combinations under the stated ABO model, but do not uniquely identify a person or prove a relationship. Many people share each blood group, and clinical or genetic complexities can require more evidence. Use explicit assumptions when answering a classroom inheritance problem. A transfusion compatibility question concerns antigen–antibody interactions, whereas an inheritance question concerns allele transmission; sharing the same letters does not make them the same calculation."]],"mis":"An individual carries two alleles at an autosomal locus, even when more than two alleles exist in the population.","worked":["Cross Iᴬi with Iᴮi and calculate the expected blood-group probabilities.","Gametes are Iᴬ or i from one parent and Iᴮ or i from the other. Products are IᴬIᴮ, Iᴬi, Iᴮi and ii in equal expected proportions. AB, A, B and O each have probability 1/4 under the basic model."],"workedMore":[["Explain why AB is codominance rather than incomplete dominance.","The heterozygote carries Iᴬ and Iᴮ. Both A and B antigen effects are expressed on erythrocytes. They remain distinguishable rather than forming one intermediate antigen phenotype."],["Explain how three population ABO alleles coexist with two alleles in an individual.","The population contains several variants at the same locus. A normal diploid individual inherits one allele from each parent. Two individual copies can therefore be drawn from a population containing more than two alternatives."]],"practice":[["Determine offspring possibilities for IᴬIᴮ × ii.","The AB parent supplies Iᴬ or Iᴮ; the O parent supplies i. Expected offspring are Iᴬi or Iᴮi, each with probability 1/2. Under the supplied basic model, AB and O offspring are not expected.",6],["Distinguish an ABO inheritance calculation from a red-cell compatibility explanation.","Inheritance follows parental allele segregation and combinations in offspring. Transfusion reasoning concerns donor-cell antigens meeting recipient antibodies. A shared group label does not replace the different mechanisms and assumptions.",6],["Evaluate: “Two matching blood groups prove that these individuals are related.”","Many unrelated people share the same group. The ABO model can rule out some combinations under defined assumptions but lacks unique identification power. Relationship claims require additional appropriate genetic and contextual evidence.",6]],"quiz":["Which describes codominance?",["Both allele products are expressed in a heterozygote","Every heterozygote is intermediate","One allele vanishes from DNA","All alleles are equally common"],0,"Codominance differs from incomplete dominance; AB is a standard simplified example of co-expression."],"quizzes":[["Does a dominant allele have to be common?",["No, dominance concerns heterozygote phenotype","Yes, always over 50%","Yes, always over 90%","Only if it is on an autosome"],0,"Dominance describes expression relationships and does not by itself determine population frequency or fitness."],["What is the basic group AB genotype?",["IᴬIᴮ.","ii.","Iᴬi only.","Iᴮi only."],0,"IᴬIᴮ."],["How many ABO alleles does an ordinary diploid individual carry at this locus?",["Two.","All population alleles at once.","One hundred.","None if group O."],0,"Two."],["Can a blood group alone uniquely prove parentage?",["No; it may exclude combinations under assumptions but does not uniquely establish identity.","Yes, because only one person has each group.","Yes, because plasma contains every parent’s genes.","Only if both groups are A."],0,"No; it may exclude combinations under assumptions but does not uniquely establish identity."]],"images":["y11-abo","y11-recessive-inheritance"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"sex-linked","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"09 · X-linked inheritance and conditional risk","goal":"Calculate inheritance of X-linked recessive traits using explicit chromosome and probability assumptions.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["Autosomal and X-linked inheritance","Autosomal loci are on non-sex chromosomes. An X-linked locus is on the X chromosome and follows transmission patterns influenced by the sex-chromosome arrangement. In a standard XX/XY model for an X-linked recessive allele, an XY individual has only one allele at that X locus, so a recessive allele can be expressed without a matching second copy. Fathers pass their X chromosome to daughters and their Y to sons in that model, so an X-linked allele is not directly passed from father to son. Use symbols such as Xᴬ and Xᵃ and write the Y appropriately. Avoid treating sex-linked as a synonym for a trait that merely differs between sexes because of hormonal or environmental effects."],["Conditional probabilities in sex-linked crosses","For an XᴬXᵃ mother and XᴬY father, the standard model predicts XᴬXᴬ, XᴬXᵃ, XᴬY and XᵃY offspring with equal probability. The probability of an affected son among all offspring is one quarter, while the probability of being affected conditional on the offspring being a son is one half. The wording of the question determines the denominator. A carrier daughter in an X-linked recessive model has a different phenotype from an affected daughter under the simplified assumptions. Show the gametes and explain which cases satisfy the requested outcome. Do not label half of all offspring affected simply because half of the sons are affected. The square represents a model with stated chromosomal and inheritance assumptions."],["Chromosomal model and notation","For typical classroom crosses, XX and XY describe common chromosomal patterns associated with female and male reproductive development. This chromosome model is a useful simplification and is not interchangeable with a person’s gender identity or a complete account of every variation in sex development. At many X-linked loci, an XY individual has one X allele rather than a matching copy on the Y. Use a superscript allele symbol, such as Xᴴ and Xʰ, to track the locus rather than imply that every Y chromosome carries the same allele."],["Haemophilia and colour vision examples","Some haemophilia forms and red–green colour-vision differences are used as X-linked recessive examples. In the simplified model, an XY individual with the relevant X allele expresses the trait because there is no corresponding ordinary allele at that locus on the Y. An XX heterozygote may be described as a carrier under stated assumptions. Actual expression can be influenced by mechanisms such as X inactivation, so the simple model should be identified as the model being used rather than a universal account of all clinical variation."],["Carrier mother and unaffected father","For XᴴXʰ × XᴴY, expected products are XᴴXᴴ, XᴴXʰ, XᴴY and XʰY. Under the simple recessive model, half the sons are affected, while one-quarter of all children are affected if X- and Y-bearing fertilisations are equally likely. These two answers differ because their denominators differ. Write whether the question asks about a son, any child, or a particular already-known subgroup before choosing a percentage."],["Transmission patterns","A father passes his X chromosome to daughters in the usual XX/XY model and his Y to sons, so an ordinary X-linked allele does not pass directly father-to-son. A mother can pass either X allele to offspring of either chromosomal category. These patterns help distinguish X-linked from autosomal models in pedigrees, but a small family can fit multiple explanations by chance. Absence of an observed transmission is evidence to evaluate, not an automatic proof of every possible alternative."],["Affected father and carrier mother","For XʰY × XᴴXʰ, expected daughters include XᴴXʰ and XʰXʰ, while sons include XᴴY and XʰY. Affected daughters are therefore possible under an X-linked recessive model when the allele contributions permit them. The shortcut “only males can be affected” is incorrect. Construct the cross from parental gametes instead of replacing reasoning with a slogan about rarity."],["Probability and assumptions","Sex-linked calculations assume the stated allele effects, ordinary chromosome transmission and any supplied penetrance or viability conditions. They predict probabilities rather than fixed family compositions. Human genetic counselling uses more information than a classroom square. Course examples should present the biology respectfully and avoid treating a phenotype as a statement about a person’s ability or value. Where data are uncertain, identify the missing genotype or sample information rather than inventing it."]],"mis":"An affected father does not pass his X chromosome to a son in the standard XX/XY model. State whether a probability is conditional on offspring sex.","worked":["For XᴴXʰ × XᴴY, calculate affected risk among sons and among all children under equal X/Y fertilisation.","Sons receive Y from the father and either maternal X, so half are XʰY. Among all four equally likely products, one is affected under the simple model. Risk is 1/2 conditional on a son, but 1/4 for any child; the sample spaces differ."],"workedMore":[["Cross XʰY with XᴴXʰ and determine daughter outcomes.","Daughters all receive paternal Xʰ. Maternal contribution is Xᴴ or Xʰ, giving XᴴXʰ or XʰXʰ. Under the simple model, half the daughters are carriers and half affected; daughters are not universally protected."],["Explain why a usual direct father-to-son transmission does not fit ordinary X-linked inheritance.","A father contributes Y rather than X to an XY son in the standard model. The son’s X is maternally inherited. Thus the paternal X-linked allele is not directly transmitted through that father-to-son route."]],"practice":[["An affected XʰY father and XᴴXᴴ mother have children. Predict outcomes under complete recessive expression.","Daughters receive paternal Xʰ and maternal Xᴴ, so are XᴴXʰ. Sons receive paternal Y and maternal Xᴴ, so are XᴴY. Daughters are carriers and sons unaffected under the stated model; the paternal affected allele does not enter sons through his Y.",6],["Evaluate: “A recessive X-linked trait can affect only XY individuals.”","An XY individual can express one recessive X allele at the relevant locus. An XX individual with two relevant recessive alleles can also express the trait in the simple model. Frequency patterns do not establish an absolute prohibition on affected daughters.",6],["A marking key gives 25% affected children but a student gives 50% affected sons. Explain when both values are biologically consistent.","For a carrier mother and unaffected father, half the sons are affected in the simple cross. If sons represent half the expected children, affected sons represent one-quarter of all children. Both values can be correct for different questions, but an answer must match the requested denominator.",6]],"quiz":["A carrier mother and unaffected father have an X-linked-recessive model. What is risk conditional on a son?",["1/2","1/4","3/4","0"],0,"Half the sons receive the mother’s affected allele; among all offspring the affected-son outcome is one quarter under the stipulated sex probabilities."],"quizzes":[["Who receives a father’s X chromosome in the usual XX/XY model?",["Daughters.","Sons only.","No offspring.","Every son and no daughter."],0,"Daughters."],["For XᴴXʰ × XᴴY, what is affected probability among all children with equal X/Y fertilisation?",["One-quarter.","One-half solely because half the sons are affected.","All.","Zero."],0,"One-quarter."],["Can an XX individual express an X-linked recessive trait in the simple model?",["Yes, if both relevant X alleles are recessive.","Never under any cross.","Only if no X chromosome is present.","Only by becoming a Y chromosome."],0,"Yes, if both relevant X alleles are recessive."],["What should be specified before a conditional probability answer?",["The subgroup or denominator being considered.","Only the colour used for symbols.","The number of teeth.","The donor plasma group."],0,"The subgroup or denominator being considered."]],"images":["y11-x-linked"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]],"tables":[{"title":"Carrier mother × unaffected father","headers":["Maternal gamete","Paternal X^H","Paternal Y"],"rows":[["X^H","X^H X^H: unaffected under this model","X^H Y: unaffected son"],["X^h","X^H X^h: carrier under this recessive model","X^h Y: affected son"]]}]},{"id":"pedigrees","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"10 · Pedigree analysis and genetic evidence","goal":"Infer compatible inheritance models, genotypes and conditional risks from family evidence.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["Genotype, phenotype and a notation system","A genotype describes the allele combination at the relevant locus or loci, while a phenotype describes the observed or measured trait. A homozygote has two matching alleles at a diploid locus and a heterozygote has different alleles. In a simple dominance model, A can represent a dominant allele and a a recessive allele, but explain the symbols before using them. Dominance concerns the phenotype expressed in a heterozygote, not which allele is common, stronger or more evolutionarily advanced. Environmental effects and gene interactions can complicate the relationship. Identify parental genotypes from evidence, derive possible gametes and then combine them. A Punnett square displays possible combinations and expected probabilities under its assumptions rather than recording actual offspring automatically."],["Autosomal and X-linked inheritance","Autosomal loci are on non-sex chromosomes. An X-linked locus is on the X chromosome and follows transmission patterns influenced by the sex-chromosome arrangement. In a standard XX/XY model for an X-linked recessive allele, an XY individual has only one allele at that X locus, so a recessive allele can be expressed without a matching second copy. Fathers pass their X chromosome to daughters and their Y to sons in that model, so an X-linked allele is not directly passed from father to son. Use symbols such as Xᴬ and Xᵃ and write the Y appropriately. Avoid treating sex-linked as a synonym for a trait that merely differs between sexes because of hormonal or environmental effects."],["Conditional probabilities in sex-linked crosses","For an XᴬXᵃ mother and XᴬY father, the standard model predicts XᴬXᴬ, XᴬXᵃ, XᴬY and XᵃY offspring with equal probability. The probability of an affected son among all offspring is one quarter, while the probability of being affected conditional on the offspring being a son is one half. The wording of the question determines the denominator. A carrier daughter in an X-linked recessive model has a different phenotype from an affected daughter under the simplified assumptions. Show the gametes and explain which cases satisfy the requested outcome. Do not label half of all offspring affected simply because half of the sons are affected. The square represents a model with stated chromosomal and inheritance assumptions."],["Reading and evaluating pedigrees","A pedigree shows relationships and trait occurrence across generations using a stated symbol convention. Squares and circles commonly represent sexes in the classical convention, shading indicates the recorded phenotype, and lines show parentage and partnerships. Infer genotypes using an explicit inheritance hypothesis and test it against every relevant relationship. An autosomal recessive trait can appear in offspring of unaffected carrier parents, while an autosomal dominant trait often appears in successive generations, but small families can produce ambiguous patterns. X-linked inheritance adds sex-specific transmission clues. Absence of a phenotype does not always prove absence of an allele. State uncertainty when several models fit, and distinguish a calculated probability under a model from a medically confirmed diagnosis or a guarantee for a real family."],["Symbols and relationships","A pedigree represents family relationships and the recorded phenotype using a stated symbol convention. Generations, individual numbers and a clear legend make the evidence traceable. Shading indicates the defined phenotype, not every allele an individual carries. An unshaded person can still carry a recessive allele. Read the legend and assumptions first, then refer to specific individuals when justifying a conclusion. A diagram of relationships is not automatically evidence that every untested genotype is known."],["Recessive clues and constraints","Two unaffected parents with an affected child can support recessive inheritance under a fully penetrant simple model, because both may transmit a recessive allele. This does not prove that all possible real-world alternatives are impossible. For an autosomal recessive model, affected individuals are aa; an unaffected parent of an affected child must contribute a and ordinarily has Aa under the stated assumptions. Other unaffected relatives may remain AA or Aa, so preserve uncertainty instead of assigning every blank symbol the same genotype."],["Dominant clues and age effects","A dominant model often produces affected individuals in successive generations, but pedigree size and biological complexities matter. Age-dependent onset can make an apparently unaffected individual difficult to classify, as in Huntington disease examples. A missing phenotype in one generation does not universally disprove every dominant explanation. Classroom tasks may specify complete penetrance and known phenotype at the relevant age; use those supplied assumptions explicitly rather than silently importing clinical complexity or ignoring it."],["Compare competing models","Father-to-son transmission can help exclude an ordinary X-linked route under the standard model, while affected daughters and sons do not alone distinguish autosomal inheritance. Assess all relevant relationships, not one isolated symbol. Some small pedigrees remain consistent with more than one model. A strong answer names the proposed model, cites a specific transmission or parent–child pattern and explains why it supports or excludes that model under the assumptions."],["Screening, consent and privacy","Genetic screening can identify a variant or estimate a risk before symptoms develop, but results can carry uncertainty and implications for relatives. Informed consent, confidentiality and appropriate interpretation matter. A screening result is not necessarily a diagnosis or an inevitable prediction of future health. Evaluate benefits such as informed planning alongside possible anxiety, misuse of information and limitations of the test. Do not turn a classroom pedigree exercise into requests for classmates’ sensitive family medical histories."]],"mis":"An unaffected person can carry a recessive allele. A small pedigree may fit more than one inheritance model.","worked":["Under complete autosomal recessive inheritance, an unaffected parent has an affected aa child. Infer the parent’s genotype and justify.","The affected child must receive a from each parent. The parent is unaffected, excluding aa under complete penetrance. The parent must therefore be Aa under the stated model, rather than assuming every unaffected relative is AA."],"workedMore":[["A pedigree shows two unaffected parents with an affected child. Explain a compatible recessive model and identify an assumption.","Two Aa parents can each contribute a to produce an aa affected child. The pattern is compatible with autosomal recessive inheritance. The inference uses the specified complete expression and accurate phenotype assumptions; it is not an unconditional exclusion of every biological alternative."],["For Aa × Aa parents, an unaffected sibling is considering a recessive carrier probability. Calculate it.","The offspring ratio is AA:Aa:aa = 1:2:1. Excluding the affected aa category leaves AA:Aa = 1:2. Carrier probability given unaffected status is 2/3 under the model."]],"practice":[["Explain why an unaffected person unrelated to an affected parent–child transmission cannot automatically be labelled AA.","Unaffected phenotype can correspond to AA or Aa in a complete recessive model. The pedigree may lack evidence that distinguishes those possibilities. Use an uncertainty notation or both possible genotypes until additional relationship or test evidence resolves it.",6],["Explain a limitation of using current phenotype alone in a Huntington-disease pedigree.","The condition can have age-dependent onset. A younger person without current symptoms may still carry a disease-associated allele. Genotype inference requires the task’s age and penetrance assumptions or additional testing evidence.",6],["Evaluate one benefit and two considerations for genetic screening of an asymptomatic person.","A result can support informed planning or appropriate follow-up. Informed consent and confidentiality are needed because results can affect the person and relatives. Uncertainty, anxiety and possible misuse must be considered; a screening result does not always mean inevitable disease.",6]],"quiz":["What does an affected aa child imply about two unaffected parents under the stated simple recessive model?",["Both are Aa","Both must be AA","Both must be aa","Neither supplied a"],0,"Each parent supplied a and each remains unaffected, making both heterozygous under complete penetrance and no new mutation."],"quizzes":[["A carrier mother and unaffected father have an X-linked-recessive model. What is risk conditional on a son?",["1/2","1/4","3/4","0"],0,"Half the sons receive the mother’s affected allele; among all offspring the affected-son outcome is one quarter under the stipulated sex probabilities."],["Does a dominant allele have to be common?",["No, dominance concerns heterozygote phenotype","Yes, always over 50%","Yes, always over 90%","Only if it is on an autosome"],0,"Dominance describes expression relationships and does not by itself determine population frequency or fitness."],["Can an unshaded individual be a recessive carrier?",["Yes, under a recessive model.","No, shading contains every allele.","Only if the person has no chromosomes.","Only in plant pedigrees."],0,"Yes, under a recessive model."],["What is the strongest pedigree justification?",["A specific relationship linked to the model and stated assumptions.","Only counting the diagram’s colours.","Assuming every small pedigree proves one unique model.","Assigning all unaffected people homozygous genotypes without evidence."],0,"A specific relationship linked to the model and stated assumptions."]],"images":["y11-x-linked","y11-recessive-inheritance","y11-pedigree"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"reproductive-anatomy","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"11 · Reproductive anatomy and gamete pathways","goal":"Trace sperm and oocyte pathways and relate reproductive structures to function.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["Testes and seminiferous tubules","Testes contain seminiferous tubules where germ cells undergo processes leading to sperm production. Sertoli cells provide support within the tubules; they are somatic support cells, not products formed by primary spermatocytes becoming a new cell type. Interstitial Leydig cells contribute testosterone production. These structures have distinct roles in a coordinated system. The scrotum contributes to a temperature environment suitable for normal sperm production, rather than acting as a storage site for urine."],["Sperm transport and accessory glands","Sperm develop functional properties during passage through the epididymis and can be transported through the vas deferens and associated ducts towards the urethra. Accessory glands contribute seminal fluid that supports transport and the chemical environment. Sperm are cells, while semen is the mixture of sperm and secretions; the terms are not interchangeable. Urinary and reproductive pathways share parts of the male urethral route, but coordinated physiology prevents normal simultaneous passage of urine and semen."],["Ovaries and oocyte transport","Ovaries contain follicles in which oocytes develop, and contribute reproductive hormone production. Ovulation releases an oocyte from a follicle towards the uterine-tube pathway. Fertilisation commonly occurs within the uterine tube, not normally in the vagina or bladder. The tube then supports transport towards the uterus. A labelled diagram should distinguish ovary, uterine tube, uterus and cervix, rather than use “womb” to represent every internal structure."],["Uterus, cervix and vagina","The uterus has a muscular wall and an endometrial lining that changes through reproductive cycles and supports implantation under suitable conditions. The cervix is its lower opening region and produces mucus whose properties change with hormonal conditions. The vagina connects the cervical region with the exterior and serves as part of the birth canal. External structures collectively form the vulva; vulva and vagina are different anatomical terms. Structure–function answers should use accurate names rather than treat every passage as the same organ."],["Gamete structures and limitations","A sperm has a haploid nucleus, a head region with an acrosome, a midpiece rich in mitochondria and a flagellum supporting movement. An oocyte is much larger, contains a haploid contribution at the appropriate stage and has substantial cytoplasmic resources and protective surroundings. Size and shape relate to roles, but neither gamete alone is a complete embryo. A simple diagram may omit the precise meiotic stage; identify that limitation when chromosome accounting matters."],["Pathways link structure and fertility","Fertility depends on more than production of gametes: transport, hormonal coordination, uterine conditions and many other factors contribute. Blocked uterine tubes or interrupted sperm ducts can impair meeting of gametes without necessarily eliminating hormone production. A structure damaged in one pathway therefore does not automatically prevent every other function of the gonad. Course explanations analyse mechanisms and supplied evidence, rather than diagnose an individual from one anatomical diagram."],["Relate sperm production to delivery","Seminiferous tubules within testes support sperm production, while supporting cells and the local environment contribute to developing germ cells. Sperm pass into the epididymis for maturation and storage-related functions, then travel through the vas deferens and associated ducts towards the urethra during ejaculation. Accessory secretions contribute fluid and substances supporting transport and function. The scrotal arrangement helps maintain an environment suited to production. A vasectomy interrupts a transport route rather than removing all testosterone-producing tissue or stopping the formation of every sperm cell."],["Relate the ovarian and uterine routes","An ovary contains follicles supporting developing oocytes and endocrine activity. After ovulation, the secondary oocyte can enter a uterine tube, where fertilisation commonly occurs if viable sperm are present. Cilia and muscular activity contribute to movement towards the uterus. The endometrium changes across the cycle and can support implantation of an appropriately developed embryo. The cervix connects uterus and vagina and has changing mucus properties. Distinguish ovaries, uterine tubes and uterus: producing a gamete, providing a fertilisation route and supporting implanted development are different functions."]],"mis":"The urethra is not the uterine tube. Trace each gamete through the correct named structures.","worked":["Trace the main sperm route from production towards the exterior and distinguish sperm from semen.","Sperm arise within seminiferous tubules, then pass through connected pathways towards the epididymis. Transport continues through vas deferens and associated ducts towards urethra and exterior. Accessory secretions combine with sperm to form semen; sperm cells and the total fluid mixture are not identical."],"workedMore":[["Identify the structures linking ovulation, common fertilisation location and implantation.","Ovulation releases an oocyte from an ovarian follicle towards the uterine tube. Fertilisation commonly occurs in the tube if suitable sperm and conditions are present. The developing conceptus travels towards the uterus, where implantation normally involves the endometrium."],["Correct: “Sertoli cells are made when primary spermatocytes finish meiosis.”","Primary spermatocytes are germ-line cells whose meiotic products contribute to sperm formation. Sertoli cells are somatic support cells associated with developing germ cells. Support and germ-line lineages must not be confused; meiosis does not turn primary spermatocytes into Sertoli cells."]],"practice":[["Explain why a blocked reproductive duct can reduce fertility without necessarily stopping gonadal hormone production.","Gamete transport can be interrupted so gametes do not reach the necessary meeting location. Hormone-producing tissue may remain functional despite that mechanical obstruction. Transport and endocrine functions are distinct even though both contribute to reproduction.",6],["Relate sperm head, midpiece and flagellum to function.","The head carries a haploid nuclear contribution and has an acrosomal region relevant to fertilisation interactions. The midpiece contains mitochondria supporting energy supply. The flagellum supports movement; the sperm is not a whole embryo before fusion and development.",6],["Distinguish uterus, cervix, vagina and vulva.","The uterus has muscular and endometrial structures supporting reproductive processes; the cervix is its lower opening region. The vagina is a passage connecting the cervical region with the exterior. The vulva comprises external genital structures rather than being another name for the internal vaginal passage.",6]],"quiz":["Where does sperm production occur?",["Seminiferous tubules of the testes.","The urinary bladder.","Only the prostate lumen.","The uterine cervix."],0,"Seminiferous tubules of the testes."],"quizzes":[["What is the role of Sertoli cells?",["Somatic support of developing germ cells within tubules.","They arise as sperm products of primary spermatocytes.","They are all mature sperm tails.","They produce bile."],0,"Somatic support of developing germ cells within tubules."],["Where does fertilisation commonly occur?",["A uterine tube.","The vagina in every normal case.","The urinary bladder.","The uterine endometrium after birth."],0,"A uterine tube."],["How do sperm and semen differ?",["Sperm are cells; semen includes sperm and glandular secretions.","They are identical terms for hormones.","Semen contains only chromosomes without fluid.","Sperm are epithelial organs."],0,"Sperm are cells; semen includes sperm and glandular secretions."],["What is the endometrium?",["The changing inner lining of the uterus.","The outer layer of every sperm.","The tube from kidney to bladder.","A pulmonary valve."],0,"The changing inner lining of the uterus."]],"images":["y11-male-reproduction","y11-female-reproduction","y11-testis"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"gametogenesis","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"12 · Spermatogenesis and oogenesis","goal":"Link meiotic stages, timing and unequal division to human gamete production.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["From germ cell to sperm","Spermatogenesis proceeds from dividing germ-line precursors through primary spermatocytes, meiotic divisions and haploid spermatids, which differentiate into sperm. Normal meiosis reduces chromosome complement, while subsequent differentiation changes form and function without another complete meiotic reduction. A schematic four-product model is useful for chromosome outcomes. Somatic support cells such as Sertoli cells assist the process but are not themselves the normal haploid gamete products."],["Spermiogenesis changes structure","Haploid spermatids acquire the specialised organisation of sperm through spermiogenesis. Changes include nuclear condensation, acrosomal development, flagellum formation and rearrangement of cytoplasmic components. This is differentiation rather than a third meiotic division. Mature functional properties continue developing in the reproductive tract. Do not infer that every sperm shown in a testis diagram already has all the same motility characteristics as ejaculated sperm."],["Oogenesis begins before birth","Human primary oocytes begin meiosis before birth and enter a prolonged arrested stage. Following puberty, selected follicles support further development in cycles. Typically, the cell ovulated is a secondary oocyte arrested in meiosis II, with completion associated with fertilisation. The exact terminology matters when a question asks about stage rather than using “egg” loosely. The process is not the continuous production of four equal new ova from every primary oocyte after puberty."],["Unequal cytokinesis","Oogenesis allocates most cytoplasm to one large developing gamete, while smaller polar bodies receive other meiotic chromosome sets. This preserves resources in the functional oocyte while accomplishing chromosome reduction. The distinction links cell division with later development rather than suggesting polar bodies become four identical embryos. Both spermatogenesis and oogenesis use meiosis, but timing, cytoplasmic allocation and functional product number differ."],["Chromosome and DNA accounting","A typical primary human meiotic cell after replication has 46 chromosomes arranged as duplicated units. After meiosis I, a secondary cell normally has 23 duplicated chromosomes. After meiosis II, the separated chromatids provide 23 chromosomes in each haploid product. DNA amount changes across these stages, so “23 chromosomes” does not by itself say whether each is duplicated. Always identify stage and count by the relevant centromeric convention."],["Variation and errors","Crossing over and independent assortment contribute to gamete variation, while segregation errors can alter chromosome number. Rates and patterns depend on biological context; a classroom comparison should not claim that every older gamete has an error or that every conception follows a perfectly synchronised model. Karyotypes and genetic analyses provide different kinds of evidence. Explain a potential risk mechanism using the supplied data rather than turning general biology into a personal prediction."],["Compare timing and products, not only chromosome number","Spermatogenesis begins from diploid germ-cell lineages and, after meiosis and differentiation, can generate large numbers of specialised haploid sperm. In oogenesis, primary oocytes begin meiosis before birth and remain arrested until later developmental events. A selected oocyte completes the first meiotic division with unequal cytoplasmic allocation, producing a secondary oocyte and a polar body. The second division is normally completed in association with fertilisation. Consequently, the two pathways differ in onset, continuity, arrest stages, cytoplasmic allocation and functional products despite both involving reduction to haploid sets."],["Account for chromosomes and DNA at each stage","Before replication, a diploid human germ cell has 46 chromosomes. After replication, it still has 46 chromosomes by the centromere convention but has twice the DNA amount, with two sister chromatids per chromosome. After meiosis I, each resulting nucleus has 23 duplicated chromosomes; after meiosis II, a normal gamete has 23 chromosomes with one chromatid each. Homologues separate first and sister chromatids separate second. Nondisjunction at either stage can produce abnormal chromosome combinations, and subsequent fertilisation can alter zygote dosage. State the stage and counting convention before comparing diagrams."]],"mis":"Oogenesis does not produce four equivalent functional ova from each primary oocyte. Its meiotic cytokinesis is unequal.","worked":["Compare spermatogenesis and oogenesis in division outcome and timing.","Both involve meiotic reduction and processes generating haploid genetic contributions. Spermatogenesis can yield four haploid spermatid products with subsequent differentiation; oogenesis retains one large functional product and polar bodies. Oogenesis includes prolonged arrests beginning before birth, whereas sperm production follows a different postpubertal ongoing pattern."],"workedMore":[["Give chromosome counts after replication, after meiosis I and after meiosis II for normal human gamete production.","The replicated primary cell has 46 chromosomes with sister chromatids. After meiosis I each secondary cell has 23 duplicated chromosomes. After meiosis II each haploid product has 23 chromosomes with sisters separated; chromosome number and DNA amount are distinct."],["Explain the functional benefit of unequal oogenic cytokinesis.","Chromosome sets are distributed through meiosis. Most cytoplasm and cellular resources remain in one large developing oocyte. Polar bodies receive smaller cytoplasmic portions, so haploid outcomes do not mean four equivalent functional eggs."]],"practice":[["Why is forming a sperm flagellum not another round of meiosis?","Flagellum formation is a structural differentiation process in the haploid product. It does not ordinarily halve the chromosome complement again. Meiosis changes chromosome distribution, while spermiogenesis establishes specialised sperm features.",6],["Explain why “an ovulated egg has completed every meiotic step” is imprecise in humans.","The ovulated cell is typically a secondary oocyte. It is arrested in meiosis II rather than having completed that division beforehand. Completion is associated with fertilisation, so stage-specific terminology matters for precise questions.",6],["Explain two ways meiosis contributes to genetically varied gametes.","Crossing over exchanges corresponding regions between nonsister chromatids of homologues. Independent assortment produces alternative combinations of maternal and paternal homologues. These rearrange combinations, while mutation can originate new sequence variants.",6],["Compare chromosome and DNA accounting immediately after replication and after meiosis I.","After replication, a typical diploid human cell has 46 duplicated chromosomes with two chromatids each. After meiosis I each nucleus normally has 23 duplicated chromosomes. Homologue separation reduces the set number; chromosome number is counted by centromeres.",4]],"quiz":["What is spermiogenesis?",["Differentiation of spermatids into specialised sperm.","A third complete meiotic division.","Production of Sertoli cells from sperm.","Formation of bile."],0,"Differentiation of spermatids into specialised sperm."],"quizzes":[["What is typically ovulated in humans?",["A secondary oocyte arrested in meiosis II.","Four fully equal ova from one follicle.","A diploid sperm.","A blastocyst before fertilisation."],0,"A secondary oocyte arrested in meiosis II."],["Why is oogenic cytokinesis unequal?",["It retains most cytoplasm in one functional developing gamete.","It makes every product identical in size.","It prevents chromosome reduction.","It creates all support cells from the oocyte."],0,"It retains most cytoplasm in one functional developing gamete."],["How many chromosomes does a normal human secondary meiotic cell have after meiosis I?",["23, ordinarily still duplicated.","46 solely because sisters remain together.","92 unduplicated chromosomes.","Zero."],0,"23, ordinarily still duplicated."],["What do both gametogenic processes share?",["Meiotic reduction of chromosome complement.","Four equally functional ova.","Identical developmental timing.","No genetic variation."],0,"Meiotic reduction of chromosome complement."]],"images":["y11-spermatogenesis","y11-oogenesis"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]],"tables":[{"title":"Spermatogenesis and oogenesis","headers":["Feature","Spermatogenesis","Oogenesis"],"rows":[["Timing","Production from puberty in the useful model","Begins before birth with meiotic arrest"],["Cytoplasmic allocation","More even across meiotic products","Unequal; most cytoplasm retained in one main cell"],["Functional products","Four sperm-lineage cells per completed meiotic sequence","One large functional gamete with polar-body products"],["Specialisation","Small motile cells after spermiogenesis","Large cytoplasmic contribution to early development"],["Meiosis II completion","Before mature sperm function","Normally associated with fertilisation"]]}]},{"id":"reproductive-hormones","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"13 · Reproductive hormones and menstrual cycles","goal":"Explain hypothalamic–pituitary–gonadal coordination and interpret ovarian and uterine changes.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["A coordinated hormonal axis","The hypothalamus releases GnRH signals that influence anterior-pituitary secretion of FSH and LH. These hormones act on gonadal tissues, which produce hormones and participate in feedback regulation. The system links brain signalling, gamete development and reproductive tissue changes. It is not one gland releasing every hormone directly into the uterus. Trace the origin, target and effect of a hormone separately; naming a hormone without its causal role is insufficient for an explanatory answer."],["Regulation in testes","FSH supports Sertoli-cell-associated processes relevant to sperm development, while LH stimulates Leydig-cell testosterone production. Testosterone supports reproductive and other functions and contributes to negative feedback on the axis. Inhibin contributes feedback particularly relevant to FSH regulation. These hormones coordinate supporting tissues and germ-cell development rather than becoming chromosomes inside sperm. Feedback changes signalling; it does not imply that a gonad intentionally decides a future offspring’s genotype."],["Follicular development","During the follicular phase, FSH supports follicular development and developing follicles contribute oestrogen production. The endometrium rebuilds and proliferates under changing hormonal conditions. Several follicles may begin developing, but a dominant follicle commonly becomes prominent. Cycle length and timing vary between individuals and cycles. A standard 28-day graph is a teaching example, not proof that every ovulation occurs on day 14 or that calendar predictions guarantee avoidance of pregnancy."],["The ovulatory positive-feedback episode","In suitable conditions, sustained high oestrogen near the preovulatory period contributes to positive feedback and an LH surge. The surge supports final follicular changes and ovulation. This is an important exception to the claim that reproductive regulation consists only of negative feedback. An exam answer should link sustained hormonal conditions → altered feedback → LH surge → ovulation, rather than list four events with no relationship."],["Corpus luteum and secretory phase","After ovulation, follicular tissue forms the corpus luteum, producing progesterone and oestrogen. These hormones support a secretory endometrium suitable for implantation and contribute to feedback that limits further cycle stimulation. If pregnancy does not develop, the corpus luteum regresses and hormone levels fall. If implantation-associated pregnancy proceeds, embryonic signalling including hCG supports continued corpus-luteum function initially. The ovary and uterus undergo linked but distinct changes."],["Menstruation and cycle restart","A fall in supporting ovarian hormone levels leads to shedding of the functional endometrial layer, producing menstruation in the nonpregnant cycle. This is not the entire uterus leaving the body and is not simply an unfertilised oocyte producing all menstrual material. With reduced negative feedback, hormonal conditions allow a new follicular phase. Treat ovarian events, endometrial changes and measured hormone curves as coordinated evidence rather than interchangeable labels."],["Reading graphs and fertility timing","Identify axes, units and the timing of peaks before naming phases. An LH peak, rising progesterone after ovulation and later hormone decline can support a coherent interpretation. Sperm can survive for several days, up to about five under favourable reproductive-tract conditions, while an ovulated oocyte has a shorter fertilisable interval, commonly around 12–24 hours. Individual variation means a single calendar day does not define a universally safe or fertile interval. These principles explain limitations of timing-based contraception without giving a personal clinical prediction."],["Read ovarian and uterine cycles together","During the follicular phase, FSH supports follicle development and the growing follicle contributes increasing oestrogen. Oestrogen supports proliferative changes in the endometrium. A sustained appropriate pre-ovulatory hormonal context can generate positive feedback and an LH surge, associated with ovulation. After ovulation, the corpus luteum contributes progesterone and oestrogen, supporting secretory endometrial conditions and feedback regulation. If pregnancy is not established, luteal function declines, hormone levels fall and menstruation can follow. Ovarian events and uterine events are coordinated cycles, not identical anatomical changes."],["Pregnancy signalling changes the usual cycle outcome","After implantation begins, trophoblast-related hCG signalling supports corpus-luteum function during early pregnancy, maintaining hormonal support rather than allowing the ordinary non-pregnant decline. Placental hormone production contributes increasingly as pregnancy progresses. A hormone concentration graph must be interpreted in this physiological context. An LH peak is not evidence of an implanted pregnancy, and a fixed cycle diagram does not guarantee a universal fertile date. Compare the sequence, feedback relationships and biological outcome rather than assuming one hormone controls every reproductive event independently."]],"mis":"Ovulation is not menstruation. A nominal day-14 model does not predict the ovulation date of every cycle.","worked":["Explain the hormonal causal sequence leading towards ovulation in the standard cycle model.","Developing follicles contribute rising oestrogen. Sustained high preovulatory oestrogen under suitable conditions produces positive feedback and an LH surge. The LH surge supports final follicular changes and ovulation; it is not an example of only negative feedback."],"workedMore":[["Explain endometrial shedding when pregnancy does not develop.","The corpus luteum regresses in the nonpregnant pathway. Progesterone and oestrogen support declines. The functional endometrial layer breaks down and is shed; this is not loss of the whole uterus."],["Explain distinct roles of FSH, LH and testosterone in the reproductive axis.","FSH supports Sertoli-associated processes relevant to spermatogenesis. LH stimulates Leydig-cell testosterone production. Testosterone supports relevant functions and contributes feedback, coordinating rather than replacing these distinct cell roles."]],"practice":[["A graph shows an LH surge followed by a sustained progesterone rise. Explain a compatible ovarian sequence.","The LH surge supports ovulation and associated follicular changes. The corpus luteum subsequently contributes progesterone. The rise is consistent with the luteal phase, subject to the graph’s timing and measurement assumptions.",6],["Explain why a fixed day-14 rule does not reliably identify the fertility interval for every person.","Cycle lengths and ovulation timing vary. Sperm survival can extend over several days under favourable conditions, while oocyte survival is shorter. A single assumed date ignores these biological variations and cannot guarantee a personal fertile or nonfertile interval.",6],["Compare negative feedback and the preovulatory positive-feedback episode.","Negative feedback tends to reduce upstream stimulation when appropriate downstream hormone signals rise. Sustained high preovulatory oestrogen can temporarily promote upstream signalling and an LH surge. The system therefore has phase-dependent regulation rather than one unchanging direction of response.",6]],"quiz":["What stimulates Leydig-cell testosterone production?",["LH.","Bile salts.","Insulin as the direct gonadotropin.","Pepsin."],0,"LH."],"quizzes":[["What commonly supports ovulation?",["An LH surge.","Permanent absence of all pituitary hormones.","A rise in urinary urea alone.","Disappearance of every follicle before development."],0,"An LH surge."],["Which tissue forms after ovulation from follicular tissue?",["The corpus luteum.","A pulmonary alveolus.","The placenta immediately in every cycle.","A renal glomerulus."],0,"The corpus luteum."],["What supports the secretory endometrium after ovulation?",["Progesterone with other coordinated hormonal effects.","Only salivary amylase.","The entire chromosome count doubling.","Only platelet nuclei."],0,"Progesterone with other coordinated hormonal effects."],["Is day 14 a universal ovulation rule?",["No; cycle timing varies.","Yes, regardless of cycle length.","Yes, because an LH graph fixes every individual.","Only in every pregnancy."],0,"No; cycle timing varies."]],"images":["y11-menstrual-cycle","y11-testis"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]],"tables":[{"title":"Hormones: source, target and role","headers":["Signal / source","Principal target or effect","Cycle connection"],"rows":[["FSH / anterior pituitary","Follicular support; Sertoli-cell roles in testes","Gamete-supporting functions"],["LH / anterior pituitary","Ovulation/luteal functions; testosterone-producing cells","Pre-ovulatory surge under appropriate feedback"],["Oestrogens / follicles and other sources","Endometrial and feedback effects","Follicular proliferation; feedback depends on context"],["Progesterone / corpus luteum, later placenta","Secretory endometrial support; feedback","Post-ovulation and pregnancy support"],["Testosterone / testes","Spermatogenic support and reproductive development","Feedback to hypothalamic–pituitary pathways"],["hCG / trophoblast-related tissues","Supports early corpus luteum","Changes the non-pregnant decline pathway"]]}]},{"id":"fertilisation","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"14 · Fertilisation, cleavage and implantation","goal":"Trace the early conceptus and explain chromosome restoration and changes before implantation.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["Meeting of gametes","Fertilisation commonly occurs in a uterine tube when a suitable sperm reaches the oocyte and their interactions proceed. Sperm undergo functional preparation in the reproductive tract and interact with the oocyte’s surrounding layers. This is a sequence of biological processes, not simply any sperm touching the outside of an ovary. Many gametes never contribute to fertilisation, and the availability of gametes does not alone guarantee conception. Use anatomy and timing together in a causal explanation."],["Fusion and prevention of multiple entry","Successful fertilisation involves sperm–oocyte membrane interactions and fusion of haploid genetic contributions. Changes in the oocyte and its surrounding environment help reduce entry of additional sperm, supporting a normal chromosome complement. Failure to prevent multiple genetic contributions can disrupt development. The precise mechanisms are more complex than a cartoon door closing; the model explains why controlled contribution matters, not every biochemical interaction at the surface."],["Diploidy is restored","The maternal and paternal haploid contributions combine to establish a diploid zygote under the normal model, with 46 human chromosomes. The combination creates a new genotype from parental allele contributions. Fertilisation does not make every maternal allele dominant or guarantee a phenotype midway between the parents. The secondary oocyte normally completes meiosis II in association with fertilisation. Track chromosome complement separately from the subsequent repeated rounds of DNA replication and cell division."],["Cleavage without proportionate enlargement","Early mitotic divisions, called cleavage, increase the number of cells while the overall conceptus does not initially enlarge in proportion to that number. The smaller cells are blastomeres, and development progresses through a compact morula towards a blastocyst. A diagram with many cells does not necessarily represent a physically larger object than the starting zygote. Cleavage changes cellular organisation and provides material for developmental pathways; it is not another meiotic production of gametes."],["Blastocyst organisation","A blastocyst contains an inner cell mass and an outer trophoblast-associated layer around a cavity. These regions contribute differently to later development, with the inner cell mass contributing to embryonic tissues and trophoblast contributing to implantation-associated and extraembryonic structures. Do not label every outer cell as already a fully formed placenta or every inner cell as already a specialised organ. Early structures provide developmental precursors rather than miniature complete body systems."],["Implantation and signalling","Implantation normally involves interaction of the developing blastocyst with a suitably prepared uterine endometrium. Trophoblast-associated hCG signalling supports early corpus-luteum function, helping sustain hormonal conditions before later placental contributions become prominent. Fertilisation and implantation are therefore different events at different usual locations. Failure of either stage can affect pregnancy establishment. A conceptus implanting outside the usual uterine location requires medical assessment; classroom explanation identifies the anatomical difference rather than offers management advice."],["Trace recognition, fusion and restoration of diploidy","Sperm must reach and interact with the oocyte’s surrounding structures under suitable conditions. Capacitation-related changes and acrosomal processes contribute to fertilisation competence and access. Fusion triggers changes helping prevent polyspermy and supports completion of the oocyte’s meiotic sequence. Maternal and paternal haploid contributions establish the diploid genetic complement of the zygote. Fertilisation is therefore more than one sperm entering an empty shell: it involves specialised cells, recognition, membrane events, chromosome contributions and activation of development. The oocyte supplies most early cytoplasm and organelles."],["Separate early growth, cleavage and implantation","Cleavage partitions the early embryo into more cells without a proportionate increase in its overall size. A morula develops before the blastocyst, which contains an inner cell mass and an outer trophoblast-related layer surrounding a cavity. Hatching from the surrounding coat and interaction with a receptive endometrium precede progressive implantation. The inner cell mass contributes the embryo proper, while trophoblast-related tissues contribute to placental support. The sequence links cell division, differentiation and maternal conditions; success at fertilisation does not guarantee that every subsequent developmental step succeeds."]],"mis":"Fertilisation usually occurs in a uterine tube; implantation occurs later in the uterine lining.","worked":["Compare normal locations and main events.","Fertilisation commonly occurs in a uterine tube and involves gamete interactions and combination of genetic contributions. Implantation involves the developing blastocyst interacting with uterine endometrium. They are distinct stages; fertilisation does not mean immediate implantation at the same location."],"workedMore":[["A diagram shows one large cell becoming many smaller cells of similar total volume. Explain the process.","Early mitotic cleavage increases cell number. Division partitions the initial cytoplasm, so overall size does not initially increase in proportion to cell count. This produces blastomeres and supports progression towards morula and blastocyst stages, not new gametes."],["Explain how embryonic signalling helps maintain early endometrial support.","Trophoblast-associated hCG supports early corpus-luteum function. The corpus luteum continues contributing progesterone and other hormones. These help sustain endometrial conditions rather than following the ordinary nonpregnant hormone-decline pathway."]],"practice":[["Explain why a normal zygote has 46 chromosomes despite each parental gamete contribution being haploid.","Each contribution normally supplies 23 chromosomes. Their combination restores the diploid complement. This is not another pre-fertilisation replication within each gamete that turns both individually diploid.",6],["Distinguish inner cell mass and trophoblast roles without labelling them as already complete organs.","The inner cell mass contributes to embryonic tissue development. The outer trophoblast-associated region contributes to implantation and extraembryonic structures. They are developmental precursors with different roles, not fully specialised organ systems at this stage.",6],["Explain why mechanisms limiting additional sperm entry matter.","Normal development requires controlled combination of maternal and paternal chromosome contributions. Additional sperm genetic contribution can create an abnormal complement. Post-fertilisation changes help limit this risk; the mechanism is more than an arbitrary competition rule.",6]],"quiz":["What normally restores diploidy?",["Combination of haploid gametic genetic contributions at fertilisation.","A third meiotic division.","Cleavage removing every paternal chromosome.","The gallbladder making sperm."],0,"Combination of haploid gametic genetic contributions at fertilisation."],"quizzes":[["What characterises early cleavage?",["Cell number increases without proportionate initial overall growth.","Four equal sperm are produced.","Every cell undergoes meiosis.","The conceptus always doubles its diameter each division."],0,"Cell number increases without proportionate initial overall growth."],["What is a blastocyst component?",["An inner cell mass and an outer trophoblast-associated layer.","Only mature bones.","Only four sperm tails.","A fully formed fetal lung."],0,"An inner cell mass and an outer trophoblast-associated layer."],["Where does implantation normally occur?",["The uterine endometrium.","The urinary bladder.","The testicular epididymis.","The bronchial epithelium."],0,"The uterine endometrium."],["What contributes to maintaining early corpus-luteum activity in pregnancy?",["hCG signalling.","Pepsin directly.","Absence of every hormone.","Only maternal platelets."],0,"hCG signalling."]],"images":["y11-cleavage","y11-implantation"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"development","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"15 · Embryonic development and placental exchange","goal":"Explain germ layers, developmental support and the separate maternal and fetal circulation paths.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["Gastrulation establishes germ layers","During gastrulation, cell movements and differentiation establish ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm. These germ layers contribute to different embryonic tissues. Ectoderm contributes structures including epidermis and nervous tissues; mesoderm contributes structures including muscle, connective tissues and much of the cardiovascular system; endoderm contributes epithelial components of digestive and respiratory systems and associated organs. These are broad lineage relationships, not a claim that an entire complex organ always arises from only one layer."],["Organogenesis and coordination","Subsequent development organises tissues into functioning systems through changing signals, cell behaviour and expression patterns. Cells do not simply enlarge into completed organs independently of their neighbours. Timing matters because disruption during a sensitive developmental period can affect particular structures. A timeline is a useful model but normal developmental variation and differences between gestational dating conventions must be considered. State whether a question measures time from fertilisation or from a clinical gestational reference."],["Extraembryonic support","Membranes and associated tissues support protection, exchange and development without all becoming the fetus’s body. The amnion encloses a fluid-containing environment that cushions the developing fetus and supports movement. Chorionic structures contribute to placental organisation. The placenta includes fetal-derived and maternal contributions; it is not simply made from the three embryonic germ layers alone. A diagram should distinguish developing embryo, membranes, maternal uterine tissue and exchange surfaces."],["Maternal and fetal blood remain distinct","Maternal blood and fetal blood normally move through separate pathways at the placental exchange region. Materials cross intervening barriers rather than all maternal blood flowing directly through fetal arteries. Oxygen and nutrients can move towards the fetus, while carbon dioxide and other wastes move towards maternal circulation. Saying the blood “never exchanges any cells under any circumstance” is too absolute; the core model is normally separate circulations with exchange across tissues, not unrestricted mixing."],["Exchange is selective but not absolute protection","Placental transfer depends on molecular properties, transport mechanisms and physiological conditions. Some antibodies, medicines, infectious agents and harmful exposures can cross or influence development. The placenta is not a perfect barrier that guarantees protection from every substance. Nutrition and exposure patterns can affect development through different mechanisms. Educational examples explain potential effects of alcohol, nicotine and other drugs without claiming that any single classroom datum predicts an individual pregnancy outcome."],["Umbilical vessel directions","The umbilical cord normally contains two arteries carrying fetal blood towards the placenta and one vein carrying relatively oxygen-rich blood from placenta towards fetus. This fits the definition of artery as carrying blood away from the heart and vein as returning towards it; oxygen content does not define the vessel name. Label arrows as well as oxygen status. A diagram with red and blue colours alone can encourage the wrong rule if it omits direction."],["Placental signalling","The placenta contributes hormonal signalling as pregnancy develops, supporting maternal and pregnancy physiology. Early hCG supports the corpus luteum, while later placental hormone production contributes to ongoing support. Exchange and endocrine roles are different functions of the same complex structure. A placenta does not pump fetal blood with a separate heart or produce every maternal hormone. Link claims to a named transfer or signalling mechanism rather than describing it only as “feeding the baby”."],["A germ-layer map predicts contributions, not complete organ ownership","Ectoderm contributes to the nervous system and epidermis. Mesoderm contributes to muscle, much connective tissue, blood and cardiovascular structures. Endoderm contributes to epithelial linings of much of the digestive and respiratory tracts and to parts of associated glands. An organ such as the intestine also contains muscle, vessels, nerves and connective tissues, so its complete structure draws on more than one developmental lineage. Use the germ-layer map to explain component origins rather than claiming one layer alone creates every cell in a whole complex organ."],["Placental exchange depends on both surfaces and circulation","Chorionic villi increase the fetal-associated exchange area near maternal blood spaces. Fetal capillaries remain within villous tissues, and the intervening layers create a transport barrier with selective properties. Gradients, membrane permeability and transport processes influence movement, while maternal and fetal blood flow help maintain supply and removal. Oxygen and nutrients reach the fetus; carbon dioxide and nitrogenous wastes can move towards maternal removal pathways. A placenta is therefore an exchange organ and endocrine organ with limits, rather than a perfect filter through which only beneficial substances can pass."]],"mis":"Placental exchange normally occurs across tissues between separate maternal and fetal circulations; it is not unrestricted mixing of their blood.","worked":["Explain oxygen and carbon dioxide transfer without claiming unrestricted blood mixing.","Maternal and fetal blood normally follow separate pathways at exchange surfaces. Oxygen moves towards fetal blood across tissues under suitable gradients and conditions. Carbon dioxide moves towards maternal blood for removal; exchange does not require unrestricted direct mixing."],"workedMore":[["Explain why an umbilical vein can carry relatively oxygen-rich blood.","Vein naming describes blood direction towards the heart, not a universal low-oxygen rule. The umbilical vein returns blood from placental exchange towards the fetus. Umbilical arteries carry fetal blood away towards the placenta and are relatively less oxygenated."],["State broad contributions of the three germ layers and a limit of the model.","Ectoderm contributes epidermis and nervous tissue; mesoderm contributes muscle, connective and cardiovascular tissues. Endoderm contributes epithelial components of digestive and respiratory systems and associated organs. Complex organs can contain tissues from multiple origins; the broad model is not a one-layer-only rule for each organ."]],"practice":[["Evaluate: “The placenta blocks every drug, so maternal exposures cannot affect development.”","Placental transfer is selective and depends on molecular and physiological properties. Some substances cross or otherwise affect developmental conditions. The placenta does not guarantee universal protection; risk depends on exposure and timing rather than the claimed absolute barrier.",6],["A diagram labels the entire placenta as fetal tissue from the three germ layers. Correct the interpretation.","Placental organisation includes fetal-derived chorionic/trophoblast-associated components. Maternal uterine tissues also contribute. It is not simply identical to the embryonic body formed through the three germ layers.",6],["Why must an embryonic-development timeline identify its time origin?","Time from fertilisation and clinical gestational dating use different starting conventions. A numbered week can therefore refer to different developmental timing if the reference is unstated. Use the supplied convention and account for biological variation rather than comparing mismatched week numbers.",6]],"quiz":["Which germ layer contributes extensively to muscle and connective tissues?",["Mesoderm.","Only ectoderm in every case.","Only the trophoblast as an embryonic germ layer.","A red blood cell."],0,"Mesoderm."],"quizzes":[["What best describes normal placental blood flow?",["Separate maternal and fetal pathways with exchange across intervening tissues.","Unrestricted maternal blood flow through every fetal artery.","No transfer of any molecule.","A shared single heart pumping both bodies."],0,"Separate maternal and fetal pathways with exchange across intervening tissues."],["What is the usual umbilical-vessel arrangement?",["Two arteries towards placenta and one vein towards fetus.","Two veins towards placenta and one artery towards fetus.","Only one artery and no vein.","Four bronchi."],0,"Two arteries towards placenta and one vein towards fetus."],["Is the placenta a perfect protective barrier?",["No; transfer and effects depend on substances and conditions.","Yes, against every medicine and infectious agent.","Yes, because oxygen cannot cross.","Only because all blood completely mixes."],0,"No; transfer and effects depend on substances and conditions."],["What contributes to placental structure?",["Both fetal-derived and maternal tissues.","Only the three embryonic germ layers with no maternal contribution.","Only maternal erythrocytes fused into an organ.","Only amniotic fluid."],0,"Both fetal-derived and maternal tissues."]],"images":["y11-germ-layers","y11-placenta"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]],"tables":[{"title":"Germ layers and component contributions","headers":["Layer","Examples","Important limit"],"rows":[["Ectoderm","Epidermis; nervous tissues","An organ contains multiple tissue components"],["Mesoderm","Muscle; blood; connective and cardiovascular tissues","Not every part of every organ is mesodermal"],["Endoderm","Much digestive/respiratory epithelium; glandular components","Supporting muscle, vessels and nerves have other origins"]]}]},{"id":"birth","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"16 · Birth and the transition of fetal circulation","goal":"Explain labour feedback and circulatory changes when lungs replace placental gas exchange.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["Why fetal circulation differs","Before birth, the placenta performs gas exchange and fetal lungs are not ventilated in the postnatal way. Fetal circulation includes pathways that bypass much pulmonary flow and modify blood passage through the liver. The fetal heart still pumps blood, and oxygenated and less oxygenated streams are not perfectly isolated everywhere. A circulation diagram should explain functional routes rather than simply colour the whole fetus uniformly blue or red."],["The three important bypass pathways","The ductus venosus permits some umbilical venous blood to bypass much hepatic circulation. The foramen ovale provides a connection between atria, supporting flow from right atrium towards left under fetal pressure conditions. The ductus arteriosus links pulmonary arterial outflow with the aorta, allowing much flow to bypass the high-resistance fetal pulmonary circuit. These are distinct anatomical structures, not three names for the umbilical vein."],["Labour as coordinated physiology","Labour involves coordinated uterine contractions and changes in the cervix, followed by delivery and later placental expulsion. Hormonal and mechanical signals interact; initiation is more complex than one hormone acting alone at a universally fixed moment. Oxytocin can strengthen uterine contractions during labour. Use a staged model to organise events but recognise that duration and progression vary. A classroom diagram explains processes rather than provides instructions for managing childbirth."],["A positive-feedback loop","Stretch associated with the presenting part of the fetus and cervical changes can promote oxytocin signalling, which strengthens contractions and can increase the relevant mechanical stimulus. This is a positive-feedback relationship during the process. Delivery removes the sustaining stimulus and changes the system. Positive feedback amplifies change rather than directly returning a variable towards a set point; compare it with the negative-feedback loops used for many homeostatic variables."],["Ventilation changes pulmonary flow","With lung expansion and effective breathing, pulmonary vascular resistance falls and blood flow through the lungs increases. Oxygenation now depends on pulmonary exchange. Clamping or separation of the placental pathway removes the previous exchange route and changes circulatory loading. These events alter pressure relationships and help shift the infant towards the postnatal circulation pattern. The transition is a physiological sequence, not simply a diagram in which three holes all close at the same second."],["Functional and anatomical closure","Changing atrial pressures supports functional closure of the foramen ovale, while changes in oxygen and signalling contribute to ductus arteriosus constriction. The ductus venosus also ceases its fetal bypass role as umbilical flow ends. Functional closure and later anatomical remodelling are distinct, with timing varying between pathways and individuals. Do not give one universal one-week closure date for every structure. Persistent abnormal pathways may need assessment, but classroom explanation focuses on mechanisms."],["An integrated comparison","Fetal circulation relies on placental exchange and characteristic bypasses; postnatal circulation relies on ventilated lungs and linked pulmonary/systemic circuits. Umbilical vessels cease their exchange role after separation. The heart chambers and major vessels are not all newly created at birth: existing structures operate under changed resistance and pressure relationships. Link the circulatory transition with the earlier heart and alveolar modules to explain why air entry can change blood-routing function."],["Name the stages of labour by their outcomes","The first stage involves progressive cervical effacement and dilation with coordinated uterine contractions. The second stage extends from full dilation to delivery of the baby. The third stage involves placental separation and expulsion. These are organising stages, not fixed durations applying to every labour. Mechanical stretch and hormonal signalling can reinforce contractions during the process. The physiological explanation should link the changing cervix, uterine force and delivery outcome, while recognising that the onset of labour involves several interacting maternal and fetal signals rather than one simple trigger."],["Trace the circulation transition in the correct direction","Before birth, high pulmonary vascular resistance limits much lung flow, while the placenta provides gas exchange. Effective ventilation lowers pulmonary resistance, increasing pulmonary flow and return to the left atrium. Changed atrial pressure relationships support functional foramen-ovale closure. Oxygen-related and other changes promote ductus-arteriosus constriction, while loss of umbilical flow removes the ductus-venosus bypass function. These events shift flow towards two linked postnatal circuits. The transition concerns pressure, resistance and connection function, not the sudden construction of a new heart at the first breath."]],"mis":"Functional closure of a fetal shunt and later anatomical remodelling are different events. The three shunts do not all close simultaneously.","worked":["Name the three major fetal bypass pathways and explain their roles.","Ductus venosus allows some umbilical venous flow to bypass much liver circulation. Foramen ovale connects atria, supporting fetal right-to-left routing under relevant pressure conditions. Ductus arteriosus connects pulmonary arterial outflow with aorta, bypassing much fetal pulmonary flow."],"workedMore":[["Explain a positive-feedback relationship involving oxytocin and labour.","Relevant stretch and cervical changes promote signalling including oxytocin release. Oxytocin strengthens uterine contraction, which can increase the mechanical stimulus. The loop amplifies the process until delivery changes or removes the sustaining stimulus."],["Explain how lung expansion supports a change from fetal to postnatal routing.","Effective expansion reduces pulmonary vascular resistance. More blood flows through lungs and pulmonary gas exchange becomes established. Changed pressure relationships and cessation of placental flow support closure or loss of fetal bypass function."]],"practice":[["Evaluate: “All fetal shunts permanently seal at exactly the same moment as the first breath.”","Functional changes and anatomical remodelling are different events. Different structures respond through different pressure and signalling mechanisms. Timing varies, so a simultaneous universal permanent-closure claim is inaccurate.",6],["Compare the main gas-exchange route before and after birth.","Before birth, placental exchange supplies oxygen to fetal circulation through umbilical return. After effective breathing begins, pulmonary alveolar exchange becomes the main route. Changing flow resistance and pressure support rerouting through the lungs rather than simply changing the blood’s colour.",6],["Distinguish labour amplification from a negative homeostatic loop.","Labour feedback can strengthen the stimulus and contractions during the process. Negative feedback generally counteracts a deviation from a regulated range. The mechanisms have different response directions and should not be described as the same type merely because both use hormones.",6],["Name the three stages of labour through their defining outcomes.","First: cervical effacement and dilation. Second: delivery of the baby after full dilation. Third: placental separation and expulsion.",3]],"quiz":["Which fetal structure connects the atria?",["The foramen ovale.","The ductus arteriosus as an atrial opening.","The ureter.","The cervix."],0,"The foramen ovale."],"quizzes":[["What does the ductus arteriosus connect?",["Pulmonary arterial outflow and the aorta.","The vagina and bladder.","The two kidneys.","The stomach and trachea."],0,"Pulmonary arterial outflow and the aorta."],["What commonly happens to pulmonary vascular resistance with effective lung expansion?",["It falls, supporting greater pulmonary flow.","It necessarily becomes infinite.","It stays identical in every pathway.","It turns blood into amniotic fluid."],0,"It falls, supporting greater pulmonary flow."],["What type of feedback can strengthen labour contractions?",["Positive feedback.","Only negative feedback reducing every contraction.","DNA replication feedback.","No signalling of any kind."],0,"Positive feedback."],["How do functional and anatomical closure differ?",["Flow can cease before later structural remodelling is complete.","They are always simultaneous in every infant.","Functional closure means the structure never existed.","Anatomical closure occurs before fertilisation."],0,"Flow can cease before later structural remodelling is complete."]],"images":["y11-fetal-circulation","y11-placenta"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]],"tables":[{"title":"Labour stages: organise the physiological sequence","headers":["Stage","Defining outcome","Key structures/events"],"rows":[["First","Cervical effacement and dilation","Coordinated contractions and cervical change"],["Second","Delivery of baby after full dilation","Contractions and movement through birth canal"],["Third","Placental separation and expulsion","Uterine contraction and placental delivery"]]}]},{"id":"contraception","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"17 · Contraception: mechanisms and limitations","goal":"Compare contraceptive mechanisms and evaluate effectiveness using assumptions and evidence.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["Different stages can be targeted","Contraceptive methods reduce the probability of pregnancy through mechanisms affecting gamete production or release, sperm access, fertilisation or other reproductive conditions. They are not all the same chemical and do not all protect against sexually transmitted infections. A useful comparison identifies mechanism, correct-use requirements, limitations and relevant evidence. The course explains biology; personal method choice and medication timing require appropriate clinical information rather than a classroom diagram."],["Barrier methods","Condoms and other barriers reduce sperm passage towards the reproductive tract when used appropriately. Some barriers also reduce exposure to certain sexually transmitted pathogens, but protection is not complete against every infection or every exposure route. Breakage, incorrect use and inconsistent use affect real-world outcomes. A barrier does not ordinarily stop hormone production or change chromosome complement. Link the physical obstruction to reduced likelihood that sperm reach an oocyte."],["Hormonal methods","Hormonal contraceptives use specific regimens that can alter ovulation, cervical mucus and endometrial conditions, with effects depending on the formulation. Feedback-related suppression of gonadotropin signalling can inhibit ovulation for relevant methods. Do not state that every hormonal product works identically or that it guarantees absence of all ovulation in every use circumstance. Hormonal contraception does not itself provide reliable STI protection. The mechanism is regulation of reproductive physiology, not deletion of reproductive genes."],["Intrauterine methods and spermicides","Copper-containing intrauterine devices create local conditions that impair sperm function and fertilisation; hormonal intrauterine systems have formulation-dependent local effects, including cervical-mucus changes and altered endometrial conditions. Spermicide acts locally to impair sperm but has limitations when used alone. It is inaccurate to define all these methods as simply preventing an already implanted embryo from growing. State the particular method and principal supported mechanism rather than impose one universal explanation."],["Sterilisation and transport","Vasectomy interrupts sperm transport through the vasa deferentia; tubal procedures interrupt the pathway through which gametes normally meet. These actions do not necessarily stop gonadal hormone production, and procedures do not all have immediate guaranteed effects or simple reversibility. The course distinction is anatomical transport versus endocrine function. Students should not interpret the word “permanent” as a licence to infer identical clinical outcomes for every technique."],["Fertility awareness and emergency contraception","Fertility-awareness methods depend on recognising reproductive timing and avoiding relevant exposure during potentially fertile intervals, with reliability affected by biological variation and use. Emergency contraception includes different approaches with different mechanisms and clinical timing requirements. Some medicines principally delay or inhibit ovulation; a copper IUD is another clinically used approach under appropriate conditions. Avoid memorising one universal post-exposure time window or claiming every emergency method ends an established implanted pregnancy. These lessons provide mechanisms, not individual dosing guidance."],["Perfect use and typical use","Perfect-use estimates reflect consistent correct use, while typical-use estimates include common errors and inconsistency. Compare figures for the same time period, population and outcome. A yearly pregnancy rate is not automatically a probability for one exposure. Benefits and limitations can involve reversibility, access, adherence, side effects and STI considerations, with evidence and personal circumstances informing clinical decisions. A high effectiveness estimate does not mean zero risk."],["Compare methods through the step they change","Fertility awareness changes the timing of exposure; barriers reduce sperm access; relevant hormonal methods suppress ovulation or alter cervical mucus; spermicides impair sperm locally; sterilisation interrupts a gamete pathway. Copper and hormonal intrauterine devices have distinct mechanisms and use contexts. Emergency contraceptive medicines can delay or inhibit ovulation, while an appropriate intrauterine method provides another clinical pathway. A comparison should state mechanism, use dependence, important limits and whether STI exposure is reduced. Neither a course diagram nor a nominal cycle date supplies personalised instructions for selecting or timing medication."],["Separate effectiveness, risk, benefit and autonomy","An effectiveness percentage needs an outcome, denominator, time period and use definition. Typical-use results include missed or incorrect use, whereas perfect-use results concern consistent correct use. A potential benefit can include a reversible and effective reduction in pregnancy probability; limitations can involve use demands, adverse effects, access or permanence. Ethical evaluation considers informed choice, consent, fair access and cultural perspectives. Assess the specific method and evidence rather than assuming that one approach is biologically and personally suitable for every individual."]],"mis":"Pregnancy prevention and protection against STIs are different outcomes. One contraceptive mechanism does not provide both automatically.","worked":["Compare a condom, a relevant ovulation-suppressing hormonal method and vasectomy.","A condom provides a physical barrier reducing sperm passage. A relevant hormonal method alters signalling and physiology, including inhibition of ovulation under appropriate conditions. Vasectomy interrupts sperm transport, without requiring elimination of gonadal hormone production."],"workedMore":[["Explain why a typical-use annual pregnancy rate cannot be read as a per-exposure probability.","The rate refers to outcomes over a defined period in a population. Frequency, use patterns and biological timing influence accumulated risk. One exposure has a different reference and cannot be assigned the annual percentage without a suitable model."],["Explain why reducing pregnancy risk is not identical to preventing STIs.","Pregnancy prevention targets reproductive events or gamete access. STIs can spread through relevant pathogen exposure routes not eliminated by many contraceptive methods. Some barriers reduce certain exposure risks, but neither pregnancy effectiveness nor barrier use guarantees universal infection prevention."]],"practice":[["Evaluate: “Sterilisation must remove every reproductive hormone.”","Some procedures interrupt gamete transport rather than remove gonads. Hormone-producing tissue can remain functional. Anatomical prevention of gamete meeting and endocrine suppression are different mechanisms.",6],["Why is “all emergency contraception ends an implanted pregnancy” an inaccurate universal description?","Approaches differ and relevant medicines can act principally by delaying or inhibiting ovulation. Copper intrauterine contraception has a distinct supported mechanism and clinical use conditions. Method-specific evidence and timing are needed; one blanket post-implantation claim does not describe them all.",6],["List evidence features needed for a fair effectiveness comparison.","Use the same outcome and time period, such as pregnancy over one year. Identify perfect versus typical use and relevant population conditions. Consider uncertainty, adherence and other benefits or limitations rather than ranking unlike percentages.",6]],"quiz":["What does a barrier principally reduce?",["Sperm passage when used appropriately.","All hormone production permanently.","All pathogens through every route.","The chromosome number of every oocyte."],0,"Sperm passage when used appropriately."],"quizzes":[["What does vasectomy primarily interrupt?",["Sperm transport through the vasa deferentia.","Testosterone production in every case.","Urine filtration.","All blood flow through testes."],0,"Sperm transport through the vasa deferentia."],["Why can typical-use effectiveness differ from perfect use?",["Real-world errors and inconsistent use affect outcomes.","DNA replication stops in typical users.","Perfect use includes every possible error by definition.","The same rate has no time reference."],0,"Real-world errors and inconsistent use affect outcomes."],["Does hormonal contraception itself provide reliable STI protection?",["No.","Yes, against every pathogen.","Only by changing ABO group.","Yes, because gonadotropins are antibiotics."],0,"No."],["Why is a fixed calendar assumption limited?",["Ovulation timing and cycle patterns vary.","All cycles have exactly the same length.","Sperm never survives more than a minute.","Fertilisation occurs only in the bladder."],0,"Ovulation timing and cycle patterns vary."]],"images":["y11-menstrual-cycle","y11-male-reproduction"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]],"tables":[{"title":"Contraception: mechanisms, benefits and limitations","headers":["Method group","Mechanism / potential benefit","Important limitation"],"rows":[["Fertility awareness","Changes timing of exposure; no device or drug required","Cycle variability and use demands affect effectiveness"],["Barrier","Reduces sperm access; some reduce relevant STI exposure","Correct consistent use; incomplete protection"],["Steroid hormone","Formulation-specific ovulation/mucus effects; reversible approaches available","Use/adverse-effect considerations; no reliable STI protection"],["Spermicide","Local impairment of sperm; user-controlled approach","Limited effectiveness alone; no universal STI protection"],["Sterilisation","Interrupts gamete transport; long-term approach","Procedural burden and limited reversibility; no STI protection"],["Emergency pill / appropriate IUD","Method-specific post-coital prevention pathway","Clinical timing and eligibility differ; does not terminate an established implanted pregnancy"]]}]},{"id":"sti","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"18 · Sexually transmitted infections and screening","goal":"Explain pathogen transmission, prevention principles and the limits of symptoms and screening.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["An infection is a pathogen process","Sexually transmitted infections involve pathogens that can spread through particular intimate-contact or fluid-exposure routes, depending on the organism. They include bacterial and viral examples with different biology and management. They are not caused by contraception itself or by a particular blood group. A clear explanation identifies the pathogen category, relevant route and a mechanism reducing that route rather than treating every STI as one identical disease."],["Symptoms do not reliably reveal every infection","Some infections can be present without obvious symptoms, while similar symptoms can have different causes. Absence of symptoms therefore does not establish absence of infection. Screening and appropriate testing provide evidence beyond appearance. A positive screening result may require interpretation or confirmatory assessment depending on the test. Classroom tasks should use anonymised supplied data, not ask students to disclose personal sexual or medical histories."],["Barrier and exposure principles","Correctly used barriers can reduce contact with relevant fluids or covered surfaces for some pathogens. They do not cover every possible skin area or eliminate every transmission route. Reducing exposure opportunities, informed communication and appropriate testing contribute to prevention principles. Avoid claiming that one barrier guarantees complete protection from all infection, or that a method preventing ovulation directly kills pathogens. Mechanistic accuracy matters more than a slogan."],["Vaccination and treatment categories","Vaccination can reduce risk from particular pathogens such as relevant HPV types or hepatitis B, but no single vaccine covers all STIs. Antibiotics act against susceptible bacterial infections, not as universal antiviral medicines. Resistance and appropriate diagnosis matter. Viral infections have different possible prevention and management pathways. These are biological distinctions, not directions to self-select treatment from a lesson; appropriate clinical assessment is required."],["Screening and public health","Screening can identify infection or associated risk in people without obvious symptoms, supporting follow-up and reduced transmission under appropriate programmes. Programmes require suitable tests, consent, confidentiality and consideration of access. Infection prevalence and uptake influence programme outcomes. An effective test is not enough if people cannot access follow-up. Evaluate both scientific evidence and practical conditions rather than equate more testing with automatic elimination of every pathogen."],["Stigma and evidence quality","Accurate education describes routes and prevention without judging individuals. Privacy and respectful language support appropriate help-seeking and reliable public-health communication. Anonymous self-report surveys can have recall and sampling limitations, so interpret percentages carefully. A correlation between a demographic factor and infection prevalence is not by itself a biological cause or justification for stereotyping. Use mechanism and evidence to explain risk, not assumptions about a person."],["Compare pathogen groups through treatment targets","Examples of bacterial STIs include chlamydia and gonorrhoea, while HIV, HPV and herpes infections involve viruses. Treatments depend on pathogen biology; an antibiotic acting on a bacterial target does not automatically treat a viral infection. Some infections can be cured with appropriate treatment, while others can be managed or prevented through different measures. Vaccination can reduce risk from certain relevant pathogens but is not a universal vaccine against every STI. The key learning is pathogen specificity and evidence-based prevention, not a claim that all genital symptoms indicate the same organism."],["Explain why early detection changes outcomes","Some infections remain asymptomatic while still affecting health or being transmitted. Untreated infections can cause complications, including reproductive-tract damage for relevant pathogens, and can affect partners or pregnancy outcomes. Screening and testing seek particular evidence, with accuracy and timing limits. Safe-sex measures reduce relevant exposure, and appropriate testing, treatment and follow-up interrupt additional parts of the transmission chain. Respectful communication avoids attaching moral judgement to infection status. Clinical decisions require current professional advice; the biological argument links exposure, infection, detection and a defined prevention or treatment mechanism."]],"mis":"No symptoms does not establish absence of infection. Screening detects relevant evidence and may require further assessment.","worked":["Explain why relying only on visible symptoms is an inadequate infection-control strategy.","Some infected people have no obvious symptoms. Transmission can still occur through relevant routes. Appropriate testing and prevention measures provide evidence and protection beyond appearance alone."],"workedMore":[["Explain why an antibiotic is not a universal treatment for every STI.","Some STIs involve bacteria while others involve viruses or other pathogens. Antibiotics target susceptible bacterial structures or processes and do not universally target viruses. Diagnosis, resistance and pathogen-specific management are needed; category differences determine the biological rationale."],["Evaluate: “One HPV vaccine prevents every STI and makes testing unnecessary.”","HPV vaccination targets particular HPV types, not all pathogens. Other infections and relevant residual risks can remain. Appropriate screening or testing decisions depend on the programme and clinical context, not the universal claim."]],"practice":[["Explain why barrier use can reduce but not universally eliminate STI transmission.","It can reduce exposure to covered fluids or surfaces when used correctly. Some routes involve areas not completely covered or other exposures. Use errors and pathogen differences mean no blanket guarantee follows.",6],["Why might an accurate screening test have limited impact if follow-up access is poor?","Testing identifies people who may need further assessment or intervention. Without accessible follow-up, results may not lead to effective management or reduced transmission. Programme outcomes depend on the pathway from testing through care, as well as test performance.",6],["A voluntary survey reports a high infection percentage. State two limitations and one improvement.","Voluntary participation can create sampling bias. Self-report can have recall or reporting limitations. A suitable representative sample and reliable anonymised measurement improve inference while protecting confidentiality.",6]],"quiz":["Can an STI be present without obvious symptoms?",["Yes.","No, every infection is visibly obvious.","Only if the person has no immune cells.","Only after DNA disappears."],0,"Yes."],"quizzes":[["What do antibiotics primarily target in this comparison?",["Susceptible bacteria rather than being universal antivirals.","Every virus equally.","Only ovarian hormones.","All human DNA."],0,"Susceptible bacteria rather than being universal antivirals."],["What does HPV vaccination address?",["Particular HPV types, rather than every STI.","Every bacterial and viral infection.","All pregnancy risk.","All inherited alleles."],0,"Particular HPV types, rather than every STI."],["Does preventing ovulation automatically prevent infection transmission?",["No; reproductive and pathogen mechanisms differ.","Yes, because pathogens require an oocyte.","Yes, in every hormonal method.","Only by changing chromosomes."],0,"No; reproductive and pathogen mechanisms differ."],["What is essential in a screening programme?",["Appropriate testing, consent, privacy and follow-up.","Only a large advertisement.","Publication of every participant’s identity.","Assuming no false results can occur."],0,"Appropriate testing, consent, privacy and follow-up."]],"images":["bacteria","virus"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]]},{"id":"art","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"19 · Assisted reproduction and genetic screening","goal":"Explain IVF, ICSI and prenatal or preimplantation tests, and evaluate their limits and ethics.","prior":"Recall DNA, chromosomes, cell structure and probability as a proportion of a stated set of outcomes.","sections":[["Different causes require different approaches","Difficulty establishing pregnancy can arise from gamete production, transport, hormonal, uterine or other interacting factors. Assisted reproductive technology addresses particular stages, but no single method guarantees success or treats every cause. A mechanism-based comparison starts with the affected step and explains how an intervention may help. Course examples use supplied cases without inferring an individual diagnosis or recommending a procedure from a classroom description."],["In vitro fertilisation","IVF involves bringing oocytes and sperm into an appropriate laboratory environment for fertilisation and early development, followed by transfer of a suitable embryo into the uterus under clinical conditions. It can bypass some transport barriers, but implantation and further development still require suitable conditions. It does not mean a complete pregnancy occurs in a test tube. Success depends on multiple factors and may vary across attempts; one transferred embryo does not guarantee one live birth."],["Intracytoplasmic sperm injection","ICSI involves injection of a selected sperm into an oocyte and can address some difficulties associated with sperm access or fertilisation. It is a specialised step within assisted reproduction, not identical to implantation and not a guarantee that every subsequent developmental stage succeeds. It does not automatically remove every genetic risk or make the resulting embryo genetically unrelated to the gamete contributors. Compare the assisted fertilisation step with IVF’s broader laboratory pathway."],["Prenatal testing pathways","Ultrasound supplies structural and developmental information without obtaining the same data as a DNA test. Maternal blood screening can estimate risk or examine relevant fetal-derived signals, depending on the test. Chorionic villus sampling obtains placental-associated tissue, while amniocentesis obtains amniotic fluid containing material that can be tested. Screening and diagnostic procedures differ in purpose and limitations. No single test detects every disorder, and invasive sampling has considerations that must be evaluated clinically."],["Preimplantation genetic testing","In relevant assisted-reproduction settings, sampled embryo material can be tested for specified genetic information before transfer. The test does not necessarily survey or rule out every possible condition, and interpretation depends on the method, sampled cells and target. Selecting among embryos is distinct from editing every gene in an embryo. Explain what is measured and why it may inform a decision, while preserving uncertainty and recognising possible ethical disagreements."],["Benefits, limitations and ethical reasoning","Potential benefits include helping establish pregnancy or providing information about a defined genetic risk. Limitations can involve success rates, physical burden, cost, access and test uncertainty. Ethical considerations include informed consent, embryo handling, privacy, implications for relatives and fairness of access. A strong exam response applies principles to the specific proposal and acknowledges evidence and perspectives; simply stating “it is good” or “it is wrong” does not analyse a decision."],["Interpreting success-rate claims","A clinic’s percentage may refer to fertilisation, implantation, pregnancy or live birth, per cycle or per embryo transfer, and in a selected patient group. These denominators and outcomes are different. Compare equivalent age groups, treatment contexts and time periods, and consider uncertainty. A 60% fertilisation rate does not imply a 60% live-birth rate. A fair biological evaluation follows the complete sequence from gametes through development rather than treating an intermediate event as the final outcome."],["Compare sampling methods and what they reveal","Ultrasound uses sound-based imaging to assess structures and development. Maternal blood screening can examine biochemical or cell-free DNA-related information, depending on the test, and estimates or assesses defined risks. Chorionic villus sampling obtains placental-associated tissue, while amniocentesis samples amniotic fluid containing fetal-derived material. These samples support different analyses and have different limitations; invasive procedures require consideration of procedure-related risk. A normal result for one tested condition does not exclude every genetic, structural or developmental problem, and a positive screening result is not automatically a final diagnosis."],["Distinguish helping fertilisation from selecting or editing embryos","IVF can bring gametes together outside the usual tubal pathway; ICSI assists a particular sperm–oocyte interaction; embryo transfer places an embryo in the uterus. Preimplantation genetic testing examines sampled embryo material for specified information before a transfer decision. These procedures address different stages and do not all modify the inherited DNA sequence. A strong response follows the actual intervention through to its intended outcome, then evaluates success rate, uncertainty, physical burden, consent, embryo handling, privacy and equitable access. Claims must use comparable patient groups and denominators."]],"mis":"Fertilisation, implantation, pregnancy and live birth are different outcomes. Their percentages cannot be compared without their denominators.","worked":["Explain how IVF may help when a uterine-tube transport problem prevents gametes meeting.","Oocytes and sperm can be brought together under suitable laboratory conditions. This bypasses the usual requirement for their meeting within the tube. Embryo transfer, implantation and further development still require suitable conditions, so bypassing one step does not guarantee birth."],"workedMore":[["Distinguish laboratory fertilisation approaches without confusing them with implantation.","IVF provides a laboratory setting for fertilisation and early development. ICSI specifically injects a selected sperm into an oocyte in relevant assisted-reproduction contexts. Neither act is implantation itself, and later developmental success remains uncertain."],["A report states 70% of sampled oocytes fertilised but provides no live-birth data. Explain what can and cannot be concluded.","The stated outcome is fertilisation among the sampled oocytes. It does not provide implantation, pregnancy or live-birth probability. Later stages, patient context and the denominator must be supplied before comparing final treatment success."]],"practice":[["Compare a risk-estimating prenatal screening result with a targeted diagnostic test.","Screening can identify increased likelihood or a need for follow-up. A diagnostic test investigates specified information more directly, with its own scope and accuracy. Neither should be described as detecting every possible condition or eliminating all uncertainty.",6],["Distinguish ultrasound, chorionic villus sampling and amniocentesis by evidence source.","Ultrasound provides imaging of relevant structures and development. Chorionic villus sampling obtains placental-associated tissue for specified testing. Amniocentesis samples amniotic fluid containing relevant material; these are not interchangeable observations.",6],["Evaluate a proposal to offer a targeted preimplantation genetic test for a severe inherited condition.","Potential benefit is information that may reduce the chance of transferring an embryo with the specified condition. Consent, embryo handling, privacy and implications for family members require consideration. Test limitations, cost and equitable access matter; a balanced judgement applies these issues to the defined proposal.",6]],"quiz":["What does IVF principally include?",["Laboratory fertilisation and early development followed by appropriate embryo transfer.","A full pregnancy in a test tube.","Automatic editing of every gene.","Direct transfer of sperm into the lung."],0,"Laboratory fertilisation and early development followed by appropriate embryo transfer."],"quizzes":[["What is ICSI?",["Injection of a selected sperm into an oocyte.","An ultrasound image alone.","Transfer of maternal blood into an embryo.","A universal chromosome cure."],0,"Injection of a selected sperm into an oocyte."],["What does amniocentesis sample?",["Amniotic fluid containing material suitable for relevant testing.","Only air from lungs.","Only prostate secretion.","The entire placenta without any fluid."],0,"Amniotic fluid containing material suitable for relevant testing."],["What must a treatment-success percentage specify?",["Its outcome, denominator and relevant population.","Only a large number with no definition.","The colour of the clinic logo.","Only blood group."],0,"Its outcome, denominator and relevant population."],["Does preimplantation testing exclude every possible genetic condition?",["No; scope and accuracy depend on the particular test.","Yes, all conditions universally.","Only because it replaces all inherited DNA.","Yes, because embryo cells have no chromosomes."],0,"No; scope and accuracy depend on the particular test."]],"images":["y11-ivf","y11-cleavage","y11-placenta"],"references":[["OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e","https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e"],["SCSA Human Biology ATAR Year 11 syllabus","https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1232622/Human-Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF"]],"tables":[{"title":"Prenatal and embryo assessment: compare evidence","headers":["Method","Sample / information","Limit"],"rows":[["Ultrasound","Sound-based structural/developmental images","Not a complete DNA or disease test"],["Maternal blood screening","Biochemical or cell-free DNA-related evidence, depending on test","Screening result needs contextual interpretation"],["Chorionic villus sampling","Placental-associated tissue for specified analysis","Sampling/interpretation issues; invasive procedure"],["Amniocentesis","Amniotic fluid with fetal-derived material","Targets selected analyses; invasive procedure"],["Preimplantation genetic testing","Sampled embryo material before transfer","Does not guarantee absence of every condition"]]}]},{"id":"screening","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"20 · Cancer screening: HPV, mammography and PSA evidence","goal":"Compare named early-detection technologies and distinguish screening evidence from a diagnosis.","prior":"Connect the relevant cell, tissue and organ structures with the process being investigated.","sections":[["Screening identifies people who may need further assessment","Screening examines people in an appropriate defined group who may have no symptoms, seeking evidence associated with disease or increased risk. Diagnostic assessment investigates a particular suspected problem using appropriate follow-up. A screening signal is not itself a complete biological explanation or an inevitable future outcome. False-positive and false-negative results can occur. Evaluation therefore includes what the test measures, which condition it informs, how further assessment works and whether early identification improves meaningful outcomes."],["Cervical screening detects relevant HPV evidence","Australia’s cervical screening test looks for relevant human papillomavirus evidence, because persistent infection with particular HPV types can contribute to cervical cancer. Detecting HPV does not mean that cancer is present or inevitable. The modern test differs from describing every cervical screen simply as a microscope search for cancer cells. Appropriate assessment follows the result and clinical context. The course comparison concerns the measured biological marker and its interpretation, rather than memorising a personal screening schedule."],["Mammography is an imaging method","A mammogram uses X-rays to produce images of breast tissue. Screening images can reveal abnormalities requiring assessment before a person has symptoms. The image is different evidence from a blood biomarker or DNA-related test; further imaging or tissue assessment may be needed. A recalled person does not automatically have cancer, and a normal screen does not guarantee that no future problem can develop. Benefits and harms depend on accuracy, disease behaviour, follow-up and the context of the screening program."],["PSA is a protein signal rather than a cancer verdict","A prostate-specific antigen blood test measures a protein produced by prostate tissue. Levels can rise with prostate cancer but also with non-cancerous prostate conditions. Interpreting a result may involve history, examination, imaging and sometimes biopsy. A raised PSA is therefore a reason for contextual assessment, not a one-to-one identification of malignant cells in the bloodstream. Compare the signal with mammographic structure and HPV-related evidence: each technology measures a different aspect of biology."],["Evaluate error and the consequences of detection","A false positive identifies a possible problem when the target disease is absent; a false negative misses it when present. Predictive interpretation also depends on how common the condition is in the tested group. Further testing can create benefits and burdens, and detecting a very slowly progressing condition may not have the same implication as finding an aggressive one. A balanced evaluation considers useful early treatment, missed disease, unnecessary investigation, uncertainty and informed choice rather than claiming all screening is either perfect or pointless."],["Communicate evidence without personal diagnosis","An educational response names the technology, the measured evidence, a potential benefit and a limitation. A public explanation should define unfamiliar terms and avoid implying that a classroom scenario establishes anyone’s health status. Individual screening decisions depend on current professional guidance and personal circumstances. Scientific communication respects privacy and distinguishes an observed signal from inference. The biological lesson is how technology supplies evidence for decision-making, and why several sources of evidence may be required."]],"worked":["In synthetic teaching data, 10 of 100 tested people are recalled and 3 of those 10 are confirmed to have the target condition. What proportion of recalls are confirmed?","Among the recalled group, 3/10 × 100 = 30% are confirmed. Among all tested people, 3/100 = 3% are confirmed. The denominators answer different questions, and the example does not estimate a real screening program’s performance."],"practice":[["Why is a positive HPV screen not equivalent to a cervical cancer diagnosis?","The test detects relevant viral evidence. Infection can clear and does not inevitably produce cancer; further assessment considers persistence, other findings and clinical context.",3],["Compare the evidence obtained from mammography and PSA testing.","Mammography supplies X-ray images of tissue structure; PSA testing measures a prostate-derived protein in blood. Neither result alone identifies every relevant cancer with certainty, so interpretation and follow-up matter.",4],["Explain a false negative and one consequence.","The test fails to identify the target condition when it is present. A person may not receive the intended early follow-up, showing why sensitivity and appropriate assessment of symptoms or risk still matter.",3]],"quiz":["A raised PSA level establishes which conclusion?",["A prostate-related signal needs contextual interpretation","Prostate cancer is proven in every case","No further tests can add information","The result measures HPV"],0,"PSA can rise in cancer and non-cancerous conditions."],"images":["mitosis","y11-female-reproduction"],"mis":"A screening result is evidence for an assessment pathway, not a guaranteed diagnosis.","references":[["Cancer Australia: prostate diagnosis","https://www.canceraustralia.gov.au/cancer-types/prostate-cancer/how-prostate-cancer-diagnosed"],["Australian Government: cervical screening","https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-cervical-screening-program/getting-a-cervical-screening-test/how-cervical-screening-works"],["Australian Government: breast screening","https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/breastscreen-australia-program/having-a-breast-screen/how-breast-screening-works"]],"tables":[{"title":"Named screening technologies","headers":["Technology","What it measures","Why follow-up matters"],"rows":[["Cervical HPV screening","Relevant viral evidence","Infection is not identical to cervical cancer"],["Mammography","X-ray image of breast structure","An abnormal image is not automatically a malignancy"],["PSA blood test","Prostate-derived protein level","Non-cancerous conditions can also increase the result"]]}]},{"id":"fetal-exposures","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"21 · Fetal development: nutrition, alcohol, nicotine and other drugs","goal":"Explain how exposure timing, placental transfer and developmental processes influence fetal outcomes.","prior":"Connect the relevant cell, tissue and organ structures with the process being investigated.","sections":[["Sensitive periods connect timing with the affected process","Development involves organised proliferation, cell movement, differentiation and tissue interactions. A disturbance can have different effects depending on which processes are occurring, its duration and intensity, and maternal and fetal conditions. Early organ formation and later growth do not represent identical biological tasks. Exposure does not translate into one universal predictable outcome in every pregnancy. A causal explanation identifies the developmental process that may be affected while distinguishing population-level risk from a diagnosis of an individual fetus."],["Nutrition supplies materials and supports development","Maternal nutrition contributes substrates and micronutrients reaching the developing fetus through maternal physiology and placental transport. Folate supports processes important to early neural-tube development, while adequate iron contributes to oxygen-transport capacity. Inadequacy, excess or disrupted absorption can have different consequences. The placenta does not manufacture every required nutrient from nothing, and an adequate energy intake does not guarantee adequacy of every micronutrient. The educational mechanism concerns biological supply and developmental function rather than personalised dose recommendations."],["Alcohol can affect cellular development","Alcohol can cross the placenta and affect developing tissues, including processes important to brain development. Exposure-related effects depend on several interacting factors and are not limited to a visible abnormality at birth. Fetal metabolism and developmental vulnerability differ from adult conditions, so an adult’s immediate appearance does not measure fetal exposure or effect. Risk evidence supports prevention, while a single classroom case cannot establish an individual outcome or assign blame. Distinguish the mechanism, evidence and limit of prediction."],["Nicotine and smoke involve different contributing substances","Nicotine can affect vascular and cellular signalling, while smoke contains additional substances, including carbon monoxide, that can compromise oxygen delivery. Maternal exposure can therefore influence placental and fetal conditions through several routes. These mechanisms are not captured by the claim that smoke simply touches the fetus’s lungs before birth. The placenta is an exchange interface and does not perfectly block every exposure. Consider maternal blood oxygen transport, placental supply and developmental processes as linked components."],["Other drugs differ in their biological targets","Some substances affect receptor signalling, vascular function, metabolism or other processes and can cross the placenta or alter maternal conditions. Illicit substances are not one chemically uniform group, and prescribed medicines also require context-specific evaluation during pregnancy. A biological explanation should name the proposed target and evidence rather than assume that every substance produces the same defect. Clinical decisions balance risks of exposure and untreated conditions; students should not infer medication changes from a classroom mechanism alone."],["Evaluate evidence ethically and communicate uncertainty","Directly assigning harmful exposures to pregnant participants would be unethical, so evidence often combines observational studies, mechanistic research and other appropriate models. Confounding, uncertain exposure measurement and individual variability affect interpretation. Converging evidence can support a risk conclusion without predicting every individual outcome. Respectful communication provides clear biological reasoning, avoids stigma and acknowledges access to care and support. An effective extended response links exposure, maternal–placental conditions, developmental processes, evidence and uncertainty."],["A named illicit-drug example makes the target explicit","Cocaine can influence cardiovascular signalling and produce vasoconstriction, potentially compromising uteroplacental perfusion and fetal supply. Opioids such as heroin act through opioid-receptor pathways; repeated fetal exposure can produce dependence-related adaptation and withdrawal after exposure ends at birth. These are different mechanisms, not one universal drug effect. Other exposures, nutrition and social or clinical conditions can confound observations, so risk evidence must be interpreted carefully and without stigma. The examples explain why named targets and changing supply conditions matter, rather than predicting an individual pregnancy outcome."]],"worked":["Explain why the placenta cannot be described as a barrier that blocks every harmful substance.","Its role requires exchange across tissues, and transfer depends on molecular properties and transport mechanisms. Some exposures cross or alter maternal and placental conditions, so separate maternal and fetal blood does not imply complete protection."],"practice":[["Why can the same exposure at different developmental stages have different effects?","Different stages involve different growth, differentiation and organ-development processes. Disrupting a process while it is occurring can have consequences different from exposure after that stage.",3],["Explain one oxygen-related pathway by which smoke exposure may affect fetal conditions.","Carbon monoxide can reduce effective maternal oxygen transport, affecting the oxygen supplied to the placental exchange interface and potentially the developing fetus. Other smoke-related mechanisms may also contribute.",3],["Why is an observational association not a precise prediction for one fetus?","Exposure measurement, confounding, developmental timing and biological variation affect outcomes. Group-level risk evidence informs understanding but does not establish a particular individual result.",4]],"quiz":["Which statement best connects exposure with development?",["Timing and affected processes influence risk","The placenta blocks all chemicals","Every drug has one identical effect","An adult’s lack of symptoms proves no fetal exposure"],0,"Use the developmental process and exposure context."],"images":["y11-germ-layers","y11-placenta"],"mis":"Separate blood pathways do not guarantee that all harmful substances or effects are blocked.","references":[["HealthyWA: pregnancy and substance exposure","https://www.healthywa.wa.gov.au/Articles/F_I/Is-it-safe-to-use-drugs-or-drink-alcohol-when-pregnant"],["Pregnancy Birth and Baby: folate and pregnancy","https://www.pregnancybirthbaby.org.au/pregnancy/nutrition-and-lifestyle/folate-and-pregnancy"],["CDC: substance use during pregnancy","https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/treatment/substance-use-during-pregnancy.html"]]},{"id":"unit2-synthesis","year":"11","unit":"2","title":"22 · Unit 2 synthesis: inheritance, reproduction and evidence","goal":"Integrate several mechanisms in a structured response using original supplied data and explicit reasoning.","prior":"Connect the relevant cell, tissue and organ structures with the process being investigated.","sections":[["Start with the process asked for","Read the command term and define the system or relationship being tested. A description names relevant events; an explanation connects causes and consequences; a comparison pairs corresponding features; an evaluation judges evidence and limitations. Begin with the supplied case rather than writing everything remembered about the topic. Select relevant structures, establish the direction of movement or information and connect the observation with a biological mechanism. Use labelled diagrams or calculations when they add explanatory information rather than decoration."],["Connect organisational levels","A complete response can move from molecules to cells, tissues and organs. DNA sequence and expression influence proteins; chromosome behaviour influences gamete combinations; fertilisation combines contributions; developmental environments influence phenotype. These are related levels, not interchangeable labels. Explain the missing link when a question moves from a molecular observation to an organ-level or offspring-level result."],["Use quantities with the correct denominator","State the measured variable, unit and reference group. A probability among sons has a different denominator from a probability among all offspring. A genotype probability does not necessarily match a phenotype probability under every inheritance model. Show working and distinguish a mathematical prediction from a guarantee for one observed sample."],["Evaluate alternative explanations","A proposed mechanism can be plausible while the supplied evidence remains incomplete. Identify a specific alternative or procedural limitation, explain its effect and suggest a relevant improvement. For a small pedigree, several models may fit; for a screening signal, follow-up may be needed; for an embryo-development study, exposure timing and sampling matter. Avoid the empty phrase more research is needed unless the proposed work resolves a stated uncertainty."],["Construct the final scientific argument","Answer directly, organise relevant evidence and explain why it supports the conclusion. Use correct scientific names, figure labels, units and relationship arrows. Distinguish original observations from synthetic teaching examples and secondary sources. A suggested marking response identifies useful scientific ideas; identical phrasing is not required. After comparing, rewrite one weak link from memory and test the explanation on a changed condition. This retrieval step checks understanding beyond recognition of the model answer."]],"worked":["Two heterozygous parents, Aa × Aa, have a recessive condition expressed only in aa. Calculate expected affected probability.","The four equally likely allele combinations are AA, Aa, aA and aa. One of four is aa, so the model probability is 25% per conception. This is an expectation, not a requirement that exactly one of four children is affected."],"practice":[["A carrier mother and unaffected father are modelled for an X-linked recessive condition. Calculate affected probability among sons and among all offspring under equal sex probability.","Among sons, one half inherit the condition-associated maternal X. Among all offspring, the affected-son probability is 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4. The denominators differ.",4],["Explain how the same inherited genome can support different embryonic tissues.","Different regulatory environments activate different patterns of transcription and protein production, supporting differentiation. Most nucleated cells retain the inherited genome rather than deleting all unused genes.",4],["Why does embryo transfer following IVF not guarantee a live birth?","Implantation and continuing development require additional suitable embryonic and maternal conditions. Transfer is one intermediate step, so its occurrence does not establish every later outcome.",3]],"quiz":["Which answer best demonstrates scientific explanation?",["A causal chain supported by the supplied evidence","An unconnected list of key words","A copied general essay ignoring the case","A conclusion with no stated denominator"],0,"Connect the mechanism with the actual evidence and question."],"images":["meiosis","y11-placenta","y11-recessive-inheritance"],"mis":"A worked answer is a model of reasoning, not a substitute for applying the idea to a changed case.","references":[]},{"id":"inquiry-planning","unit":"I","title":"01 · Plan a valid, safe and ethical investigation","goal":"Turn a biological question into a testable design with justified controls and safeguards.","prior":"A conclusion requires evidence collected by a method appropriate to the question.","images":["method"],"sections":[["Choose the investigation type","An experiment manipulates a variable under controlled conditions. An observational investigation measures patterns without assigning treatments. Secondary-data analysis uses existing measurements; modelling tests consequences of assumptions. Choose the approach that can address the question ethically and practically. A literature review identifies prior evidence and gaps; it is not just collecting sources that agree with the expected answer."],["Make variables measurable","A hypothesis predicts a relationship with a biological explanation. Identify the independent variable, dependent variable and factors to control. Operational definitions state exactly how each will be changed or measured. For example, light exercise intensity can be defined by a standardised step rate and duration, while recovery can be measured as time for pulse to return within a specified interval of baseline."],["Plan sufficient, fair measurements","Use appropriate equipment, consistent procedures, suitable comparison conditions, repeated measurements and enough participants or samples. Random allocation can reduce systematic group differences in experiments; representative sampling supports generalisation. Repeated readings of one person are not the same as independent participants. Consider instrument precision and whether the method actually measures the intended biological outcome."],["Safety and ethics belong in the method","Identify hazards, likely exposure and controls, including a stopping rule and appropriate supervision. Human participation requires informed consent, voluntary withdrawal, privacy and minimisation of harm. Do not assign unsafe deprivation, infection or unapproved medicines in a school investigation. For animal work, consider replacement, reduction and refinement and follow institutional approval requirements. An ethical limitation may require secondary data instead of direct experimentation."],["Start with a question that the method can answer","A useful question identifies a biological relationship, population or system and a measurable outcome. A prediction states the expected observation; a hypothesis proposes a testable relationship with a reason. For an osmosis investigation, “How does sucrose concentration affect the percentage mass change of standard potato cylinders after a fixed immersion time?” is measurable. A hypothesis links increasing external concentration with reduced water entry or increased water loss through osmosis. Background research should identify what is already known and which procedural choices matter, not simply provide an attractive introductory paragraph."],["Define a fair comparison operationally","Specify concentration units, cylinder dimensions, immersion time, solution volume, temperature, blotting and weighing procedure. The dependent measure can be percentage mass change = (final mass − initial mass)/initial mass × 100, allowing comparison of different starting masses. Use independent tissue samples with suitable replication at each concentration. Standardise preparation and randomise allocation where feasible to reduce systematic differences. A control condition provides a reference, while controlled variables are factors kept suitably consistent; they are not the same use of the word control."],["Plan sample size and measurement quality","Choose enough independent samples to reveal variability and justify the comparison. Repeating one balance reading checks reading consistency but does not replace biological replicates. A balance’s resolution limits how finely mass can be distinguished, while calibration affects systematic accuracy. The method must be sensitive enough for the expected change. Avoid reporting many decimal places when the instrument cannot support them. Record the raw measurements and procedural deviations so that unexpected results can be investigated rather than silently removed."],["Create a risk assessment with controls","Identify the hazard, the possible harm, who may be exposed and how risk will be reduced. In the potato example, cutting tools create injury risks and solutions or spills can affect handling and surfaces. Use teacher-approved preparation, appropriate supervision, suitable handling procedures, clear labels and prompt spill management. No samples or solutions are to be eaten. Disposal and cleaning should follow school procedures. A risk assessment is more useful than a generic instruction to be careful because it links an identified hazard with a practical control."],["Ethics applies to data and living participants","For human research, explain purpose, foreseeable risks, voluntary participation, withdrawal, privacy and appropriate consent. Avoid coercion by making clear that participation or results do not determine a student’s worth. Protect identifiable health information. Animal investigations require appropriate ethical justification and approval; replacement, reduction and refinement guide evaluation. Integrity includes accurate recording, acknowledgement of sources and honest reporting of exclusions. A scientifically interesting question does not make an unsafe or unethical direct method acceptable; use a model or secondary data where appropriate."]],"read":"Use the scientific-method image to follow question, prediction, data collection and evaluation. Notice that findings can lead to revised questions. A diagram of the cycle is a planning aid; it does not guarantee that an inadequately controlled investigation becomes valid simply by following arrows.","mis":"Reliability and validity are different: repeated consistent measurements can still measure the wrong thing or contain a systematic bias.","worked":["Design a safer investigation of pulse recovery after a standard activity.","Use a school-approved low-risk activity with consent and health screening, consistent duration and pace, a defined baseline measurement and an explicit stopping rule. Measure pulse at specified recovery intervals with the same suitable method. Repeat trials where appropriate and sample enough participants. Report limitations from fitness variation and measurement timing rather than claiming the results apply to everyone."],"practice":[["Give an operational definition of a dependent variable.","For example: pulse recovery time in seconds until pulse returns within five beats per minute of a measured resting baseline."],["Why may random allocation improve an experiment?","It reduces the risk that pre-existing group differences systematically favour one treatment condition."],["Identify independent and dependent variables in the potato investigation.","Independent: sucrose concentration. Dependent: percentage mass change after the specified immersion period. The procedure must define how each is measured or manipulated."],["Why standardise blotting before weighing?","Different amounts of surface solution can alter measured final mass without reflecting water movement within the tissue. A consistent blotting procedure reduces this procedural confound."],["Distinguish repeated readings and independent replicates.","Repeated readings assess the same sample’s measurement consistency; independent replicates include separate biological samples and help estimate sample-to-sample variation. Both can be useful but answer different reliability questions."],["Write a risk–control pair more specific than “be careful”.","Cutting tools can cause cuts; have preparation completed or supervised under the teacher-approved method, use appropriate equipment and keep hands clear of the cutting path. The response identifies both the harm and a practical control."]],"quiz":["Which is a controlled experiment?",["Assigning appropriate treatments and measuring outcomes under controlled conditions","Only collecting opinions agreeing with a claim","Changing every factor simultaneously"],0,"Manipulation and control support a valid test of a specific variable."],"tables":[],"workedMore":[["Turn a vague proposal into a reviewable method.","Vague: “put potato in salty water and see what happens.” Improved: prepare teacher-approved equal-sized cylinders, record initial masses with a suitable calibrated balance, randomly allocate independent samples to defined concentrations, immerse in equal solution volumes for the same time and temperature, use a standard blotting procedure, record final masses and calculate percentage changes. Include replicate numbers, risk controls and data-recording tables. This permits evaluation of validity, precision and reproducibility."]],"year":"11"},{"id":"inquiry-data","unit":"I","title":"02 · Analyse data, graphs and uncertainty","goal":"Calculate summaries, choose graphs and justify conclusions within the limits of the data.","prior":"Units, sample size and measurement methods affect interpretation.","images":["method","teaching-pulse"],"sections":[["Summarise without hiding variation","For readings 60, 64, 64, 68 and 74 beats per minute, mean = 330/5 = 66; median = 64; range = 74 − 60 = 14. State units and sample size. The mean can be affected by extremes. An interquartile range describes the spread of the middle half of ordered data; state the quartile convention used. A summary does not replace inspection of the original observations."],["Choose a graph for the variables","Use a scatter plot for paired numerical variables and consider an appropriate trend or best-fit line. Use connected time-series points when the sequence supports that representation. Bar charts compare categories; histograms show frequencies in continuous intervals; pie charts show parts of a defined whole. Label quantities and units, choose sensible scales and distinguish observations from an inferred trend."],["Calculate and interpret change","Change is final minus initial. If glucose falls from 8.0 to 5.0 mmol L⁻¹ in 30 minutes, average gradient = (5.0 − 8.0)/30 = −0.10 mmol L⁻¹ min⁻¹. A negative gradient indicates decrease, not a negative concentration. Ratios and percentages require a stated denominator. Scientific notation expresses 3,200,000 as 3.2 × 10⁶; use <, >, Δ and ≈ accurately."],["Evaluate error and sampling","Random variation affects scatter; systematic error can shift measurements consistently. More repeats can improve an estimate but cannot automatically fix calibration error or unrepresentative sampling. Distinguish instrument resolution, procedural limitations and biological variability. Probability from a sample is an estimate: three of five observations satisfying a criterion is 60% of that sample, not proof that precisely 60% of all people will do so."],["Discrete and continuous variables determine representation","A discrete variable takes countable separate values, such as the number of affected individuals or allele copies. A continuous variable can vary over an interval within measurement resolution, such as mass, temperature or time. Categorical variables classify observations, such as treatment group or genotype. A numerical code assigned to categories does not make the biological variable continuous. Bar charts suit category comparisons; histograms display frequency across numerical intervals; scatter plots display paired numerical values. Choose the representation from the nature of the data and the question, not only from how the table happens to be written."],["Construct a frequency table using stated boundaries","For measurements 51, 54, 56, 58, 61, 62, 64, 68, 71 and 74, use intervals 50 ≤ x < 60, 60 ≤ x < 70 and 70 ≤ x < 80. Frequencies are 4, 4 and 2, summing to ten. Non-overlapping boundaries ensure a value of 60 belongs in only one interval. Relative frequencies are 40%, 40% and 20%. If intervals have unequal widths, simple bar heights can mislead unless the representation accounts for density. State whether a graph shows frequency, proportion, mean or another quantity."],["Calculate quartiles with an explicit convention","For ordered values 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 11, median = (6+7)/2 = 6.5. Using the median-of-halves convention, the lower-half median is Q1 = (4+5)/2 = 4.5 and the upper-half median is Q3 = (8+9)/2 = 8.5. IQR = Q3 − Q1 = 4. For an odd sample size, state whether the overall median is excluded from the halves; software conventions can differ. The IQR describes spread of the central half and is less sensitive to extreme values than the full range."],["Build and interpret a line of best fit","Plot paired observations using a labelled horizontal independent-variable axis and a vertical dependent-variable axis with units. A best-fit line represents the overall relationship rather than joining every fluctuation. For an appropriate straight-line trend, balance the scatter and avoid forcing the line through zero unless justified. Select two widely separated points on the line, which need not be original observations, to estimate gradient Δy/Δx. If the line passes through (0, 5) and (4, 13), gradient = 2 and the model is y = 2x + 5. The intercept is the predicted y at x = 0 in this model, not necessarily a meaningful biological baseline outside the measured context."],["Translate between a graph, equation and biological statement","A positive gradient indicates increase of y with x; a negative gradient indicates decrease. Units of gradient are y-units per x-unit. Interpolation estimates within the sampled range; extrapolation extends beyond it and usually requires stronger assumptions. A straight line may approximate only part of a saturating or feedback-controlled relationship. From an equation, calculate a prediction and compare it with observed values; from a graph, identify trend, variability and any change in slope. A correlation describes a relationship, while a causal claim requires suitable design and supporting reasoning."],["Probability and numerical uncertainty need context","If 8 of 20 independent observations meet a stated criterion, the observed proportion is 0.40. It estimates a probability for an appropriate population under suitable sampling assumptions, not a certainty for the next observation. Measurement uncertainty concerns the value obtained, while sampling variability concerns estimates from different samples. A consistent calibration bias can shift every measurement without increasing scatter. Larger samples reduce some random sampling variation but do not automatically remove bias. Use mean, median, range and probability for their proper purposes and retain units and denominators."],["Convert fractions, percentages and ratios","A percentage states a number per hundred: 3/8 = 0.375 = 37.5%, while 12% = 12/100 = 3/25. A ratio compares quantities in a stated order: 30 affected to 70 unaffected simplifies to 3:7, whereas the affected proportion is 30/(30+70) = 0.30. Do not replace the total denominator with only the comparison group. To estimate 19.8/4.1, use 20/4 ≈ 5 before calculating about 4.83; this rough check helps identify a misplaced decimal. Retain sensible precision and units throughout a biological calculation."],["Construct and interpret a histogram","For continuous measurements grouped into equal-width intervals, place interval boundaries on the horizontal axis and frequency on the vertical axis. Using the worked table, draw adjoining bars for 50 ≤ x < 60, 60 ≤ x < 70 and 70 ≤ x < 80 with heights 4, 4 and 2. The bars represent intervals, so gaps would misleadingly suggest separate categories. If widths differ, frequency density = frequency/interval width makes bar area proportional to frequency. Describe the concentration and spread of observations without pretending the grouped table preserves every original value."]],"read":"The method-cycle image places analysis after collection and before evaluation. Apply the calculations to the numerical examples on the teaching pages; the image is not a graph of those example pulse or glucose measurements. Check units and denominators before drawing a biological conclusion.","mis":"A trend or correlation does not establish causation, and a larger sample does not eliminate every source of bias.","worked":["A pulse sample is 58, 60, 60, 62 and 80. Find mean and median and explain their difference.","Mean = 320/5 = 64 beats per minute. Median = the middle ordered value, 60. The high value of 80 pulls the mean upward more than it changes the median. Investigate the high value’s context rather than deleting it merely because it differs. It could reflect real variation or a measurement issue."],"practice":[["A quantity rises from 40 to 50. Calculate percentage increase.","Increase = 10; relative to the initial 40, percentage increase = 10/40 × 100 = 25%."],["How would you improve a method with a consistently miscalibrated sensor?","Calibrate or replace the instrument and reassess measurements; simply repeating the same biased readings is insufficient."],["A sensor displays 0.01 g increments. Why is a reported change of 0.0003 g unsupported by that display alone?","The claimed change is much smaller than the stated resolution. Additional validated methods would be required; extra decimal places do not create measurement information."],["For ordered values 3, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 18, calculate IQR using median-of-halves.","Q1 = (5+6)/2 = 5.5; Q3 = (12+14)/2 = 13; IQR = 13 − 5.5 = 7.5."],["A best-fit line for a teaching dataset passes through (2 min, 90 beats min⁻¹) and (6 min, 66 beats min⁻¹). Calculate the gradient.","Gradient = (66−90)/(6−2) = −6 beats min⁻¹ per minute. The negative sign describes declining pulse over that interval; it does not imply a negative pulse rate."],["What makes a histogram boundary convention useful?","It prevents overlap and ambiguity so each observation is counted once. For example, 50 ≤ x < 60 and 60 ≤ x < 70 place a value of 60 only in the second interval."],["State a specific improvement for random timing variation and one for systematic calibration error.","Use a more consistent timing procedure or repeated independent timing for random variation; calibrate or replace the instrument for systematic error. More repeats of an unchanged biased instrument do not solve the bias."],["Convert 18% to a simplified fraction, then express 9 affected and 36 unaffected as a ratio and affected percentage.","18% = 18/100 = 9/50. Affected:unaffected = 9:36 = 1:4. The affected proportion is 9/45 = 0.20, so the affected percentage is 20%, not 25%.",4],["Construct a histogram from frequencies 4, 4 and 2 for intervals 50–<60, 60–<70 and 70–<80. Explain why the bars touch.","Use labelled numerical interval boundaries on the horizontal axis and frequency vertically; draw bars spanning those intervals with heights 4, 4 and 2. Adjacent intervals cover a continuous measurement scale with no omitted gap. State the sample size of ten; do not infer exact within-interval measurements.",4],["Construct a scatter plot for recovery time 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 min and pulse 105, 98, 89, 82, 76, 71, 68 beats min⁻¹. Add an appropriate fitted trend and interpret it.","Place time on the horizontal axis and pulse on the vertical axis, label units, plot all observations and draw a trend representing the overall relationship rather than joining noise as a claimed law. Pulse decreases over the interval, with the rate of decline not necessarily remaining linear beyond it. Retain the distinction between observed points and fitted model.",6],["A category frequency table has counts 4, 4 and 2 from ten observations. Calculate pie-chart angles.","Proportions are 0.4, 0.4 and 0.2. Multiplying by 360° gives 144°, 144° and 72°. Angles total 360°; the categories must be mutually exclusive parts of the stated whole.",3],["Explain accuracy, precision and resolution using a balance that repeats 5.20 g for a certified 5.00 g mass.","The consistent result demonstrates repeatability but a systematic difference from the reference indicates limited accuracy. Resolution is the smallest displayed or distinguishable increment and is a separate instrument property. Repeating the same biased reading does not correct the calibration.",4]],"quiz":["Which graph is usually appropriate for frequency counts in continuous intervals?",["Histogram","Pie chart of unrelated totals","A diagram without axes"],0,"A histogram represents the distribution across numerical intervals."],"tables":[{"title":"Worked frequency distribution","headers":["Interval","Frequency","Relative frequency"],"rows":[["50 ≤ x < 60","4","40%"],["60 ≤ x < 70","4","40%"],["70 ≤ x < 80","2","20%"],["Total","10","100%"]]}],"workedMore":[],"year":"11"},{"id":"inquiry-evidence","unit":"I","title":"03 · Evaluate claims and communicate a scientific argument","goal":"Build an evidence-based report and separate observations, interpretation and unsupported claims.","prior":"Study design and data quality constrain what a conclusion can establish.","images":["method"],"sections":[["Read beyond a headline","Identify the actual research question, sample, comparison group and measured outcome. A claim about a new therapy is weaker if supported only by cultured cells than if supported by suitable controlled human evidence. Check whether the report discusses uncertainty, adverse effects and alternative explanations. Peer review helps scrutiny but does not make an individual study immune to error."],["Use a claim–evidence–reasoning structure","State the claim precisely, present relevant evidence with quantities and explain the biological mechanism linking the evidence to the claim. A numerical difference without context is not a complete argument. Consider whether the effect could arise from confounding, chance, measurement error or a biased sample. A model can explain a pattern while still simplifying reality; identify assumptions and limits."],["Organise a scientific report","Include a title, question, hypothesis, method, results, analysis, discussion and conclusion as appropriate. Results present observations; discussion interprets them and evaluates the method. Refer to numbered tables and figures with units, sample sizes and clear captions. Use scientific names and terminology consistently, acknowledge sources and distinguish original data from secondary information. Avoid claiming more precision than the method provides."],["Adapt the explanation without changing the evidence","For a scientific audience, give enough methodological detail for evaluation. For the public, explain unfamiliar terms and compare risks using common denominators while retaining the important uncertainty. Ethical communication avoids sensational promises and stigmatising interpretations of genetic differences. Suggestions for further research should address an identified limitation rather than merely saying that more research is needed."],["Evaluate the source and the actual evidence separately","Identify authorship, purpose, funding context, methods, sample and whether the full study is available. A reputable source can report an uncertain finding, and a polished infographic can overstate one. Distinguish primary measurements from a review, commentary, advertisement or personal account. Check whether a claim about humans rests on humans, animal models or cultured cells. The relevant issue is how well the evidence supports the particular conclusion, not whether the source uses impressive scientific vocabulary."],["A scientific argument needs a warranted link","A claim states the conclusion; evidence supplies relevant observations; reasoning explains why those observations support the claim through biological principles. Include quantities when available and consider alternative explanations. For example, a treatment group’s improvement is more informative when compared with a suitable control than when viewed in isolation. A large relative difference can still reflect a small absolute difference if the baseline rate is low. State limitations that materially change the strength or scope of the claim instead of adding a vague sentence that all science has uncertainty."],["Evaluate models through purpose and assumptions","A model represents selected relationships and omits others. A negative-feedback flow chart may omit molecular detail to clarify direction; a token drift model may omit realistic mating patterns to isolate sampling. Judge whether the simplification suits the question. Use the model to predict an outcome and compare it with evidence. If the prediction fails, consider parameter choice, omitted mechanisms and data quality. A diagram’s arrows communicate an argument; drawing an arrow is not independent proof that the relationship exists."],["Communicate to a defined audience","A scientific report needs operational methods, raw and processed results, units, sample size, analysis and justified conclusions. A public explanation should define technical terms and use meaningful risk comparisons without removing important uncertainty. Nomenclature includes appropriate gene, species, organ and unit conventions; genus names are capitalised and species epithets lowercase in scientific names, usually italicised in formatted work. Refer to tables and figures by number and give captions explaining what was measured. A reader should be able to distinguish data from interpretation and source material from newly written explanation."],["Construct an evaluative extended response","Start by answering the question directly. Organise evidence around the required comparison or causal chain, then evaluate the strongest limitations and implications. If asked to assess a therapy, distinguish biological plausibility, observed benefit, adverse effects and access. If asked to evaluate an evolutionary claim, compare molecular, anatomical and contextual evidence. Propose a follow-up that addresses a specific remaining uncertainty. Avoid substituting a memorised essay on the general topic for an argument responding to the supplied stimulus."]],"read":"Use the scientific-method cycle as a reminder that communication and evaluation lead to new questions. A clear-looking diagram is a representation, not independent experimental evidence. Check whether its labels and arrows are supported by the actual study.","mis":"A scientifically phrased conclusion is not automatically justified; the design and measurements must support it.","worked":["Ten volunteers report better memory after an advertised supplement, with no control group. Evaluate a claim that the supplement caused improvement.","The design cannot separate treatment effects from expectation, repeated testing, sleep changes or participant selection. The sample is small and the measurement may be subjective. A more informative study would use suitable validated outcomes and an ethically approved comparison design, potentially including random allocation and blinding. The reported improvement is an observation; causation is not established."],"practice":[["What should a useful method improvement do?","Address a specific identified limitation, such as calibration error, biased sampling or an uncontrolled confounding variable."],["Why cite an image source?","It identifies where the visual came from and allows readers to check its context and attribution."],["A newspaper gives only relative risk reduction. What extra information is needed for a useful comparison?","Baseline and treated absolute rates, denominators, outcome definitions, time period and uncertainty help determine the size and relevance of the effect."],["A model of genetic drift uses a biased sampling spoon. Why is this a problem if the purpose is to isolate chance?","The tool introduces systematic preference among token types, so changes cannot be attributed solely to random sampling. The method no longer isolates the intended mechanism."],["Write one specific limitation and follow-up for a small non-random clinical sample.","The sample may not represent the intended population, limiting generalisation. A suitably designed larger and more representative study with an appropriate comparison would test whether the effect persists across that population."],["Why must an extended response refer to the supplied data?","The task assesses application and reasoning in that context. Relevant numerical or qualitative evidence is needed to support the conclusion rather than only recalling general facts."]],"quiz":["Which conclusion is best supported by an uncontrolled small before–after study?",["An observed change occurred in the sample","The treatment certainly works for everyone","All alternative explanations are impossible"],0,"The observation is narrower than a strong causal or population-wide claim."],"tables":[],"workedMore":[],"year":"11"},{"id":"enzyme-investigation","year":"11","unit":"I","title":"04 · Investigation workshop: enzyme concentration and rate","goal":"Conduct a teacher-approved enzyme investigation, calculate a rate proxy and evaluate a complete report.","prior":"Recall enzyme specificity, concentration, controlled comparisons, graph scales and the distinction between raw and processed data.","sections":[["Define a testable comparison","Investigate how the concentration of an appropriate amylase preparation affects the time needed to reach a standard starch endpoint. Predict that greater active enzyme concentration can reduce endpoint time when substrate and other conditions are appropriately controlled. The hypothesis needs biological reasoning: more available active sites can support more successful reactions per unit time before other factors limit rate. A preliminary trial helps establish suitable stock strength, sampling interval and measurable times. The method is a teacher-approved model, not an instruction to collect human saliva."],["Make the procedure operational","One possible school method uses a fixed 5 mL volume of 0.5% starch and a fixed 1 mL volume of a standardised enzyme preparation at several stated relative concentrations. Keep temperature, pH, mixing, total volume and starch preparation consistent. Under an approved iodine-test procedure, sample at fixed intervals and identify a consistently defined endpoint. Use separate reaction mixtures as independent replicates. Actual concentrations and intervals need piloting for the available enzyme; a recipe does not guarantee the same activity for every stock."],["Assess risks and record raw observations","A written assessment identifies hazards, possible harm and controls, including handling iodine and other reagents according to school procedures, eye protection where required, spill management and no tasting. Record enzyme preparation, temperature, concentration, start time, endpoint criterion and any procedural deviations. Do not delete slow readings simply because they weaken the expected pattern. Repeated timing of the same reaction is different from independent replicate reactions. A safe, competent investigation requires teacher approval and observed student work; reading these pages does not demonstrate that competence."],["Process the synthetic teaching data","For relative enzyme concentrations of 25%, 50%, 75% and 100%, use synthetic replicate endpoint times of (230,240,250), (115,120,125), (75,80,85) and (55,60,65) seconds. Mean times are 240,120,80 and 60 seconds. The reciprocal of each mean time gives a rate proxy of approximately 0.00417,0.00833,0.0125 and0.0167 s⁻¹. This proxy assumes a comparable amount of reaction is required to reach the endpoint. Clearly label these as supplied synthetic results rather than observations you personally collected."],["Graph and evaluate the relationship","Plot relative concentration on a labelled horizontal axis and the chosen rate proxy vertically. Distinguish the supplied points from a fitted trend, and retain reasonable precision. The expected trend in this model does not establish unlimited proportionality at all concentrations, temperatures or substrate levels. Endpoint judgement, sampling intervals, mixing and stock activity can affect timing. Propose specific improvements addressing identified limits; simply repeating a method with a systematically inconsistent endpoint does not remove that bias."],["Write the investigation as a scientific report","Present the question and reasoned hypothesis, operational method, safety and ethics, raw replicate table, processed calculations, graph, analysis, evaluation and justified conclusion. Compare the evidence with the prediction and discuss alternative explanations. State whether the evidence comes from an actual approved practical, a simulation or this synthetic dataset. A conclusion answers the specific question under the tested conditions and does not claim a universal optimum for every human enzyme. Cite sources and separate observations from interpretation."]],"worked":["Calculate a rate proxy for a mean endpoint time of120 s.","Rate proxy=1/time=1/120≈0.00833 s⁻¹. It compares reactions reaching an equivalent endpoint and is not automatically the instantaneous concentration change per second."],"practice":[["Name the independent and dependent variables.","Independent: relative active enzyme concentration. Dependent: time to the stated endpoint, with a derived reciprocal-time rate proxy if justified.",3],["Calculate the mean of230,240 and250 s, then its reciprocal.","Mean=(230+240+250)/3=240 s;1/240≈0.00417 s⁻¹.",3],["Explain why total volume and substrate amount should be consistent.","Different total volumes or starting substrate amounts change concentrations or the amount of reaction needed for the endpoint, confounding interpretation of an enzyme-concentration comparison.",4],["Give a specific limitation of sampling only every60 seconds when the fastest endpoint is near60 seconds.","A coarse interval can make endpoint-time uncertainty large relative to the result and may obscure differences. Pilot a suitably shorter standard interval or use an appropriate validated continuous measure.",4]],"quiz":["Why use separate replicate reaction mixtures?",["To estimate reaction-to-reaction variability","To guarantee the hypothesis is true","To remove every systematic error automatically","To replace a clear endpoint definition"],0,"Independent replicates address variability but do not automatically remove bias."],"images":["y11-enzyme-data"],"mis":"A reciprocal-time proxy is useful only when the endpoint and relevant reaction amount are comparable. It is not every possible definition of reaction rate.","tables":[{"title":"Synthetic enzyme investigation results","headers":["Relative enzyme","Replicate endpoint times / s","Mean / s","1 / mean time / s⁻¹"],"rows":[["25%","230,240,250","240","0.00417"],["50%","115,120,125","120","0.00833"],["75%","75,80,85","80","0.0125"],["100%","55,60,65","60","0.0167"]]}]}];
const names={'1':'Unit 1 – The functioning human body','2':'Unit 2 – Reproduction and inheritance',I:'Scientific Inquiry Skills'};
const modules=lessons.map((m,i)=>({...m,id:'hb11-'+m.id,year:'11',unit:names[m.unit],freshAuthored:true,autoAdvance:true,order:i+1,subtitle:m.sections.length+' teaching sections · sourced diagrams · worked examples · written practice',screens:[],questions:[],bank:[]}));
root.HBYear11Lessons=lessons;root.HumanBiologyATAR=(root.HumanBiologyATAR||[]).filter(m=>String(m.year)!=='11').concat(modules);
root.HBYear11Course={modules,resolve:key=>key==='hb11-inquiry-types'?'hb11-inquiry-planning':key,renderCatalog(host,list,open,unit){
const esc=s=>String(s??'').replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
host.innerHTML='<section class="hb-new-catalog"><div class="hb-new-catalog-head"><span>YEAR 11 / TEXTBOOK EDITION</span><h2>'+esc(unit)+'</h2><p>'+modules.length+' detailed lessons: '+modules.filter(m=>m.unit===names[1]).length+' for Unit 1, '+modules.filter(m=>m.unit===names[2]).length+' for Unit 2 and '+modules.filter(m=>m.unit===names.I).length+' for inquiry. Learn the mechanisms, study sourced diagrams, attempt worked examples and explain the evidence.</p><p><a href="human-biology-year11-coverage.html">View Year 11 syllabus evidence and teaching map →</a></p><div class="hb-new-unit-tabs">'+Object.values(names).map(name=>'<button type="button" data-unit="'+name+'" class="'+(name===unit?'selected':'')+'">'+name.split(' – ')[0]+' <b>'+modules.filter(m=>m.unit===name).length+'</b></button>').join('')+'</div></div><label class="hb-new-search-label">Find a lesson <input class="hb-new-search" type="search" placeholder="Search this unit’s lessons and teaching…"></label><p class="hb-new-search-status" role="status"></p><div class="hb-new-cards">'+list.map(m=>'<button type="button" data-lesson="'+m.id+'"><span class="hb-new-card-number">LESSON '+m.title.split(' · ')[0]+'</span><h3>'+esc(m.title.replace(/^\d+ · /,''))+'</h3><p>'+esc(m.goal)+'</p><span class="hb-new-card-meta">'+m.sections.length+' explanations · '+m.images.length+' image studies · '+m.practice.length+' written questions</span><strong>Open lesson →</strong></button>').join('')+'</div></section>';
host.querySelectorAll('[data-lesson]').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>open(list.find(m=>m.id===b.dataset.lesson)));
host.querySelectorAll('[data-unit]').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{const s=document.getElementById('unit');s.value=b.dataset.unit;s.dispatchEvent(new Event('change'));});
host.querySelector('.hb-new-search').oninput=e=>{const q=e.target.value.trim().toLowerCase();let n=0;host.querySelectorAll('[data-lesson]').forEach(b=>{const m=list.find(m=>m.id===b.dataset.lesson);b.hidden=!!q&&!JSON.stringify([m.title,m.goal,m.sections]).toLowerCase().includes(q);if(!b.hidden)n++;});host.querySelector('.hb-new-search-status').textContent=n+' lessons shown';};
root.vaultVisibleLessonTopics=list.map(topic=>({topic,open:()=>open(topic)}));
}};})(window);
